VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: RAYMOND J REILLY MB,BCH,BAO\u000aCASE: BG-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: David Wu, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Kenneth R Lee, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS":\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, modified Bloom Richardson grade 1, approximately REMOVED_DATE \u000acm in greatest dimension. See note.\u000aCarcinoma is adjacent to the core biopsy site.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is identified.\u000aMicrocalcifications are associated with invasive carcinoma.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform type, present as a\u000aminor component of the invasive carcinoma (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMicrocalcifications are associated with DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aInvasive carcinoma IS REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aAll other margins are negative for DUCTAL CARCINOMA and DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN\u000aSITU. (>0.3cm).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with associated microcalcifications.\u000aExtensive sclerosing adenosis with associated microcalcifications.\u000aNote: The exact size of the carcinoma is difficult to determine since it was\u000anot clearly distinct by gross exam. Although the largest size seen on any\u000asingle glass slide is REMOVED_DATE cm, the carcinoma is present in 10 of 14 slides\u000arepresenting 8 consecutive sliced sections, each of which is approximately REMOVED_DATE \u000acm thick. The carcinoma would be classified as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID NX MX. Case was also reviewed\u000aby DR. D. Dillon, in the breast pathology division who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Right needle directed breast biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast mass\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a#1) Right needle directed breast biopsy (short stitch/superior; long\u000astitch/lateral)\u000aRADIOLOGY: Architectural distortion and clip and ca++ in specimen. Clip and\u000aca++ are seen in specimen. L. Frost, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "right needle directed breast biopsy, short superior/long lateral", and\u000aconsists of an oriented 16 gram excisional breast specimen (3.8 cm\u000aanterior/posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial/lateral x REMOVED_DATE cm superior/inferior). The\u000aspecimen comes oriented by the surgeon with a long stitch designating lateral,\u000ashort stitch designating superior. There is a metallic wire entering the\u000alateral aspect and exiting the anterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen\u000ais accompanied by a radiograph and report. Correlation with the radiograph\u000asuggests that the clip is localized within the lateral aspect of the specimen.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a single foci of hemorrhage (0.2\u000acm diameter) that is located within REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the\u000ainferior, REMOVED_DATE cm of the superior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margins.\u000aThe foci of hemorrhage is greater than 1 cm from the anterior posterior\u000amargins. Surrounding this area of hemorrhage is dense white irregularly\u000acircumscribed fibrous tissue, admixed with fat (80-90% fibrous tissue, 10%\u000afat). A metallic clip is identified in slice 4 of slices anterior posterior).\u000aThe specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: anterior perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slices 2,3 respectively, 1 frag each, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slices 4,5 including clip, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 6, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 7, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 8, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 9, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice 10, 1 frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: posterior perpendicular, 5 frags, ESS\u000adwu/jl\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by David Wu M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Kenneth R Lee M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFIRST ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE  DR. DEBORAH DILLON\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
