VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy with regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: George Lin, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA) SPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST SIMPLE MASTECTOMY":\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA AND DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm (slide measurement)\u000aHistologic type - Ductal\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - I (Well) (3 to 5 points)\u000aTubules - > 75% (1 point)\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate (2 points)\u000aMitotic counts - 1 point\u000aExtensive Intraductal Component(EIC)- Negative\u000aLymphovascular invasion (LVI) - Absent\u000aDermal lymphovascular invasion (LVI)- Absent\u000aSkin invasion - Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Not applicable\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Deep\u000aA p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID double stain confirms the absence of a myoepithelial cell layer\u000ain the areas of invasive carcinoma.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present\u000aTypes - Cribriform\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Absent\u000aNecrosis in DCIS - Absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total - REMOVED_DATE blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Deep\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aBiopsy cavity, Columnar cell change.\u000aIn the medial aspect of the upper outer quadrant, there are two fibrotic areas\u000a(1.5 cm and REMOVED_DATE cm) with dystrophic calcifications and associated giant cells.\u000aThese areas may be procedure-related, or represent previously ruptured cysts.\u000aClinical correlation is needed.\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb NX MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000a(This portion of the case was also reviewed by Dr. Jane Brock of the Breast\u000apathology service, who concurs.)\u000aB) SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST REDUCTION MAMMOPLASTY (685.0 GM):\u000aBenign breast tissue with no significant pathologic change, negative for\u000atumor.\u000aC) SPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT AXILLARY TAIL TISSUE":\u000aFibroadipose tissue with no significant pathologic change, negative for\u000atumor.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 65 year old female with history of left breast cancer treated with\u000abreast conserving therapy 15 years ago. Left breast recurred cancer vs new\u000aprimary.\u000aOperation: Left breast simple mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast recurrent cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. Lt breast simple mastectomy short suture - superior, long lateral\u000aB. Rt breast reduction mammoplasty.\u000aC. Lt axillary tail tissue\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "Lt breast simple mastectomy", consists of a mastectomy\u000aspecimen (1330 g; REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is partially surfaced by a\u000atan-white skin ellipse (21.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm) which exhibits a nipple-areolar complex\u000a(5.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an unremarkable everted nipple (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). There\u000ais a short stitch designating superior and a long stitch designating lateral.\u000aThe deep margin (inked black) is smooth, intact, and exhibits no skeletal\u000amuscle. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit a tan-white hemorrhagic\u000awell-healed cavity (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) located in the inferior aspect of the\u000alower outer quadrant, REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin. A metallic clip is\u000aidentified within this area.\u000aTwo areas of calcification (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) are\u000aidentified just posteriorly to the skin in the more medial aspect of the upper\u000aouter quadrant. These areas are grossly REMOVED_DATE cm apart and are located REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the previously mentioned biopsy cavity. The remaining breast parenchyma\u000aconsists of 85% yellow lobulated adipose tissue to 15% white fibrous tissue.\u000aThe most lateral soft tissue is examined for intramammary lymph nodes, none of\u000awhich are identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant to include biopsy cavity, 1 frag in each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant to include irregular ? calcified scarring\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, 2 frags in each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, multi frags total, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "Rt breast reduction mammoplasty", consists of multiple\u000afragments of irregular fibroadipose tissue (685 g; REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate) partially surfaced by tan-pink unremarkable skin (up to REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aThe specimen is inked and serially sectioned to exhibit 80% yellow lobulated\u000aadipose tissue to 20% dense white fibrous tissue. No masses or lesions are\u000agrossly appreciated.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 frags in each, RSS.\u000aPart C, labeled "Lt axillary tail tissue", consists of an unoriented fragment\u000aof adipose tissue (7.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) within which no definitive lymph node\u000acandidates are identified and are sectioned to exhibit a tan-yellow homogenous\u000aunremarkable lobulated cut surface.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags total, RSS.\u000aJC/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Friday REMOVED_MONTH 30, 2009 at 07:41:11AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aAdditional sections from the left breast simple mastectomy were submitted as\u000afollows:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast, upper outer quadrant, firm area, multiple frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast, lower outer quadrant, firm area, multiple frags,\u000aESS.\u000aThese additional sections show breast tissue with areas of dense fibrosis,\u000anegative for tumor. The diagnosis for the mastectomy remains unchanged.\u000aThe remainder of the specimen designated "left axillary tail tissue" was\u000asubmitted for microscopic evaluation:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of specimen, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aAdditional sections show fibroadipose tissue, negative for tumor. No breast\u000aepithelium seen. No overt biopsy site changes are identified (only rare giant\u000acells and minute foci of fat necrosis are seen).\u000aThe diagnosis for part C remains unchanged. Clinical and radiologic correlation\u000ais advised to determine whether this area corresponds to the left axillary core\u000aneedle biopsy performed on REMOVED_DATE  (see REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aCOMMENT: The patient has a history of a REMOVED_DATE cm well differentiated invasive\u000acarcinoma in 1993 with 7 of 21 positive lymph nodes (reviewed as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). By\u000areport, the carcinoma was "weakly positive" for ER and PR.\u000aIn 2008, a core needle biopsy was performed of an axillary mass that showed\u000aatypical cells that were ER positive and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu negative (reviewed as\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). An MRI guided core needle biopsy of the breast showed a small\u000afocus of carcinoma (also reviewed as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aIn the mastectomy, the carcinoma is adjacent to a larger densely fibrotic area\u000athat might be the remote excision site. This finding would support\u000aclassification as a local recurrence. Radiologic correlation is suggested to\u000adetermine if the new MRI finding was at the site of the prior surgery.\u000aAfter re-examination of the specimen and extensive sampling of the lateral\u000abreast and complete examination of the specimen of axillary tissue, no nodal\u000atissue or carcinoma is found. It is possible that the prior core needle biopsy\u000aof an "axillary mass" was of one of the areas of palpable fat necrosis and that\u000athe "atypical cells" were entrapped normal epithelial cells. Clinical and\u000aradiologic correlation is suggested.\u000aA block will be sent for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis by FISH.\u000aMethod for Hormone receptor analysis:\u000aEstrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual\u000amorphometric analysis using the following criteria:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday February 02, 2009 at 08:16:27PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (2/4/09)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma\u000aFISH was performed by Clarient using the PathVysion HER-2 DNA Probe Kit with\u000athe following (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aResults: NOT AMPLIFIED\u000aHER-2/CEP-17 ratio = REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average of HER-2 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average centromere 17 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aREFERENCE RANGE:\u000aNOT AMPLIFIED HER-2/CEP-17 ratio < REMOVED_DATE \u000aEQUIVOCAL HER-2/CEP-17 ratio REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE \u000aAMPLIFIED REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/CEP-17 ratio > REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #2 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday February 04, 2009 at 06:18:26PM\u000a[report_end]
