000awas oriented by the surgeon with a short stitch designating superior, a long\u000astitch designating lateral, and a thick silk stitch designating posterior. The\u000aspecimen arrived six-inked by the surgeon per the pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is an accompanying\u000aradiograph with the specimen demonstrating a clip, calcifications, and a wire\u000alocalized within the specimen. The wire enters in the lateral posterior aspect\u000aof the specimen and does not exit. The specimen is sectioned from medial to\u000alateral and is composed of 60% adipose tissue and 40% fibrous tissue with areas\u000aof hemorrhage. There is an area (0.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is white/tan and\u000anecrotic-appearing with a possible clip and is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000asuperior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, 1 cm from the\u000amedial, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior. The specimen is\u000aentirely submitted, medial to lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast, medial to lateral (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID = 1 slice each),\u000alesion in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 16 frags, ESS.\u000ajaw/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aPrelim Dx Release by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aNo Action - Prelim Sent by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSurgical Addendum:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor - POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor - POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/13/2005\u000a[report_end]
