 wire seen in specimen. Called to Dr. Iglehart by Dr.\u000aRoza at 12:45 am.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left lumpectomy with needle loc stitch = short\u000asuperior/long lateral", and consists of a wire-localized left breast excision\u000aspecimen (4.7 cm from superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral, and\u000a1.6 cm from anterior to posterior), oriented with a long stitch designated\u000alateral and short stitch designated superior per the surgeon. The specimen is\u000ainked by the prosector per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. A localization wire is entering the specimen at the medial\u000aaspect. The accompanying specimen radiograph is reviewed. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from superior to inferior. A metal clip is found REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the medial, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior and REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior aspect\u000aof the specimen. The clip is located within a firm/white area, consistent with\u000aprior biopsy site, present at the medial and posterior margins, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aanterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior and REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margins. No other lesions are noted. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted from inferior to superior in 15 micro cassettes with the biopsy\u000acavity submitted in cassette REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aGross photographs are taken.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections from inferior to superior, each bisected, 1 frag\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left sentinel node #1", consists of a tan/white lymph node\u000a(2.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The lymph node is bisected and entirely submitted in\u000acassette REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. The remainder of the fibroadipose tissue is submitted in cassette\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of fibroadipose tissue, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aawe/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 15, 2009 at 05:23:27PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aA prior core needle biopsy for a REMOVED_DATE cm mass revealed a REMOVED_DATE cm invasive\u000acarcinoma that was ER and PR positive and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu negative (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). No\u000aresidual invasive carcinoma was seen in this specimen after complete\u000amicroscopic evaluation of the margin. However, the gel pledgets marking the\u000acenter of the core needle biopsy site are present at the junction of the deep\u000aand medial margins and the breast tissue at this aspect of the biopsy cannot be\u000aevaluated. Clinical correlation is necessary to determine if there is breast\u000atissue present at this location or if this margin is at the pectoral muscle.\u000aThe appropriate AJCC classification (6th edition) is REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (sn).\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in < 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 18, 2009 at 11:32:51AM\u000a[report_end]
