VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: Esther Rhei M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Sandro Santagata, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Michelle S Hirsch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000a****************************************************************************\u000aCOMMENT: This case was re-reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon of the Breast Pathology\u000aService. The lesional area within the breast tissue is associated with\u000asignificant stromal sclerosis, which can make the evaluation of invasive tumor\u000avery difficult. Therefore, immunostains were subsequently performed to\u000aevaluate for invasive disease: keratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID highlighted the malignant\u000acells, and p63 and calponin demonstrated the presence of myoepithelial cells,\u000aeven around small nest of malignant cells. These findings support the\u000adiagnosis of DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU. THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF INVASIVE\u000aCARCINOMA. The following represents a revised final pathology report:\u000a****************************************************************************\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR A MASS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - solid, cribriform\u000aNuclear grade - intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - absent\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks:total - 1:3 blocks\u000aFinal Margins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - anterior, posterior, medial\u000aClose (0.1-0.3 cm) - lateral (0.15 cm), superior (0.2 cm)\u000aVery close (<0.1 cm) - inferior\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - absent\u000aAJCC CLASSIFICATION (6th edition) - Tis pNx MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) - pending\u000a******************************************************************************\u000aThe original diagnosis is stated below. Please see the AMENDED DIAGNOSIS\u000aABOVE, for the Final Pathologic Diagnosis\u000a******************************************************************************\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR A MASS:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm (measured on glass slide)\u000aMultifocal - no\u000aHistologic Type - ductal\u000aHistologic Grade (SBR) - I (well)\u000aNuclear Grade - intermediate\u000aEIC - positive\u000aLymphovascular invasion - absent\u000aDermal lymphatic invasion - N/A\u000aSkin invasion - N/A\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - N/A\u000aFinal Margins (invasive) -\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - deep/posterior, anterior, medial,\u000asuperior\u000aClose (0.1-0.3 cm) - inferior, lateral\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - solid, cribriform\u000aNuclear grade - intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - absent\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks:total - 1:3 blocks\u000aFinal Margins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - anterior, posterior, medial\u000aClose (0.1-0.3 cm) - lateral (0.15 cm), superior (0.2 cm)\u000aVery close (<0.1 cm) - inferior\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - absent\u000aAJCC CLASSIFICATION (6th edition) - REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa pNx MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) - pending\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 70 y.o. female with focal asymmetric density in subareolar region left\u000abreast. USN demonstrates 6 x 9 mm nodule appearing as a benign cyst.\u000aMammographer felt mass consistent with cyst but also slightly hypoechoic and\u000asuggest aspiration and biopsy.\u000aOperation: L breast wire loc biopsy\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. L breast tissue - short stitch=superior, long stitch=lateral, whip\u000astitch=deep margin -> to mammo\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "1. L breast tissue - short stitch=superior, long stitch=lateral, whip\u000astitch=deep margin", and consists of an ovoid breast excision specimen (2.8 x\u000a1.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) marked by the surgeon with a short stitch indicating superior\u000amargin, long stitch indicating lateral margin, and whip stitch indicating deep\u000amargin. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is a localizing wire entering the specimen\u000aat the anteromedial margin which, on radiograph, localizes a mass (specimen\u000acontains wire and mass, OR called, signed SR). On cut section there is a REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm fibrous nodular area that abuts the medial, superior, inferior,\u000aanterior, and posterior margins and comes to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral\u000amargin. The specimen is submitted serially from lateral to medial with the\u000anodular area in Micros REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast, lateral, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: midportion, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: remainder of nodule, 1 frag, ESS\u000aSSA/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Sandro Santagata M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAmendment #1 by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/7/2005\u000aFIRST ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE  DR. DEBORAH DILLON\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
