VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID SKIN- OTHER THAN CYST, TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aFOLKERTH, REBECCA, DUNN, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by FOLKERTH,REBECCA DUNN,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST SKIN NODULE", INCISIONAL BIOPSY:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA OF THE BREAST, moderately differentiated\u000a(modified Bloom-Richardson grade II/III), involving the entire dermis of\u000athis incisional biopsy (0.5cm in diameter); lymphovascular invasion is not\u000aidentified; the epidermis is intact (see note).\u000aNOTE: The biopsied lesion could represent either a metastasis or local skin\u000ainvasion from an underlying breast primary.\u000aImmunoperoxidase for ER/PR and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID neu are pending and will be reported in an\u000aaddendum.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma in dermis (block 2):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) POSITIVE (3+)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity\u000ain >10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 72 year-old female with fungating left breast mass. Core biopsy on\u000a8/6/03 showed adipose tissue\u000aOperation: Left breast skin nodule biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Presumed left breast cancer\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast skin nodule\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WU,DAVID,M.D.\u000aThe specimen was received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast skin nodule", and consists of a white soft tissue\u000aellipse, measuring REMOVED_DATE cm from tip to tip x REMOVED_DATE cm excised to a depth of REMOVED_DATE \u000acm. No discreet lesions are grossly identified. The specimen is inked,\u000aserially sectioned, and entirely submitted for histologic evaluation.\u000aMicro 1: Skin tip margins, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Body of specimen, 5 frags, ESS\u000adk\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: SKIN- OTHER THAN CYST, TAG OR DEBRIDEMENT\u000a[report_end]
