aFibroadenoma (0.3 cm).\u000aSelect slides were reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, breast pathology service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Ca of LT breast.\u000aOperation: SNB, excis ca breast/lt ? ax dissection.\u000aOperative Findings: See op note.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Ca of left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. left sentinel nodes for frozen section\u000aB/2. primary tumor site left breast long suture lateral short suture superior\u000ato breast tissue bank to frozen section room\u000aC/3. superior shaved margin left breast suture superior\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LT SENT NODES (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aLymph nodes, negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: WILLIAM ROBERT WELCH, M.D.\u000aResident: KEVIN GOLDEN, M.D., PH.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "left sentinel nodes for frozen section", consists of multiple\u000airregular tan-pink soft tissues (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate) in which four\u000atan/pink lymph node candidates are identified (ranging from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000agreatest dimension). The largest lymph node is bisected and entirely frozen as\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. The remaining lymph node candidates are differentially inked (orange,\u000ablack, and blue) and entirely frozen as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "primary tumor site left breast", consists of an irregular\u000afragment of fibroadipose tissue (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a short suture\u000adesignating superior and a long suture designating lateral per surgeon. The\u000aspecimen has been received previously inked by the surgeon per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen was\u000asectioned to exhibit a tan indurated well-circumscribed mass (1.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) located less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial, and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the inferior margins. The remaining breast parenchyma consists of a\u000a40% dense white fibrous tissue to 60% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The\u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular lateral margin, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body of the specimen sequentially submitted from lateral to\u000amedial to include mass (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular medial margin, multi frags in each, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "superior shaved margin left breast", consists of an irregular\u000afragment of fibroadipose tissue (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a suture designating\u000asuperior margin per surgeon (inked red). The remainder of the specimen is\u000ainked black. The specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit an unremarkable\u000afibrofatty cut surface, consisting of 50% white fibrous tissue to 50% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags total, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aJC/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Thursday REMOVED_MONTH 23, 2009 at 11:46:47AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (5/02/09):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aImmunohistochemistry for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is negative, supporting the diagnosis.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Saturday May\u000a02, 2009 at 10:37:46AM\u000a[report_end]
