VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS AND CLIP:\u000aBiopsy site changes with adjacent adenosis (see note).\u000aColumnar cell change.\u000aFocal epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy including evaluation of margins.\u000aNOTE: Fibrous breast tissue with adenosis forms a REMOVED_DATE cm mass adjacent to\u000abiopsy site changes.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast wire localization.\u000aOperation: Wire localization.\u000aOperative Findings: None specified.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass/abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast with localization - for mammography.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and mass in the specimen, 2 fragments of wire also present\u000a(reportedly broke during surgery). D/W Dr. Lesnikoski at 1:30 PM by Dr.\u000aGerlach.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "Left breast", and consists of an irregular fragment of fibrofatty soft\u000atissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) received with an accompanying radiograph, which\u000aexhibits two needle localization wires and a metallic clip. One needle\u000alocalization wire is received detached from the specimen and the second enters\u000athrough the inferior aspect of the specimen with no definitive exit. The\u000aspecimen has been received previously inked per pathology protocol. The\u000aspecimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There are no designating sutures. The specimen\u000ais serially sectioned from superior to inferior to exhibit a tan/white\u000aindurated ill-defined mass (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) located abutting the lateral\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin. Within this mass a metallic clip is identified and there is\u000apacking material surrounding the clip. The remaining breast parenchyma\u000aconsists of 90% yellow lobulated adipose tissue to 10% whisky white fibrous\u000atissue. Approximately 70% of the specimen is submitted, including all fibrous\u000atissue.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular superior margin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast parenchyma sequentially submitted from superior to\u000ainferior to include area of clip (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and entire mass (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1 frag in each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular inferior margin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aJenC/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Saturday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 03, 2009 at 02:54:46PM\u000a[report_end]
