VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy with regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Rachel E Factor, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Andrea L Richardson, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST RADICAL MASTECTOMY s/p neoadjuvant therapy:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-\u000aRichardson grade III/III) with papillary features and extensive\u000anecrosis. The tumor is located in the upper outer breast, is\u000apredominantly exophytic, and extends through the skin with ulceration.\u000aThe overall size of the tumor is REMOVED_DATE cm by gross examination (see\u000aNOTE).There are areas of fibrosis around the tumor consistent with some tumor\u000aregression and treatment effect.\u000aLymphovascular invasion is identified.\u000aNo in situ carcinoma is identified.\u000aThe invasive carcinoma is REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep surgical\u000aresection margin. The radial skin margin is negative for tumor (more than REMOVED_DATE \u000acm).\u000aSkeletal muscle is identified, negative for tumor.\u000aNon-neoplastic breast with hematoma, radial scar and ductal\u000ahyperplasia with calcifications.\u000aMETASTATIC CARCINOMA is identified in three (3) of fifteen (15) axillary\u000alymph nodes. The largest focus measures REMOVED_DATE cm. There is no extranodal\u000aextension. Necrosis and fibrosis consistent with treatment effect is\u000apresent.\u000aNOTE: Pre-treatment, a chest CT imaging study (3/13/06) demonstrated two\u000amasses in the left breast, one REMOVED_DATE cm and a second mass inferior to the first\u000a(distance not provided) measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. After treatment with chemotherapy,\u000athe tumor masses appeared to grow in size. No subsequent radiology was\u000aperformed. By gross examination, the tumor is one fungating, exophytic mass\u000aprimarily in the upper outer quadrant measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. There is skin\u000aulceration and extensive necrosis and fibrosis. METASTATIC CARCINOMA is present\u000ain two lymph nodes. Since this tumor enlarged following chemotherapy, this\u000awould correspond to a Miller-Payne Score of Grade 1 and would be classified as\u000aan AJCC (sixth edition): yREMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb pREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Mx.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast locally advanced breast cancer, right breast cancer both\u000aER negative and PR negative, HER-2/neu negative. Status post neoadjuvant\u000achemotherapy with minimal response, with progression of fungating mass.\u000aOperation: Left breast extended modified radical mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast extended modified radical mastectomy, short suture superior,\u000along suture axillary tail.\u000aB/2. Additional left axillary contents.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast extended modified radical mastectomy short suture\u000asuperior, long suture axillary tail", consists of a left modified radical\u000amastectomy specimen (1180 grams; REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm-overall dimension), to\u000ainclude left breast (21.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, axillary tail (11.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000atwo orienting sutures (short-superior; long-axillary tail), tan/pink skin\u000aellipse (23.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm), areola (3.5 cm in diameter) and everted tan/pink\u000anipple (1.0 cm in diameter). There is a variegated\u000ayellow/violaceous/tan/white, firm, malodorous necrotic mass (12.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with a predominantly fungating/exophytic component, which protrudes from\u000athe skin predominantly within the upper outer quadrant, focally extending to\u000athe upper inner quadrant, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to the nipple, coming to within REMOVED_DATE \u000acm of the deep margin (inked black), and REMOVED_DATE cm from the radial (skin)\u000aresection margin. Further sectioning of the mass reveals a focally hemorrhagic\u000ato vascularized component (less than 30%), predominantly at the lateral-most\u000aaspect of the mass. Approximately 3 cm medial to the aforementioned mass is a\u000atan/white, well-circumscribed rubbery nodule (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) within the\u000aupper inner quadrant. The remaining breast tissue consists of approximately\u000a60% yellow lobulated adipose tissue and 40% tan/white, dense focally nodular\u000afibrous stroma. Within the axillary tail, there are multiple red/tan/white,\u000afirm lymph nodes (ranging from REMOVED_DATE up to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). Within\u000athe largest lymph node, there are multiple foci of tan/white firm probable\u000atumor. Photographs are taken.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass at deepest extension, 2 frags total, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Radial/skin margin (perpendicular sections), nearest mass, 3 frags\u000atotal, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Possible vascular component of mass, 3 frags total, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tan/white nodule medial to mass, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant (away from mass), 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 single whole lymph nodes, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 single whole lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 single lymph node, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Single bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Single bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Single bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Single sectioned lymph node, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Single tan/white necrotic firm lymph node, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Additional left axillary contents", consists of a tan/pink rubbery\u000anodule (0.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Also received in the same container is a portion\u000aof yellow/lobulated adipose tissue (2.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). No lymphoid tissue is\u000aidentified within this portion of tissue.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nodule, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Adipose tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aDFI/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/15/2006\u000aSurgical Addendum: (9/18/06)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000areceptor. No normal epithelium is present to serve as an internal positive\u000acontrol for progesterone receptor. External controls demonstrate appropriate\u000aimmunoreactivity.\u000aThe tumor cells are positive for keratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, supporting the diagnosis of\u000apoorly differentiated carcinoma.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
