VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Anna Laury, M.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, DOUBLE WIRE LOCALZIED EXCISION FOR TWO MASSES:\u000aLarge duct papilloma(s) (1.2 cm) involved by a HIGHLY ATYPICAL DUCTAL\u000aPROLIFERATION (see note).\u000aThe papilloma is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin (inked yellow) over a\u000abroad front).\u000aA core biopsy site is present associated with the papilloma.\u000aNote: There are large intraductal papillomas in the specimen that\u000acorrespond to the radiologic masses.\u000aThere is a cribriforming monomorphic population of cells that is only\u000apresent within the papilloma. Although the population is highly\u000aatypical, it would not be classified as carcinoma in situ due to the\u000alocation solely within the papilloma. However, possible involvement of\u000athe papilloma by DCIS in the adjacent tissue cannot be excluded, due to the\u000aproximity of the papilloma to the superior margin.\u000aThe entire specimen was examined microscopically, including margins.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISION OF TISSUE BEHIND NIPPLE:\u000aMinute fragment of breast tissue with lactiferous sinuses and cautery\u000aartifact.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 49 year old premenopausal female, family history of breast cancer\u000a(sister), S/P REMOVED_DATE  US-guided core biopsy R 6:00 axis lobulated mass\u000ademonstrating fragments of a papilloma with atypia - excision recommended.\u000aSecond retroareolar mass on ultrasound, history of unilateral, spontaneous,\u000aoccasionally bloody nipple discharge. Previous surgery: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aOperation: R retroareolar terminal duct excision and wire localized biopsy REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R nipple discharge R breast mass REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Right breast tissue, short stitch/superior, long/lateral, whipstitch/deep\u000amargin, blue bowtie/divided ducts (yellow/superior, red/lateral,\u000aorange/inferior, green/medial).\u000aB/2) Tissue behind nipple rt breast.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aTwo masses and two intact hookwires are within the specimen. Dr. Gombos.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. RT breast tissue", consists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan\u000afibroadipose tissue (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is oriented per surgeon as\u000afollows: Short stitch equals superior, long stitch equals lateral, whipstitch\u000aequals deep margin, and blue bowtie equals divided duct. The specimen is\u000areceived previously inked per surgeon as follows: Yellow equals superior, red\u000aequals lateral, orange equals inferior, green equals medial, blue equals\u000aanterior, and black equals deep/posterior (please note, surgeon's inking\u000adeviates from standard pathology protocol). The specimen is received with a\u000aneedle-localization wire (wire #1) loosely penetrating the divided ducts\u000adesignated per surgeon with the blue bowtie the anterior aspect of the\u000aspecimen. There is a second needle-localization wire (wire #2 entering the\u000asuperior aspect of the specimen with no clear exit point. The specimen was\u000apreviously radiographed confirming the presence of two masses and two intact\u000ahook wires within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from\u000aanterior to posterior. The ducts corresponding to needle-localization wire #1\u000aare identified in the anterior aspect of the specimen, however, no discrete\u000amass or lesion is identified corresponding to wire #1. REMOVED_DATE cm posterior to\u000awire #1 is a bulging white/tan homogeneous solid nodular mass (0.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) corresponding to needle-localization wire #2. The mass shells out from the\u000asurrounding breast parenchyma, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the yellow/superior margin,\u000a0.5 cm from red/lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the orange/inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the green/medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the blue/anterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the black deep/posterior margin.\u000aThe remainder of the cut surface is composed of approximately 85% yellow/tan\u000alobulated adipose tissue and 15% focally dense white/tan fibrous stroma. No\u000aadditional masses or lesions are identified. The specimen is entirely\u000asequentially submitted from anterior to posterior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin to include divided ducts with blue bowtie,\u000asectioned perpendicular to ducts (corresponding to wire #1), multi frags total,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Intervening tissue between wire #1 and wire #2 mass, 1 frag in\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice with wire #2 to include mass (in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID through REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of specimen from anterior to posterior\u000a(posterior/deep margin perpendicularly sectioned in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total,\u000aESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Tissue behind nipple, RT breast", consists of an unoriented\u000airregular pink/tan soft tissue fragment (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is inked and\u000asubmitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000alm/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
