VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JULIE L JONES M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Eric C Huang, M.D., Ph.D.; Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS AND PRIOR CORE SITE:\u000aSclerosing papilloma with florid epithelial hyperplasia and apocrine\u000ametaplasia, and associated core biopsy site (0.7 cm).\u000aNote: There is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the entire\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast mass, s/p core biopsy, which demonstrated a complex\u000asclerosing lesion, for further evaluation with wire localized excision.\u000aOperation: Left wire localized excisional breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast wire localization - short stitch/superior, long\u000astitch/lateral - FS mammo at 10:37.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aMass in specimen. OR called. Dr. Twohig.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast wire localized excision short stitch superior long stitch\u000alateral", and consists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan\u000afibroadipose tissue (2.6 N-L x REMOVED_DATE A-P x REMOVED_DATE S-I cm), which is oriented per\u000asurgeon with two sutures as indicated. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A silver metal\u000aneedle localization wire is received free floating in the specimen container.\u000aThe accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates a mass within the specimen.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral. In the medial\u000aaspect of the specimen there is an irregular pink/tan firm fibrous lesion (0.7\u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe posterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin. The remainder of the\u000aspecimen is composed of greater than 90% yellow/tan adipose tissue and less\u000athan 10% delicately tan fibrous stroma.\u000aThe specimen is entirely sequential submitted from medial to lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass with medial margin, perpendicular section, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central specimen, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000almb/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/16/2007\u000a[report_end]
