VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elgida Radoncipi, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed ductal and lobular features, solid type\u000a(intermediate nuclear grade) with calcifications and with single cell necrosis\u000ais present in 1 of 7 slides (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), see NOTE.\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed features is present at the inferior margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMedial, superior, lateral, anterior and posterior margins are negative for\u000aDCIS.\u000aOther findings:\u000aCalcifications in normal epithelium\u000aBiopsy site\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for CIS with mixed features and LCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aAn E-cadherin stain performed highlights absent membranous staining in LCIS and\u000apartial loss in the CIS with mixed features.\u000aNOTE: In addition to low nuclear grade classic LCIS seen, a large duct is\u000apresent spanning REMOVED_DATE cm (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) filled with an atypical population of intermediate\u000anuclear grade and with single cell necrosis, which although predominantly\u000aE-cadherin negative is not typical of classic LCIS. This focus is present at\u000athe inferior margin. Dr. A. Richardson reviewed selected slides from the case\u000aand concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None provided.\u000aOperation: Left breast needle-loc excisional bx.\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy; short - superior; long -\u000alateral.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and, "#1. Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy short\u000asuperior long lateral", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE \u000a(inferior to superior) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior) cm breast biopsy with a\u000alocalization wire in place and short suture designating superior as well as a\u000along suture designating lateral. The specimen radiograph is reviewed in\u000acentricity and shows a broken wire, clip and calcifications in the specimen.\u000aGrossly, the wire exits the superior margin (inked red). The specimen is inked\u000aper pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange).\u000aThe tissue consists of dense fibrous parenchyma. A biopsy cavity is identified\u000a(0.2 cm in diameter), with clip. The biopsy cavity is REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior\u000aand posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from inferior, REMOVED_DATE from lateral, 1 cm from medial and\u000asuperior margins. A gross lesion at the site of the radiologic calcification\u000ais not seen. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral and\u000aentirely submitted for microscopic examination. The medial and lateral margins\u000aare perpendicularly sectioned and entirely submitted for microscopic\u000aexamination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular sections through the medial margin, 6 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sectioning from medial to lateral, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sectioning from medial to lateral (clip and biopsy cavity\u000ain REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sectioning from medial to lateral, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular sections through the lateral margin, 4 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000a3 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, ESS.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone\u000areceptor, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis\u000ausing the following criteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor\u000acells but only a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for\u000ainvestigational use only.\u000aERV/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 02, 2009 at 03:57:36PM\u000a[report_end]
