VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Veerle I.J. Bossuyt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Andrea L Richardson, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU with apocrine features, micropapillary and cribriform\u000atype, (intermediate nuclear grade) is present in 3 slides of 14; largest focus\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE cm on slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present.\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aDCIS is present <0.1 cm from the (red) superior and <0.1 cm from the (yellow)\u000alateral margin (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). DCIS is present >0.3 cm from all other margins.\u000aNOTE: Dr. Susan Lester has reviewed selected slides and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 48 year old female with R breast thickening, vague architectural\u000adistortion, suspicious ultrasound findings - S/P REMOVED_DATE  core biopsy at another\u000ahospital diagnosed as DCIS, on BWH review read as atypical apocrine intraductal\u000aproliferation. REMOVED_DATE  MRI demonstrated a rapidly enhancing irregular REMOVED_DATE cm\u000amass UOQ for which wire localization and excision are recommended.\u000aOperation: R breast wire localization excision REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R MMG + U/S.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1 - right breast lesion - short stitch/superior, long/stitch lateral and\u000awhip stitch on chest wall margin (fs --> mammo).\u000aRADIOGRAPHY REPORT: Both masses in specimen. C. Denison, M.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 - right breast tissue", and consists of fibroadipose tissue measuring\u000a4.2 cm from superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom anterior to posterior. The specimen is inked prior to receiving it in\u000apathology according to the pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There are short sutures designated as superior and long suture\u000adesignated as lateral, and whip stitch on chest wall margin. There are two\u000awires localized through the lesion. No masses are grossly visualized or\u000apalpable. The first wire tip is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The second\u000awire tip is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin.\u000aThe specimen consists of 95% fibrous tissue and 5% adipose tissue. The tissue\u000ais serially submitted from medial to lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: medial margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, inferior, and medial margin (tip of first wire),\u000a1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, posterior, and inferior margin, bisected (first\u000awire tip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, posterior, and inferior margin, bisected (second\u000awire tip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, inferior, anterior and posterior margin, bisected\u000a(second wire tip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, inferior, anterior, posterior, and lateral margin,\u000abisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: superior, lateral, posterior, inferior margin, bisected, 2\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aECH/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Eric C Huang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a12/2/2005\u000aSurgical Addendum: (1/25/06)\u000aSparse calcifications are present in association with both DCIS and benign\u000abreast tissue. No necrosis is present.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aSmall calcifications are associated with DCIS and benign breast tissue.\u000aNo necrosis present.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/25/2006\u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #2 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/10/2006\u000a[report_end]
