VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: EVA C. GOMBOS M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Mariana A. Stoleru, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, CENTRAL LOWER, 14 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR A\u000aPALPABLE PAINFUL GREATER THAN 6 CM IRREGULAR MASS:\u000aBreast tissue with a mixed inflammatory infiltrate consisting of\u000aneutrophils, eosinophils, plasma cells, and scattered giant cells,\u000apredominantly centered around lobules (see note). Poorly formed\u000agranulomas are present adjacent to lobules.\u000aThere are empty spaces in the areas of inflammation lined by\u000aneutrophils.\u000aGram positive bacilli are present on the Gram stain and on the silver\u000astain (MSS). No fungal organisms or acid fast bacilli are seen (MSS and AFB\u000astains).\u000aNote: The biopsy shows features characteristic of granulomatous lobular\u000amastitis. However, the presence of bacterial forms suggests that there is\u000aalso a bacterial infection. Anaerobic bacteria should be considered.\u000aCultures would be helpful to better evaluate an infectious process.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Dan Milner.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: 45 year old woman with history of minor trauma to the breast by a\u000achild, with pain but without bruising. The breast became painful and she was\u000adiagnosed with fat necrosis and infection on a trip to Mexico and treated with\u000aclindamycin. She now has a painful palpable mass. MRI shows a 9 x 4 x 4 cm\u000aarea of heterogeneous enhancement.\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left: over 6 cm, palpable and irregular mass.\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: Central Lower\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 14 G Ultrasound\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. 14 G Ultrasound 10:55 AM - Green cassette.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and consists of multiple tan-white to tan-yellow lobulated fibroadipose\u000abreast tissue cores (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The breast tissue cores\u000aare received in a green cassette and submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 25, 2009 at 05:33:42PM\u000a[report_end]
