VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: CAPRICE CHRISTIAN GREENBERG M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christopher P. Elco, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST EXCISION AND ADDITIONAL LATERAL WALL (TWO SPECIMENS):\u000aBreast tissue with extensive abscess formation with acute inflammation.\u000aScattered giant cells and loosely formed granulomas are present within\u000athe acute inflammatory infiltrate and in breast stroma.\u000aThere are empty spaces in inflammatory areas lined by histiocytes and\u000aneutrophils.\u000aGram positive bacterial forms are present in the empty spaces and within\u000ainflammatory infiltrates (Gram stain). No organisms are seen on MSS or AFB\u000astains.\u000aNote: The granulomatous areas are not specifically located near ducts\u000aand lobules. This pattern is not typical of granulomatous lobular mastitis.\u000aInstead, the findings are most consistent with a bacterial infection by a gas\u000aforming anaerobic bacteria. Cultures taken from this procedure are positive\u000afor anaerobic gram positive rods and rare coagulase negative Staphylococci.\u000aSelected slides were also reviewed by Dr. Dan Milner.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 45 year old female with chronic mastitis of left breast x 2 months,\u000anow with acute worsening with erythema and induration. U/S suggestive of fluid\u000acollection. Patient treated with Clindamycin and Flagyl with decreased size of\u000amass (8 to REMOVED_DATE cm on exam).\u000aOperation: Left breast I&D and debridement.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast chronic mastitis.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast main mass.\u000aB/2. Additional left breast tissue lateral wall.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "left breast main mass", consists of an unoriented fragment of\u000amultifocally disrupted fibroadipose tissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). In the area\u000aof one disruption, a pink-red hemorrhagic ragged cavity/area of granulation\u000atissue is exposed (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The intact external surface is inked\u000aand the specimen is serially sectioned to exhibit an irregular, nodular area of\u000adense fibrous tissue (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) extending to the margin. The fibrous\u000atissue exhibits focal cystic areas from which green-yellow material exudes upon\u000asectioning. The uninvolved 30% of the remaining breast parenchyma is occupied\u000aby yellow lobulated adipose tissue.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast parenchyma to include hemorrhagic area (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and\u000airregular fibrous area, multi frags total, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "additional left breast tissue lateral wall", consists of an\u000aunoriented fragment of yellow-red hemorrhagic lobulated fibroadipose tissue\u000a(2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). One aspect of the specimen is focally disrupted and\u000aexhibits hemorrhagic smooth to focally cauterized area (inked blue), the\u000aremainder of the specimen is inked black and the specimen is serially sectioned\u000ato exhibit an area of fibrous tissue (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which exhibits focal\u000anecrosis and hemorrhage. The remaining cut surface (approximately 60%) is\u000acomposed of yellow-red focally hemorrhagic adipose tissue.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags total, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000ajc/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Friday\u000aMay 22, 2009 at 03:16:12PM\u000a[report_end]
