VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHERINE S. GIESS M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kevin B Long, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL NUCLEI and apocrine change in lobules (best seen in block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000a(see note).\u000aFragments of a fibroadenoma with stromal calcifications.\u000aCalcifications in lobules.\u000aNote: The patient has a history of left breast (lower outer) poorly\u000adifferentiated invasive carcinoma without DCIS treated with radiation and\u000achemotherapy in 1992 (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and right breast DCIS with microinvasion\u000atreated with mastectomy in 2006 (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThe marked nuclear atypia in lobules may be related to prior treatment.\u000aHowever, focal involvement by carcinoma in situ cannot be excluded. These\u000acells appear to be an incidental finding. This lesion would be evaluated\u000abest with excision.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor,\u000aand REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were attempted, but the atypical cells were not clearly present\u000aon the deeper levels.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: 66 year old female with history of bilateral breast cancers, with new\u000aleft upper/outer heterogeneous calcifications. Time in formalin - 2:06 pm.\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left: Non-palpable calcifications.\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: upper/outer.\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 11 G Stereotactic.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast core biopsy in three orange cassettes - A/calcs (1 cassette)\u000aand B/no calcs (2 cassettes).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, in one part, labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number, and "left breast", and consists of three orange cassettes.\u000aThe first cassette, labeled "A Ca++", contains multiple cylindrical focally\u000ahemorrhagic fibrofatty soft tissues (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The\u000aremaining two cassettes, each labeled "B", contain multiple cylindrical focally\u000ahemorrhagic fibrofatty soft tissues (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The\u000aspecimen is submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of orange cassette labeled "A Ca++", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of orange cassette labeled "B", multi frags in each, ESS.\u000ajc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday REMOVED_MONTH 24, 2009 at 10:18:53AM\u000a[report_end]
