 the superior margin, and long suture\u000adesignating the lateral margin. There is a needle localization wire in placed,\u000awhich enters through the superior aspect of the specimen and exhibits through\u000athe inferior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol (superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior\u000ablue, and posterior black) and is serially sectioned from superior to inferior.\u000aThere is a well-circumscribed dense white fibrous area consistent with the\u000aprevious biopsy cavity (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which comes within REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000alateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin,\u000a0.4 cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the inferior margin. Within this cavity, there is an elongated surgical\u000abiopsy clip. Adjacent to the previous biopsy cavity is a small area of fibrous\u000atissue within which there is an additional circular biopsy clip (designated by\u000athe specimen #2), which is located less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the previously\u000adescribed linear biopsy clip. Remainder of the breast parenchyma is tan/yellow\u000aand homogeneous with focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting\u000aless than 5% of the breast parenchyma. There is an additional irregular\u000ared/brown serous fluid filled cystic structure (1.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is\u000alocated REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000apreviously described biopsy cavity. No additional lesions or areas of\u000adisruption are grossly identified. The specimen is radiographed and submitted\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections superior to inferior, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude previous biopsy with both biopsy clips 1 and 2, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude additional cystic structure, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, 3 frags each, ESS.\u000aThe specimen radiograph is interpreted in Centricity and the area of interest\u000amarked by radiology.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aSEK/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 19, 2009 at 05:32:44PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) EQUIVOCAL (2+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in < 30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Amy Ly M.D., Electronically signed on Monday REMOVED_MONTH 23,\u000a2009 at 01:58:56PM\u000a[report_end]
