VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ALBERT A. BARROWS M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Rachel E Factor, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, 4:30, 14 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR AN IRREGULAR\u000aMASS:\u000aINVASIVE POORLY DIFFERENTIATED CARCINOMA, measuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size in\u000athis limited specimen.\u000aInvasive carcinoma is associated with a prominent lymphocytic infiltrate (see\u000anote).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen in this limited specimen.\u000aNo definite in situ carcinoma is present in this limited specimen.\u000aNOTE: Scattered high-grade malignant cells are present within a dense\u000alymphocytic infiltrate. Immunohistochemistry for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000ademonstrates a polyclonal population of lymphocytes, compatible with a reactive\u000aprocess. The high-grade malignant cells are positive for cytokeratin 7 and\u000afocally positive for keratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. 34REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID is negative. Findings support a\u000adiagnosis of invasive poorly differentiated carcinoma. Slides also reviewed by\u000aDr. Jo-Anne Vergilio (Hematopathology Service), who concurs.\u000aImmunohistochemical analysis for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu will be performed and reported in an addendum.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: L 4:30 irregular solid mass with highly suspicious associated\u000aaxillary adenopathy, status post RT breast CA - lump, XRT 1995.\u000aOperation: A 14-G ultrasound.\u000aOperative Findings: Mass.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Mass, ? invasive CA.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. L breast core samples, white cassette.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast", consists of multiple irregular, tan/white to yellow,\u000alobulated fibroadipose breast tissue cores (ranging from REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000agreatest dimension). The specimen is received in a white cassette and\u000asubmitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (9/22/06):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for INVASIVE carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
