VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: George Lin, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aFIBROEPITHELIAL LESION (2.6 CM) (see NOTE).\u000aThe lesion is focally present at the superior-lateral margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and\u000ainferior-lateral margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aOther findings in adjacent breast tissue:\u000aATYPICAL LOBULAR HYPERPLASIA, (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), confirmed by an E-cadherin immunostain\u000awhich highlights absent membranous staining in lesional cells.\u000aColumnar cell change with cysts, calcifications and focal nuclear atypia.\u000aProminent large vessel vascular calcifications (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aNOTE: The fibroepithelial lesion shows focal stromal overgrowth and focal\u000aincreased cellularity. Mitoses are present but rare, < REMOVED_DATE HPF. The lesion has\u000aa multi-nodular border with pushing margins rather than overtly infiltrative\u000amargins. There are no heterologous elements and there is no nuclear\u000apleomorphism. The differential includes a low grade Phyllodes tumor and a\u000acellular fibroadenoma. Overall, diagnostic features of a Phyllodes tumor are\u000anot present and a diagnosis of cellular fibroadenoma is favored, however close\u000aclinical follow up is recommended. Dr. Susan Lester has also reviewed the\u000aslides and agrees with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 52 year old premenopausal woman, s/p L breast core biopsy oh REMOVED_DATE \u000arevealing fibroepithelial lesion best evaluated by excision.\u000aOperation: None given.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast mass, short superior, long lateral, whip stitch in chest wall\u000amargin.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Intact wire and mass in specimen. Called to Dr. E. Rhei by J.\u000aRitner, 5:25 pm/10/10.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, medical record\u000anumber and "#1. Left breast mass (to mammo)", consists of a fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.8 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm anterior to posterior). There are three orienting sutures such that short\u000ais superior, long is lateral, and whipstitch is chest wall margin. The\u000aspecimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/ orange. There is a wire entering the specimen from the\u000alateral aspect extending towards the inferior aspect. A radiograph accompanies\u000athe specimen showing a wire associated with the specimen and designating an\u000aill-defined mass. No calcifications or clips are seen in the radiograph. The\u000aspecimen is sectioned from lateral to medial in nine slices revealing a largely\u000afibrous specimen and a white/tan lobulated firm mass with hemorrhage associated\u000awith wire (2.6 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aanterior to posterior) which is located from margins as follows: REMOVED_DATE cm\u000asuperior, REMOVED_DATE cm inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial,\u000aand REMOVED_DATE cm lateral. The mass is in slices #2 to #7. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted for histologic evaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1 with lateral margin, perpendicular, 3 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, superior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, inferior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, superior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, inferior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, superior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, inferior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, superior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, inferior portion, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9 with medial margin, perpendicular, 3 to 4 frags each,\u000aESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aGL/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a10/16/2008\u000a[report_end]
