VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aFLETCHER, CHRISTOPHER, D.M., M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by FLETCHER,CHRISTOPHER D.M.,M.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, NEEDLE-LOCALIZATION BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aFibrocystic change, including micro- and macrocysts and apocrine\u000ametaplasia.\u000aPrior biopsy site.\u000aCalcifications are identified in association with benign epithelium.\u000aMargins were oriented and examined due to clinical concern for malignancy.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Left needle directed breast biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Left breast tissue suture long lateral, short superior.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcifications in specimen. C. Denison, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by HENDERSON,JOEL M.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "#1. left breast tissue long lateral short superior", and\u000aconsists of a single fragment of yellow/pink fibroadipose tissue (5.3 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a2.5 cm). The specimen is accompanied by a whole specimen radiograph which\u000ashows a localization wire in place, marking a large spiculated density and\u000aperipheral small clusters of calfications. The calcifications are centrally\u000alocated in the specimen. The specimen arrives with localization wire in place,\u000aand is marked with sutures, including a long suture to indicate lateral, and a\u000ashort suture to indicate superior. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue;\u000asuperior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The\u000aspecimen is sliced from medial to lateral and a sliced specimen radiograph is\u000aprepared, which shows a cluster of calcifications in slice 5. This corresponds\u000ato an area of hemorrhage and fat necrosis that may be a prior biopsy site.\u000aFrom slices 2 through 5 there is a diffuse area of grey/pink fibrous tissue\u000athat includes the areas of calcifications (2.5 cm greatest dimension). The\u000aremainder of the specimen consists of unremarkable yellow, lobulated adipose\u000atissue (90%), and stranded fibrous tissue (10%). Representative sections,\u000aincluding all fibrous tissue, are submitted. Approximately 90% of the specimen\u000ais submitted.\u000aMICRO #1: Slice 1, medial margin (perpendicularly sectioned), 4 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO #2 and 3: Slice 2 (superior and inferior, respectively), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO #4: Slice 3, superior, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #5: Slices 3 and 4, inferior, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO #6: Slice 4, superior, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #7: Slice 5, post inferior, including calcifications, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #8: Slices 5 and 6, superior, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO #9: Slice 6, inferior, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #10: Slice 7, inferior only, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #11: Slice 9, inferior only, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO #12: Representative lateral margin, perpendicularly sectioned, 2 frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aNOTE: Slice 8 not submitted.\u000aReports to: IGLEHART, JAMES, DIRK M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
