u000awire enters the anterior/superior aspect of the specimen, and exits the\u000ainferior/posterior surface. Specimen is received with an accompanying\u000aradiograph demonstrating a localizing wire in place within the specimen,\u000amarking a small focus of microcalcifications. Sectioning in this area\u000ademonstrates a REMOVED_DATE cm focus of firm dense fibrous tissue, with a REMOVED_DATE cm\u000apalpably firm nodule abutting the posterior margin. Fibrous tissue radiates to\u000athe superior, anterior, posterior, medial and lateral margins. Nodule focally\u000aabuts the posterior and medial margins, is REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest anterior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest\u000alateral margin and remote from the inferior margin. Remaining specimen shows\u000adense white fibrous tissue (60-70% of parenchyma), without additional\u000adefinitive lesions or other abnormalities grossly. Entire specimen is\u000asubmitted, sequentially from superior to inferior, with perpendicular superior\u000amargin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and perpendicular inferior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. Palpable nodule (?\u000aprior biopsy site) is submitted in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially from superior to inferior, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adb/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michael W Bennett M.B., B.C.H., Electronically signed on\u000a4/3/2007\u000a[report_end]
