VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aNASCIMENTO, ALESSANDRA, F., M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by NASCIMENTO,ALESSANDRA F.,M.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aPapilloma with atypia and associated calcifications (see NOTE).\u000aColumnar cell changes.\u000aCore biopsy site.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aNOTE: Within the papilloma, there are small solid areas with a monomorphic cell\u000apopulation. Immunohistochemical stains for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, calponin, and\u000aType IV collagen demonstrate myoepithelial cells and basement membrane\u000athroughout the lesion. Features diagnostic of atypical ductal\u000ahyperplasia (ADH) are not present.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Drs. S. Lester and D. Dillon, Breast Pathology\u000aService, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 54 year old female, S/P 1988 L mastectomy for treatment of breast\u000acancer. Recent right mammogram (9/1/04) demonstrates two right breast masses;\u000a9/23/04 core biopsy of a REMOVED_DATE cm upper/inner quadrant mass revealed a papillary\u000alesion with nuclear pleomorphism, excision is recommended.\u000aOperation: R breast wire localization REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1 - right breast tissue, short/superior, long/lateral, whip\u000astitch/deep margin (fs room --> mammo).\u000aRadiograph Report:\u000aClip, calcified mass and 2 wires within the specimen.\u000a(Dr. signature illegible)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by GLEASON,BRIANA, M.D.\u000aReceived fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "#1 - right\u000abreast tissue", is a breast excision (5.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with three orienting\u000asutures and two localization wires in place. An accompanying radiograph\u000ademonstrates calcifications and a clip. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from medial to lateral in eight slices. In the central\u000afour slices there is a hemorrhagic area (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) within which are\u000aseveral hard white lesions (? foreign material). These well-circumscribed\u000awhite nodules range in size from REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension. The\u000aremainder of the breast parenchyma is composed of 95% adipose tissue and 5%\u000afibrous tissue. Approximately 70% of the parenchyma is submitted, including\u000aall lesions and fibrous tissue.\u000aMicro #1-3: slice two with hemorrhage and white nodule, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro #4-6: slice three with hemorrhage and white nodules, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro #7-9: slice four with hemorrhage and one white nodule in micro 7, 3\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro #10: nodule and hemorrhage from slice one, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro #11: hemorrhage and lateral margin in slice five, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro #12: hemorrhage and lateral margin in slice six, 1 frag, RSS.\u000abgl/\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
