VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Michael H Roh, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A MASS:\u000aBreast tissue with complex sclerosing lesion showing extensive nodular\u000asclerosing adenosis, focal apocrine metaplasia, and calcifications.\u000aImmunostains for p63 highlight myoepithelial cells, supporting the above\u000adiagnosis.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 22 y/o female with left breast irregular mass. Core biopsy with\u000acomplex sclerosing lesion, discordant with imaging finding.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast complex sclerosing lesion.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy.\u000aShort suture = superior; long suture = lateral --> mammo @1430.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Clip contained in specimen. Mass in specimen by U/S.\u000a- Elwan/Denison\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient?s name, unit\u000anumber, and "1. Left breast wire-localized, excisional biopsy (Short =\u000asuperior; long = lateral", and consists of yellow/tan, fibroadipose tissue,\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE cm (anterior to posterior) x REMOVED_DATE cm (medial to lateral) x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000a(superior to inferior). Emanating from the specimen is a wire. The specimen\u000ais inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aPosterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned into seven (7) fragments\u000afrom the superior to inferior aspect. Slice #3 contains the wire as well as\u000athe clip that was identified radiographically. The clip was within an area of\u000acalcification and was removed. Examination of the breast parenchyma reveals\u000athat it is composed of over 95% tan/white, fibrous tissue with focal, white\u000aareas of calcification.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, posterior and lateral margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, that initially contained a wire and a clip,\u000aanterior, posterior, medial, and lateral margins, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4 with anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margins,\u000a1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 with anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margins,\u000a1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6 with anterior, posterior, medial and lateral\u000amargins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 8 frags total, ESS.\u000aThe entire specimen has been submitted for microscopic evaluation.\u000aROH/mmm\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Michael H Roh M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/9/2005\u000a[report_end]
