VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLEE, KENNETH, ROBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LEE,KENNETH ROBERT,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST, EXCISIONAL BIOPSY":\u000aAtrophic breast tissue with focal fibrosis and prior biopsy site with\u000areactive changes.\u000aNote: In anticipation of the possibility of malignancy, the margins were\u000adifferentially inked and examined.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "UTERUS AND CERVIX, HYSTERECTOMY" (285g):\u000aCervix:\u000aSquamous metaplasia.\u000aEndometrium:\u000aProliferative endometrium with surface curettage effect.\u000aMyometrium:\u000aLeiomyomata (largest REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aSerosa:\u000aNo significant pathologic change.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 42 year old female s/p left breast cancer REMOVED_DATE  for treatment of\u000astage II breast cancer and s/p Cytoxan adriomycin/chemotherapy, taxol\u000aand currently on Tamoxifen. Left breast upper inner quadrant area\u000aof tenderness and thickening without mammogram or ultrasound correlate.\u000aS/p REMOVED_DATE core biopsy of the site demonstrating benign breast tissue.\u000aHeavy bleeding on Tamoxifen, history of CAH.\u000aOperation: Left breast biopsy, vaginal hysterectomy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Left breast tissue - short stitch superior, long\u000astitch lateral\u000a2. Uterus and cervix\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by HENDERSON,JOEL M.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in 2 parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart 1, "left breast tissue", consists of two fragments of tan/yellow\u000afibroadipose tissue (6.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The larger\u000afragment is marked with sutures: a short one to indicate superior and a long\u000aone to indicate lateral. The larger fragment is sectioned from superior to\u000ainferior, and seems to be composed of approximately 90% yellow adipose tissue\u000aand 10% grey/yellow rubbery fibrous tissue, predominantly present at the\u000aposterior aspect of the specimen. No biopsy site, masses or other lesions\u000aare identified. The smaller fragment consists almost entirely of yellow\u000aunremarkable adipose tissue. The larger fragment is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue;\u000asuperior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange and the\u000asmaller fragment is inked entirely in black. Representative sections,\u000aincluding all fibrous tissue is submitted. Approximately 30% of the specimen\u000ais submitted.\u000aMicro 1 - smaller fragment, bisected, 2 f, ESS.\u000aMicro 2 - slice 1, (perpendicular to superior margin) 2, RSS.\u000aMicro 3 - slide 2ibrous tissue, 1 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - slide REMOVED_DATE f, ESS.\u000aMicro 6 - slice 4 fibrous tissue, 1 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - slide 5 fibrous tissue, 2 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - slide 6 fibrous tissue, 3 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - slide 7 fibrous tissue, 4 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - slide 8 fibrous tissue, 2 f, RSS.\u000aMicro 17 - slide 9 representative sections (perpendicular to inferior\u000amargin), 2 f, RSS.\u000aPart 2, "uterus and cervix", is a fragmented 285g hysterectomy specimen\u000aconsisring of irregular fragments of tan/pink soft tissue with features\u000aconsistent with uterus and cervix (8.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aSerosa is identified on many of the fragments, and is tan/pink, smooth,\u000aglistening and focally hemorrhagic. The exocervix (4.0 cm in greatest\u000adimension) is tan/pink, smooth and glistening and markedly hemorrhagic. The\u000aintact endocervix (4.0 cm length by REMOVED_DATE cm width) is tan/pink, smooth and\u000aglistening with focal hemorrhage and a herringbone-like mucosal surface. The\u000acervical os (1.5 cm in width) is focally disrupted, possibly associated with\u000athe surgical procedure. The endometrium is fragmented, but has a granular\u000asurface texture and is diffusely hemorrhagic. The endometrial thickness is\u000aless than REMOVED_DATE cm. The myometrium (2.5 cm in maximum thickness) contains\u000amultiple intramural leiomyomata (largest REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension).\u000aRepresentative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - cervix at opposite aspect, 2 f, RSS.\u000aMicro 20 - LUS, 1 f, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE - endomyometrium & serosa with leiomyomata, 4 f, RSS.\u000aReports to: GOLDSTEIN, DONALD, PETER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: UTERUS W/ OR W/O ADNEXA- OTH THAN NEPLAST OR PROL\u000a[report_end]
