VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Michael A. Seidman, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS, A MASS,\u000aAND A CLIP:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - Comedo, solid, cribriform, apocrine\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate to high\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Present\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Present\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total - 23:29 blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Anterior, posterior, superior, inferior\u000aVery close(<0.1 cm from margin)- Lateral (multifocal)\u000aMedial (multifocal)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aSclerosing adenosis\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition) - Tis NX MX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aNOTE: Double immunostains for p63 and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID were evaluated in multiple\u000ablocks. DCIS extensively involves sclerosis adenosis. No definitive\u000amicroinvasion is identified. Selected slides were also reviewed by Dr. D.\u000aDillon, Breast Pathology Service, who concurs.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 64 year-old nulliparous, post menopausal female with hx of L breast REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, ER/PR - Her 2 Neu + breast cancer in 2001, s/p breast conserving\u000atherapy. Mammogram REMOVED_DATE  demonstrates R upper breast irregular mass, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aby U/S. Core bx REMOVED_DATE  revealed dense collagenous stroma with calcifications\u000anot considered concordant, excision recommended.\u000aOperation: R breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammogram hx of L breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. RT bst tissue < short - superior, long lateral, whip stitch - chest wall\u000amargin - to mammo\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aClip in specimen. E.P. Frost\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "Rt breast tissue", and consists of an irregular fragment of fibroadipose\u000atissue (12.0 cm M-L x REMOVED_DATE cm S-I x REMOVED_DATE cm A-P) with a short suture designating\u000asuperior, long suture designating lateral, a whip stitch designating the chest\u000awall margin, and a blue bow-tie on the tissue behind the nipple. The specimen\u000ais inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is received with a radiograph demonstrating a\u000aneedle localization wire, which enters through the anterior aspect of the\u000aspecimen and exits through the superior aspect of the specimen. The radiograph\u000aalso demonstrates a metallic clip within the specimen. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from medial to lateral to exhibit abundant, approximately\u000a98% of the entire specimen, ill-defined firm nodular dense white fibrous\u000atissue, which extends to and involves all the margins. A metallic clip is\u000aidentified within the dense fibrous tissue, located REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial\u000amargin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe anterior margin. Representative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular medial margin to include area surrounding bow-tie\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative cross sections sequentially submitted from medial\u000ato lateral to include area of clip (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1 frag in each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular lateral margin, 1 frag in each, RSS.\u000aPlease note approximately 60% of the specimen is submitted.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aJC/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 22, 2009 at 10:26:33AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) HETEROGENEOUS - 1+ TO 2+ WITH VERY FOCAL 3+\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in < 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 23, 2009 at 02:54:07PM\u000a[report_end]
