 three orienting sutures (long equals\u000alateral, short equals superior, whipstitch equals chest wall margin). The\u000aspecimen is previously inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. A localization wire enters the superior aspect\u000aof the specimen and exits the inferomedial aspect of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is accompanied by a radiograph which shows a clip and a mass/possible\u000abiopsy cavity associated with the wire. Upon correlation of the radiograph of\u000athe gross specimen, the clips/possible core biopsy site is located in the\u000asuperior medial portion of the segment. The specimen is bisected along the\u000awire. There is a tan/brown, focally hemorrhagic indurated area/possible biopsy\u000acavity (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the anterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The entire specimen\u000ais submitted for microscopic analysis.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margins, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mid portion of specimen serially sectioned from lateral to medial\u000a(possible core biopsy site/area of clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000ask/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jonathan N Glickman M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/17/2007\u000a[report_end]
