VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Jason F Schmidt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISION WITH MRI GUIDED WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A DENSITY:\u000a- CAPSS (Columnar alteration with prominent snouts and secretions).\u000a- Adenosis.\u000a- Ductal Hyperplasia, usual type, focally florid with papillomatosis.\u000a- Calcifications associated with benign breast tissue.\u000a- Fibroadenoma.\u000a- There is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of abnormal MRI (density on MRI not as subtle on mammo or\u000aultra sound). Pt had also had core bx at BWH - fibroadenoma.\u000aOperation: Left mammographic density breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left MRI.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, short = superior, long = lateral to mammo at 15:38.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aWire at the center of the specimen.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "Left breast tissue, suture short = superior, long = lateral", and consists\u000aof a breast lumpectomy specimen (medial to lateral REMOVED_DATE cm, superior to inferior\u000a4.9 cm, anterior to posterior REMOVED_DATE cm). Two orienting sutures are present and\u000adesignated by the surgeon as long - lateral and short - superior. The specimen\u000ais inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A\u000alocalization wire inserts into the lateral edge of the specimen and extends\u000asuperiorly. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates the present of a\u000alocalization wire within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from\u000aanterior to posterior. Within the specimen a single well-circumscribed\u000atan/white nodule (0.4 x REMOVED_DATE x0 .3 cm) is identified. The nodule is oriented to\u000athe margin as follows: anterior - REMOVED_DATE cm, medial - REMOVED_DATE cm, posterior - REMOVED_DATE cm,\u000ainferior - REMOVED_DATE cm, superior - REMOVED_DATE cm, lateral - REMOVED_DATE cm. No additional discrete\u000alesions are identified within the breast parenchyma. The remaining breast\u000aparenchyma is composed of lobulated yellow adipose tissue (approximately 60%)\u000aand focal areas of tan/white to pink fibrous tissue (approximately 20%).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nodule and medial and anterior margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast parenchyma and closest resection margins to nodule, 1 frag\u000aEach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Unremarkable breast parenchyma and margins, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of breast parenchyma from superior to inferior,\u000amultiple frags, ESS.\u000aJfs/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Jason F Schmidt M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Review by William R Welch M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
