VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROBERT T OSTEEN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Vijay K Vanguri, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT SENTINEL LYMPH NODES, EXCISION (INCLUDING FSA):\u000aTwo (2) lymph nodes with no tumor present.\u000aMultiple levels examined.\u000aLEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY (138 gm):\u000aNo residual invasive or in situ carcinoma identified.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aColumnar alteration of lobules with prominent snouts and secretions.\u000aBiopsy cavity.\u000aSkin with seborrheic keratosis.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium and stroma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast cancer.\u000aOperation: Left simple mastectomy and sentinel node biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA (1) Sentinel node - F.S.\u000aB (2) Left breast - suture with axilla.\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#1 SENTINEL NODE (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aTwo lymph nodes entirely frozen with no tumor seen.\u000aOR Consultation by: WILLIAM ROBERT WELCH, M.D.\u000aResident: JACQUELINE M. WILLIAM, PH.D., M.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in the frozen section room, in two parts, each\u000alabeled with the patient?s name, unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1 Sentinel Node", and consists of two pink/tan rubbery lymph node\u000acandidates (0.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, 1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with scant attached\u000ayellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. The larger lymph node candidate was\u000abisected and previously entirely frozen as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. The smaller lymph node\u000acandidate was previously entirely submitted as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (larger bisected lymph node), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (smaller node), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2 Left breast - suture at axilla", consists of a simple mastectomy\u000aspecimen (138 gm), composed of a breast (14.2 (M-L) x REMOVED_DATE (F-I) x REMOVED_DATE (A-P))\u000aand a tan/white granular skin ellipse (14.3 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a slightly inverted\u000anipple (0.9 cm) and a pink/tan areola (2.1 cm). Per surgeon, there is a suture\u000aindicating the "axilla". Approximately REMOVED_DATE cm inferior to the nipple, there are\u000atwo transmural defects (each REMOVED_DATE cm) with regular borders with no associated\u000ahemorrhage. In between the two skin defects, contiguous with the most inferior\u000anipple and extending to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the skin margin, there is a\u000awell-healed, gray/white, irregular scar (2.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm). Lateral and inferior to\u000athe nipple areola complex in the aforementioned scar, there are four\u000aill-defined gray/tan granular nodular skin lesions (ranging from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ain greatest dimension, the closest of which is REMOVED_DATE cm from the nipple and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the skin margin.\u000aThe deep margin is a smooth fascial plane with no associated skeletal muscle\u000aand is inked blue. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral.\u000aIn the inferior/central portion of the specimen (at the junction of the lower\u000ainner and lower outer quadrant), there is an ill-defined, indurated,\u000apink/white, irregular mass (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The parenchyma immediately\u000aadjacent to the lesion exhibits multiple focal areas of hemorrhage and\u000ayellow/tan, chalky fat necrosis that extend superior and lateral to the mass\u000afor approximately REMOVED_DATE cm into the lower outer quadrant. The mass is within REMOVED_DATE \u000acm of the deep margin and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the skin (subjacent to the scar and\u000atransmural defects). The associated hemorrhage and fat necrosis is within REMOVED_DATE \u000acm of the deep margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the skin. The uninvolved breast\u000aparenchyma is composed of approximately 75% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue\u000aand approximately 25% white/tan fibrous tissue with no additional masses or\u000alesions identified. The most lateral portion of the breast is examined for\u000apossible intraparenchymal lymph node, but none are identified. Representative\u000asections to include the entire mass and adjacent fat necrosis are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass with closest deep margin and skin with scar, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of mass with additional deep margin and skin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fat necrosis and hemorrhage immediately adjacent to mass with deep\u000amargin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fat necrosis and hemorrhage, superior and lateral to lesion,\u000awith deep margin, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Two largest nodular skin lesions, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant, 2 frags. RSS.\u000almb/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Vijay K Vanguri M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/18/2005\u000a[report_end]
