VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: GREGORY J KANE M.D.\u000aCASE: BG-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Peter M Sadow, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Christopher P Crum, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade III/III), measuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size (see note).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, comedo, solid, and papillary types (high nuclear\u000agrade) with central necrosis and calcifications, is present in 5 of 7 blocks\u000a(EIC positive).\u000aInvasive and in situ carcinoma are present at tissue edges (see note).\u000aNOTE: The specimen is received fragmented and unoriented with the wire not in\u000aplace. True margins and orientation cannot be evaluated on this excision,\u000aalthough invasive carcinoma and DCIS are present at tissue edges.\u000aThe specimen radiograph showed a few calcifications. However, the targeted\u000alesion was present in the second specimen.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS, "MEDIAL TO SPECIMEN A":\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade III/III), at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size (see note).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen.\u000aDCIS, comedo and solid types (high nuclear grade) with central necrosis and\u000acalcifications, is present in 4 of 4 blocks (EIC positive).\u000aInvasive carcinoma and DCIS are present at the tissue edges.\u000aNOTE: The specimen was received in two fragments and unoriented. True margins\u000aand orientation cannot be evaluated on this excision, although invasive\u000acarcinoma and DCIS are present at tissue edges.\u000aThe radiologic lesion, a cluster of calcifications, was present in this\u000aspecimen and correlates with the calcifications present in the DCIS.\u000aThere is a focus of carcinoma within one of the fragments that measures REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aIt cannot be determined whether the patient has two or more foci of invasion or\u000aif one invasive carcinoma has been transected. The best size for AJCC\u000aclassification is REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDc.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies for ER, PR, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu have been ordered and will be\u000areported in an addendum.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Suspicious microcalcifications in left breast. Left breast tender and\u000aswollen. Some bloody discharge. Previous core negative but did not get to the\u000aarea of microcalcification.\u000aOperation: Left breast resection - wire localization\u000aOperative Findings: Fibrotic breast tissue\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcification L breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a#1) Left breast wire localization - to mammo at 11:46\u000a#2) Left breast tissue medial to specimen #1 - to mammo\u000aPerform ER/PR and Her2neu studies if positive for carcinoma\u000aRADIOLOGY: #1) Wire and hook broken and few calcifications.\u000a#2) Calcifications - no wire\u000aDr. Kane notified of all findings. Dr. Sughra Raza\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. left wire loc breast biopsy", consists of multiple\u000afragments of yellow fibrofatty breast tissue with focal areas of white fibrous\u000atissue. The specimen is received in multiple fragments without the wire placed\u000aby radiology and unoriented. In aggregate the specimen measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.6 cm. The specimen is entirely submitted for histologic analysis.\u000aThe specimen radiograph shows a few calcifications at one edge of the specimen\u000aand a broken wire.\u000aNOTE: The wire was in the container, although the specimen was fragmented and\u000ano longer associated with the wire. Dr. Sadow discussed the case with the\u000aradiologist who performed mammography.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast excision, 1 frag each (most fibro-cystic material in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), ESS\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. L. breast tissue medial to specimen #1", consists of two\u000afragments of unoriented breast tissue. The specimen comes with an accompanying\u000aradiograph that shows numerous calcifications present in one of the fragments.\u000aThough the specimen is unoriented, each of the two nodules contain a cut\u000asurface and these are inked blue. The two nodular excisions of breast tissue\u000ameasure REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The specimens contain\u000afibrous nodular, possibly calcified breast tissue. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast fragment 1, 1 and 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: additional breast fragment, 2 and 1 frags, ESS\u000apes/jl\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Peter M Sadow M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aPrelim Dx Release by Peter M Sadow M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aNo Action - Prelim Sent by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a10/27/2005\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (10/28/05):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aThere appear to be rare cells in the DCIS that are ER or PR positive.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
