VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Derek K Marsee, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Christopher D. M. Fletcher, M.D. FRCPath\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST BIOPSY":\u000aBenign breast tissue with small apocrine cysts.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia with a microscopic focus\u000aof atypia.\u000aColumnar alteration of lobules with prominent snouts and\u000asecretions with calcifications.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy (including examination of margins) .\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 44-year-old female with calcifications, left breast.\u000a1/18/06 - left breast mammo: 6 to 10 clustered punctate and indistinct\u000acalcifications present, measuring 5 mm in lower inner quadrant.\u000a2/14/06: core biopsy, left breast - "breast tissue with small apocrine cysts -\u000ano calcifications, wire localization recommended.\u000aOperation: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast biopsy - short stitch = superior, long stitch = lateral, whip\u000astitch = deep margin (FS - mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aThree groups of calcifications are within the specimen.\u000aSignature: Dr. Gombos.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "Left breast biopsy", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm oval fragment of\u000afibrofatty tissue with three orienting sutures: short stitch = superior; long\u000astitch = lateral; whip stitch = deep margin. The specimen was inked by the\u000asurgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The\u000aaccompanying radiograph shows three circled areas on the specimen, but no guide\u000awire is received. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral,\u000ashowing a predominantly fatty cut surface with scant interspersed white fibrous\u000atissue and there is a REMOVED_DATE cm area in the mid portion of the specimen, which\u000acontains radio-opaque material, located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin. This\u000aappears to correlate with at least one of the circled areas of interest. A mass\u000alesion is not seen. The specimen is entirely submitted from medial to lateral,\u000arespectively in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular medial margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial breast tissue, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue adjacent to radio-opaque material, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mid to lateral breast tissue, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular lateral margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aWP/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Derek K Marsee M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Christopher D. M. Fletcher M.D. FRCPath, Electronically\u000asigned on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
