VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID LYMPH NODE BIOPSY\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aWELCH, WILLIAM, ROBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by WELCH,WILLIAM ROBERT,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "SENTINEL NODE":\u000aOne (1) lymph node; reactive giant cells present.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy.\u000a(Three levels examined.)\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "SENTINEL NODE #2":\u000aTwo (2) lymph nodes; no tumor present.\u000a(Three levels examined.)\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "AXILLARY CONTENTS":\u000aEleven (11) lymph nodes; no tumor present.\u000aRIGHT BREAST BIOPSY, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform and solid types (intermediate nuclear\u000agrade), in three of eighteen (3:18) blocks (#12, 13, 18);\u000atunor extends into terminal ducts and lobules (block #12).\u000aMicrocalcifications and necrosis are associated with DCIS (0.4 cm and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm).\u000aDCIS is within REMOVED_DATE cm, of the inked resection margins in two (2) blocks\u000a(medial in block #12 and lateral in block #18).\u000aBiopsy site with repair reaction.\u000aFibrocystic changes are present including cysts, apocrine metaplasia.\u000aSHAVE MARGIN ("CEPHALIC NEAR CALCIFICATIONS"), RIGHT BREAST:\u000aBenign breast tissue with fiborcystic change, including cysts and\u000aapocrine metaplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 50-year-old perimenopausal female with a mass (11 x 6 mm) in the\u000aleft lower breast (8/6/1999), and a right breast mass (12mm) and cluster of\u000amicrocalcifications (4 x 7mm). Patient is s/p breast biopsy (1990), s/p needle\u000abiopsy (9/8/99)\u000aOperation: Right needle-directed re-excisional breast biopsy (M-9), sentinel\u000anode mapping with gamma probe\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast cancer\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1) Sentinel node\u000a2) Sentinel node #2\u000a3) Axillary contents\u000a4) Tissue, right breast, with needle\u000a5) Shave margin, cephalic near calcifications\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aCalcifications are in the specimen.\u000aDr. Jessica Leung\u000aDr. Darrell Smith\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by HIRSCH,MICHELLE S.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in five parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart one, "1) sentinel node", consists of a single lymph node (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.7 cm) with surrounding adipose tissue. The specimen is bisected and entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro 1: Sentinel node, 2 frag, ESS\u000aPart two, "2) sentinel node #2", consists of a single fragment of yellow/tan\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Two (2) lymph nodes are identified,\u000a(largest REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). The largest lymph node is inked black.\u000aBoth lymph nodes are bisected and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro 2: Sentinel lymph node #2, (2 lymph nodes), 4 frag, ESS\u000aPart three, "3) axillary contents", consists of multiple fragments of\u000ayellow/tan fibroadipose tissue (9.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). Multiple\u000alymph nodes are identified (largest REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). The lymph\u000anodes are entirely submitted.\u000aMicro 3: 1 lymph node, bisected, 2 frag, ESS\u000aMicro 4: 6 lymph nodes, 6 frag, ESS\u000aMicro 5: 3 lymph nodes, 4 frag, ESS\u000aMicro 6: 7 possible lymph nodes, 7 frag, ESS\u000aPart four, "4) tissue, right breast with needle", consists of a single fragment\u000aof yellow/tan fibroadipose tissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an overlying skin\u000aellipse (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The presence of scars is not identified, possible\u000asecondary to the specimen being received with ink on the skin. The specimen\u000ais received with a wire needle in place. The specimen is also received inked,\u000awith only three colors identified (red, green, and black). There are two\u000asutures attached to the skin ellipse, a short suture which is attached to the\u000ared ink (potentially representing the superior margin), and a long suture\u000aattached to the end of the skin ellipse, between the red and green inks\u000a(potentially representing the lateral margin). Orange and yellow ink will be\u000aapplied to the specimen (representing probable medial and lateral margins,\u000arespectively). Black and green ink represent probably deep and inferior\u000amargins, respectively. There is a scarred cavity (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) on the\u000agreen (inferior) surface of the specimen. There are multiple foci of white,\u000afirm tissue, potentially representing calcifications, identified at the\u000ainferior margin and along the superior aspect of the scarred cavity within the\u000ainferior portion of the specimen. No other masses or nodules are identified.\u000aThe specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Breast tissue with medial margin, multi frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Breast tissue with lateral margin, multi frag, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Breast tissue with superior, inferior, deep margin and overlying\u000askin, multi frag, ESS\u000aPart five, "5) shave margin, cephalid near microcalcifications", consists of a\u000asingle fragment of yellow/tan fibroadipose tissue (2.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen is received with red ink on a portion of the specimen. The specimen\u000ais entirely submitted for histologic analysis and evaluation of margins.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Shave margin, multi frag, ESS\u000amsh\u000aReports to: CHRISTIAN, ROGER, L. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: LYMPH NODE BIOPSY\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SURGICAL MARGIN\u000a[report_end]
