VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID SOFT TISSUE BIOPSY\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRODSKY, GILBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRODSKY,GILBERT,M.D.\u000a1) SPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT CHEST WALL TISSUE", EXCISION:\u000aBreast tissue with extensive chronic inflammation with plasmacytic\u000ainfiltrate, foreign body giant cell reaction and organizing fat necrosis.\u000aThere is involvement of vessel walls within areas of inflammation,\u000ahowever, vasculitis per se is not present.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy.\u000aSee note.\u000a2) LEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aBreast tissue with focal fibrosis consistent with old biopsy site;\u000acalcified nodule (0.6 cm). and multifocal chronic inflammation in skin\u000aand breast parenchyma (see note).\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy.\u000aNOTE: There is no evidence of malignancy in either part. There are some\u000astromal, vascular, and epithelial changes consistent with chronic irradiation\u000aeffect. The pattern of inflammatory infiltrate, with loose perivascular and\u000ainterstitial aggregates, is somewhat suggestive of a collagen-vascular disease\u000abut is non-specific.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: S/P lumpectomy and axillary LN dissection in 1989.\u000aOperation: Left mastectomy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Radiation necrosis left breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1) Chest wall tissue, left\u000a#2) Left breast with stitch on lateral side\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by LIN,JONATHAN H.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in two parts, each labeled with the patient's name and\u000aunit number.\u000aPart one, labeled "#1. left chest wall tissue", consists of two fragments of\u000ayellow/white fibroadipose tissue; larger fragment measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm,\u000athe slightly smaller fragment measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. Both specimens\u000aappear to be fibrotic. No discrete masses are identified. No skin is present.\u000aRepresentative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro 1: 2 frags from larger chest wall fragment, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro 2: 2 frags from slightly smaller chest wall fragment, 2 frags, RSS\u000aPart two, labeled "#2. left breast stitch on lateral side", consists of a 574g\u000asimple mastectomy specimen measuring 23 x 15 x 4 cm with a skin ellipse\u000ameasuring 19 x 11 cm, nipple that is REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter. Sectioning of the\u000aspecimen reveals greater than 85% yellow adipose tissue. A focus of possible\u000acalcification is identified that may represent a former biopsy site or healed\u000ascar site. This area of calcification measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and is 1 cm\u000afrom the overlying skin ellipse and REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep black inked margin.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  area of fibrosis calcification with respect to black inked deep\u000amargin\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  area of fibrosis with respect to overlying skin ellipse, 2 frags,\u000aRSS\u000aMicro 7: UOQ inked black and UIQ uninked, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro 8: LOQ inked black\u000aMicro 9: LIQ, 1 frag\u000aMicro 10: nipple, 4 frags, ESS\u000ajhl\u000aReports to: IGLEHART, JAMES, DIRK M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: SOFT TISSUE BIOPSY\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000a[report_end]
