VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: COLLEEN MURPHY M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Anna Laury, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Robert D Odze, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST INFERIOR LESION WIRE-LOCALIZED LUMPECTOMY:\u000aBreast tissue with calcifications in benign epithelium,\u000afocal columnar cell change, and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION WIRE-LOCALIZED LUMPECTOMY FOR A MASS AND A\u000aCLIP:\u000aBreast tissue with focal columnar cell change and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium. Changes consistent with prior core\u000abiopsy site. there is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination\u000aof the specimen, including margins.\u000aC. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION, MEDIAL MARGIN:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia. Changes consistent with prior core biopsy\u000asite.\u000aD. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION, LATERAL MARGIN:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia. Changes consistent with prior core biopsy\u000asite.\u000aE. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION, SUPERIOR MARGIN:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU (0.4 CM), solid, cribriform and micropapillary\u000atypes. Low nuclear grade. Ductal carcinoma in situ is present within\u000a1 mm of the new margin.\u000aF. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION, FINAL ANTERIOR MARGIN:\u000aBenign breast tissue.\u000aG. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR LESION, SECOND MEDIAL MARGIN:\u000aBenign breast tissue.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of silicone implants bilaterally.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized excision x2 wires.\u000aOperative Findings: Clip in medial margin.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Atypical hyperplasia left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast inferior lesion long suture lateral short suture superior to\u000amammo.\u000aB/2. Left breast superior lesion long suture lateral, short suture superior to\u000amammo.\u000aC/3. Left breast superior lesion medial margin to mammo.\u000aD/4. Left breast superior lesion lateral margin.\u000aE/5. Left breast superior lesion superior margin.\u000aF/6. Left breast superior lesion final anterior margin.\u000aG/7. Left breast superior lesion second medial margin.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000a(1)Specimen with hook and wire. Called Dr. Murphy. Swan 1:10 pm.\u000a(2)Mass in specimen. No clip. OR called. DG.\u000a(3)Clip in specimen/ OR called. DG.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in seven parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast inferior lesion, long suture lateral, short suture\u000asuperior", consists of a lumpectomy specimen (2.8 g, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a\u000alocalization wire entering at the superior/lateral aspect. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned to reveal yellow/hemorrhagic breast tissue;\u000aa definitive clip or biopsy site is not identified. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from superior to inferior and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast inferior lesion, superior to inferior, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Left breast superior lesion, long suture lateral, short suture\u000asuperior", consists of a lumpectomy specimen (3.4 g, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a\u000alocalization wire received within the container, however, the wire is not\u000awithin the specimen. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The cut section of the specimen\u000ais composed of yellow lobulated breast tissue. No definite biopsy site or clip\u000ais identified, and the specimen is entirely submitted from superior to\u000ainferior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, superior to inferior, 10 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, "#3. Left breast superior lesion medial margin", consists of a single\u000afragment of fibroadipose tissue (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with one flat side inked\u000ablack by the surgeon; the opposing side is inked blue. A clip is identified at\u000aone edge. The specimen is entirely submitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, medial margin, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aPart D, "#4. Left breast superior lesion lateral margin", consists of a single\u000afragment of breast tissue (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with one flat side inked black\u000aby the surgeon. The other side is inked blue, and the specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, lateral margin, 8 frags, ESS.\u000aPart E, "#5. Left breast superior lesion final superior margin", consists of a\u000asingle fragment of breast tissue (1.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with one flat side inked\u000ablack by the surgeon. The other side is inked blue, and the specimen is\u000aentirely submitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, final superior margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart F, "#6. Left breast superior lesion final anterior margin", consists of a\u000asingle fragment of breast tissue (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) received with one flat\u000aside inked black by the surgeon. The other side is inked blue, and the\u000aspecimen is entirely submitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, final anterior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aPart G, "#7. Left breast superior lesion second medial margin", consists of a\u000asingle fragment of breast tissue (1.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with one flat side inked\u000ablack by the surgeon; the other side is inked blue, and the specimen is\u000aentirely submitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left breast superior lesion, second medial margin, 8 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aarl/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Robert D Odze M.D., Electronically signed on Monday REMOVED_MONTH\u000a22, 2009 at 11:27:10AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (6/26/09)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE - LOW\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aFriday REMOVED_MONTH 26, 2009 at 03:55:22PM\u000a[report_end]
