VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Cyst, other, non-neoplastic\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Matthew D. Stachler, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, CYST EXCISION:\u000aBenign ductal cyst.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A PALPABLE MASS:\u000aPHYLLODES TUMOR, low grade, REMOVED_DATE cm (see note).\u000aThe stroma is moderately cellular with moderate nuclear atypia.\u000aMitoses are present (focally up to 1 per 10 high power fields).\u000aMild stromal overgrowth is present.\u000aThe tumor focally infiltrates into the adjacent breast tissue.\u000aThe tumor is present at the anterior margin, < REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior and\u000amedial margins, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The tumor is > REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the lateral margin. The superior margin is not well seen.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies are performed with the following results for\u000alesional cells:\u000aSmooth muscle myosin heavy chain POSITIVE\u000aCytokeratin (PANK) NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID NEGATIVE\u000aNOTE: The stromal cells have an unusual myofibroblastic appearance with\u000aboth spindle and epithelial morphologies. Immunohistochemical studies\u000asupport that these are myofibroblasts and not of epithelial, myoepithelial,\u000aor vascular origin.\u000aThe differential diagnosis includes a fibroadenoma extensively involved\u000aby pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia. The presence of occasional\u000amitoses and nuclear atypia favor classification as a phyllodes tumor.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A MASS AND A CLIP:\u000aBenign breast tissue with columnar cell change and pseudoangiomatous stromal\u000ahyperplasia (PASH).\u000aNOTE: The patient underwent a prior core needle biopsy for a REMOVED_DATE cm irregular\u000amass and the biopsy showed PASH (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). PASH is again present in this\u000aexcisional biopsy. Although biopsy site changes are not seen, there has been a\u000along interval since the prior biopsy. The clip at the biopsy site was present.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the entire\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 25 year old with right breast fibroepithelial lesion and right breast\u000apseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia, S/P core biopsy. Right breast mole.\u000aOperation: 1. Right breast wire localized excisional biopsy, 2. Right breast\u000abiopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: 1. Right breast mass fibroepithelial lesion 2. Right breast\u000amole.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Right breast cyst excision.\u000aB/2) Right breast excisional biopsy - short suture/superior, long/lateral.\u000aC/3) Right breast wire localized excisional biopsy - short suture/superior,\u000along/lateral (mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen #3 - specimen x-ray shows clip and mass and intact wire. Habgo.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right breast cyst excision", consists of a single\u000airregular red/brown fragment of soft tissue (0.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The entire\u000aspecimen is submitted for histologic evaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Right breast excisional biopsy short suture superior long\u000alateral", consists of a tan/yellow firm lobulated fragment of breast tissue\u000a(2.8 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior). The specimen is marked with sutures as follows: Short equals\u000asuperior, long equals lateral. There is no needle localization wire. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is sectioned from lateral to medial to reveal a\u000afirm homogeneous irregularly-bordered white mass, which encompasses\u000aapproximately 95% of the specimen (2.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The white mass abuts\u000athe posterior, anterior, inferior, superior, lateral, and medial margins. The\u000aentire specimen is submitted for histologic evaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially sectioned, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen in relation to posterior, superior, anterior, and\u000ainferior margins, 1 frag each (2 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially sectioned, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Right breast wire localized excisional biopsy short suture\u000asuperior long lateral", consists of a single irregular tan/yellow firm\u000alobulated fragment of breast tissue (3.2 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial\u000ato lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior). The specimen is marked by sutures\u000aas follows: Short equals superior, long equals lateral. The specimen is inked\u000aper pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen contains a needle localization wire, which enters medially and\u000aexits laterally and marks a clip and a mass as demonstrated on the accompanying\u000aradiograph. The clip is loosely visible at the anterior margin. The specimen\u000ais serially sectioned from inferior to superior to reveal a homogeneous white,\u000airregular-bordered mass, which appears to encompass approximately 95% of the\u000aspecimen. The remainder of the specimen is yellow fatty tissue. The firm\u000awhite area borders the inferior margin, the superior margin, the anterior\u000amargin, the posterior margin, the lateral margin, and the medial margin. The\u000aentire specimen is submitted for histologic evaluation from inferior to\u000asuperior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior border, radially sectioned, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen serially sectioned from inferior to superior to\u000ainclude anterior, posterior, medial and lateral borders, 2 frags each (1 frag\u000ain REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior border, radially sectioned, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aEAM/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Friday August 21, 2009 at 04:35:42PM\u000a[report_end]
