VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: Esther Rhei M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Samuel G Katz, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, RE-EXCISION:\u000aResidual fibroepithelial lesion (approximately REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aLesion is present at the medial margin and very close to (<0.1 cm)\u000athe superior margin (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThe remaining margins appear negative.\u000aCore biopsy site.\u000aNOTE: Evaluation is limited by core biopsy site changes, tissue fragmentation,\u000aand cautery artifact.\u000aCase also reviewed by Dr. Dillon, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 45 yr old female screening mammogram 6 mm nodule in left breast USN\u000ademonstrated 8 mm solid mass @ 12 o'clock axis corresponding to mammographic\u000afinding. REMOVED_DATE  core bx cellular fibroepithelial lesion with stromal\u000aovergrowth --> excision recommended.\u000aOperation: (L) breast wire-loc biopsy\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal L) mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a(1) Left bst. tissue: long-lateral; short-superior; whipped deep margin to\u000amammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aClip in the specimen (DM) (A.T.)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "1. Left breast tissue: Long-lateral, short-superior, whipped stitch-\u000adeep margin", and consists of one (1) fragment of breast tissue with a wire, 3\u000astitches and an accompanying radiograph and report. The specimen comes\u000apre-inked by the surgeon. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000asuperior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral x REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior to\u000aposterior. The breast tissue has a homogeneous, tan/pink appearance with\u000aapproximately 30% fibrous tissue. The specimen is cut into two slices, and the\u000aclip is found in slice 1 after much fragmentation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fragments of Slice 1, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000asak/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Samuel G Katz M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFIRST ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE  DR. DEBORAH DILLON\u000aOnly a very small amount (0.3 cm) of the fibroepithelial lesion is present in\u000athis material. Evaluation is complicated by biopsy site changes and cautery\u000aeffect. On the basis of this material, it is not possible to distinguish\u000acellular fibroadenoma from low-grade phyllodes tumor. Close clinical follow-up\u000ais suggested.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
