l as a localization wire. The specimen is inked\u000afor the evaluation of margins: Posterior black, anterior blue, superior red,\u000ainferior green, lateral yellow, medial orange. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from anterior to posterior into sixteen slices (anterior margin slice\u000a#1 and posterior margin slice #16). There is an ill-defined/white firm\u000afibrotic area with central hemorrhage (probable core biopsy site) located at\u000athe posterior beside of the specimen. The lesion grossly abuts superior and\u000amedial margin and is less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000alateral and posterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin. The lesion\u000ameasures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The rest of the specimen is composed mostly of\u000ayellow adipose tissue (10% fibrous tissue and 90% adipose tissue). The\u000apossible lesion with core biopsy is entirely submitted for microscopic\u000aexamination as well as 100% of the residual fibrotic area. The ill-defined\u000amass is in slices #10 to #15.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: The anterior margin, slice #1, perpendicular section, multi frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrotic area of slice #4 and #5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrotic area of slice #7, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrotic area of slice #8, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrotic area of slice #9, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #11, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #12, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #13, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #14, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #15, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #16, posterior margin, perpendicular section, multi frags,\u000aESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aQY/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jeffrey F Krane M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/21/2007\u000a[report_end]
