VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aHIRSCH, MICHELLE, S., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by HIRSCH,MICHELLE S.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - solid, cribriform\u000aNuclear grade - intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - absent\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - absent\u000aMicroinvasion (<0.1cm) - absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks:total - 3:12 blocks (blocks 3, 6, 8)\u000aFinal Margins (DCIS)\u000aClose (0.1) - posterior (focal; block 3)\u000aVery close (<0.1 cm) - medial, inferior (focal; block 8)\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aPapilloma\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC CLASSIFICATION (6th edition) - Tis NX MX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES: To be reported in an addendum\u000aNote: The DCIS is present as multiple small foci adjacent to the prior core\u000aneedle biopsy site and is best seen on the original level of block 8. Although\u000aa diagnosis of severely atypical ductal hyperplasia could be considered, as\u000amany of the spaces are only partially involved, DCIS is favored due to the\u000apresence of some spaces that are completely filled.\u000aThe calcifications are associated with benign epithelium and do not appear to\u000abe associated with the areas of DCIS.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Drs. Stuart Schnitt and Susan Lester (Breast\u000aPathology), who concur with the diagnosis of DCIS.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block 8):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity\u000ain >10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 50-year-old female whose right breast mammogram demonstrates a 12 mm\u000acluster of calcifications in the UOQ. S/P core biopsy (5/7/03) revealing\u000aatypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aOperation: Right breast, wire-localization excision\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal right mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Right breast, tissue\u000aMarking stitch: short.superior; long/lateral; whipstitch/deep margin (to mammo)\u000aRadiology Report:\u000aCalcifications and wire in specimen. S. Raza M.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BENNETT,MICHAEL W.,M.B.,B.C.H\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "1) right breast tissue (short/superior, long/lateral, whipstitch/deep\u000amargin)", and consists of a portion of fibrofatty tissue (3.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aThe specimen has been marked by the surgeon with three (3) sutures\u000a(short/superior, long/lateral, whipstitch/deep margin). The specimen is inked\u000aby the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior\u000a= black; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow;\u000amedial = orange. A wire-localizing needle enters the specimen at the superior\u000amargin. The needle tip lies near the inferior portion of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from inferior to superior and consists of 95%\u000awhite, fibrous tissue which is focally hemorrhagic and extends to all margins,\u000agrossly. There is a yellow, firm nodule (0.2 cm in maximum dimension), which\u000aappears calcified, grossly (micro #8).\u000aThe specimen radiograph received with the specimen reveals one large\u000acalcification surrounded by numerous minute calcifications and the localization\u000awire.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  entire specimen from inferior to superior, 12 frag, ESS\u000amwb\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
