VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: Esther Rhei M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Joanna A Gibson, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jonathan N Glickman, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aINVASIVE TUBULAR CARCINOMA\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm (see NOTE)\u000aMultifocal - no\u000aHistologic Type - tubular\u000aHistologic Grade (SBR) - I (well)\u000aNuclear Grade - low\u000aEIC - negative\u000aLymphovascular invasion - absent\u000aDermal lymphatic invasion - N/A\u000aSkin invasion - N/A\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - N/A\u000aFinal Margins (invasive) -\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - lateral, posterior, inferior, superior,\u000aanterior\u000aClose (0.1-0.3 cm) - medial (0.2 cm; focal, block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - absent\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aComplex sclerosing lesion\u000aColumnar hyperplasia with atypia\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aAJCC CLASSIFICATION (6th edition) - REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa pNx MX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) - pending\u000aNOTE: p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID immunostain confirms the absence of myoepithelial cells in\u000ainvasive focus, supporting the diagnosis of carcinoma. This case was reviewed\u000aby Dr. A. Richardson of the Breast Pathology Service, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 43 year-old female screening mammogram at Quincy City Hospital\u000ademonstrated calcifications lower inner breast; USN (BWH) irregular area with\u000acystic spaces 2 x REMOVED_DATE  c, compatible with clustered dilated ducts at the site\u000aof mammographic density and associated with clustered segmental calcifications.\u000aCore biopsy - ADH. Excision recommended. Previous surgery - BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000a(03/03/2005).\u000aOperation: Left wire localized excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast calcifications.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. (L) breast tissue, short stitch superior, long lateral, whip stitch on\u000adeep margin - to mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aClip + Ca++ in specimen (Meyer).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "(L) breast tissue", and consists of a wire localized breast excision\u000aspecimen (4.4 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aanterior to posterior). The specimen is received inked by the surgeon per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThere are two localization wires in place protruding from the inferior, and\u000amedial margins. The specimen is received with a radiograph, which shows\u000amicrocalcifications, localization wires, and clip within the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from inferior to superior and in ten slices.\u000aThe specimen re-radiographed to display a surgical clip in slice #3 and\u000aadditional calcifications. The specimen consists of 80% yellow lobulated\u000aadipose tissue, admixed with white to pink firm fibrous tissue. The specimen\u000ais entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 1, with inferior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 2, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, with surgical clip, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 6, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 7, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 8, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 9, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 10, superior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aJgi/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Joanna A Gibson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jonathan N Glickman M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/12/2005\u000aSurgical Addendum:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor - POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor - POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/13/2005\u000a[report_end]
