VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOGINO, SHUJI, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by OGINO,SHUJI,M.D.,PH.D.\u000a1) SPECIMEN LABELED "SUBAREOLAR MASS, RIGHT BREAST":\u000aBreast tissue with atypical lobular hyperplasia, intraductal papilloma\u000awith apocrine metaplasia\u000a(0.6 cm) and adjacent prior biopsy site, epithelial hyperplasia,\u000acysts, and calcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy, including margins.\u000a2) SPECIMEN LABELED "UPPER INNER QUADRANT, RIGHT BREAST":\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH APOCRINE FEATURES, INTERMEDIATE NUCLEAR\u000aGRADE, INVOLVING AN INTRADUCTAL PAPILLOMA (blocks #19, #21).\u000aMultiple intraductal papillomas with apocrine metaplasia and prior biopsy\u000asite.\u000aThe surgical resection margins are negative for malignancy.\u000aSelected slides (#7, #19 and #21) were reviewed by Dr. S. Schnitt, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe DCIS is difficult to evaluate for ER expression as the foci within the\u000aintraductal papilloma are quite small and very little definitive DCIS is\u000apresent on the deeper levels. The cells of the papilloma are positive for ER\u000aand PR, but the DCIS cannot be evaluated. There is a REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu positive\u000apopulation of cells within the papilloma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 57 year old female with mammogram with abnormality.\u000aOperation: Right needle directed breast biopsies x 2\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast masses x 2\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1) Subareolar mass right breast\u000a#2) Upper inner quadrant right breast\u000aRADIOLOGY: #1) Clip in specimen. D. Smith,MD\u000a#2) clip in specimen. D. Smith,MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BUNDOCK,ELIZABETH A.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart one, labeled "#1. subareolar mass right breast", consists of a portion of\u000afibroadipose tissue (4.5 x 4 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a localizing wire in place and one\u000aclip identified. The specimen is received with an accompanyinmg radiograph\u000awhich is reported as "clip in specimen", signed by D. Smith. MD. The specimen\u000ais unoriented and inked black. The specimen is cut at 3 mm intervals revealing\u000aa specimen which is approximately 70% rubbery fibroadipose tissue with diffuse\u000ahemorrhage and focal fat necrosis. A prior biopsy site is present as a REMOVED_DATE cm\u000acystic area within which a clip is present. Adjacent to the biopsy cavity\u000athere is a tan/white rubbery nodule (0.7 cm greatest dimension) which extends\u000ato one margin of resection. The specimen is entirely submitted in series with\u000athe area of the clip and adjacent mass present in blocks number three through\u000asix.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 20 frags, ESS\u000aPart two, labeled "#2. upper inner quadrant right breast", consists of one\u000aportion of fibroadipose tissue (4.2 x REMOVED_DATE x 2 cm) with a localizing wire and a\u000aclip present. The specimen is received with an accompanying radiograph which\u000ais reported as "clip in specimen", signed by D. Smith, MD. The specimen is\u000aunoriented and inked black. The specimen is sectioned at 3 mm intervals\u000arevealing 80% adipose tissue with a centrally located area of hemorrhage and\u000afat necrosis surrounding a clip. There is also a cystic space (0.3 cm) present\u000ain this general area, but not adjacent to the clip, which may represent a prior\u000abiopsy site. There are no firm masses suggestive of carcinoma present in the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is entirely submitted, with the area of the clip being\u000ain block twenty-one.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, ESS\u000aeab\u000aReports to: NAUTA, RUSSELL, JAMES\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SURGICAL MARGIN\u000a[report_end]
