VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy with regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Paula C Abreu-e-Lima, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Andrea L Richardson, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA) LEFT BREAST, MASTECTOMY, PROPHYLACTIC:\u000aBenign breast parenchyma with calcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aB) RIGHT BREAST, MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY AND AXILLARY NODE DISSECTION:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA, 5 FOCI, AND METASTATIC CARCINOMA TO LYMPH NODES.\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aRESIDUAL INVASIVE CARCINOMA. There are approximately 5 foci of\u000asparsely-cellular invasive carcinoma, the largest focus involves an area\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE cm. (See NOTE.)\u000aHistologic Grade (SBR) III(Poor)\u000aNuclear Grade High\u000aEIC Negative\u000aLymphovascular invasion Absent\u000aDermal lymphatic invasion Absent\u000aSkin invasion Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion N/A\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) Deep\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU Absent\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU Absent\u000aLYMPH NODES\u000aNumber of positive nodes 13\u000aNumber of nodes examined 15\u000aTOTAL POSITIVE NODES (all specimens) REMOVED_DATE \u000aSize of largest metastatic focus REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aExtranodal extension Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aTumor bed with treatment effect.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) Pending\u000aNOTE: The residual carcinoma consists of sparsely cellular collections of\u000asingle and clustered malignant histiocytoid-appearing tumor cells. The foci\u000arange in size from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm. These foci are in a large area of fibrosis\u000awith hemosiderin deposition consistent with treated tumor bed. The lymph nodes\u000aappear mostly replaced with fibrosis tissue with scattered collections of\u000aviable tumor cells. All 15 nodes have fibrous replacement but viable tumor\u000acells are seen in 13 of these.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: *Call breast attending on arrival.\u000a#1. Locally advanced right breast cancer, s/p neoadjuvant chemotherapy. 6\u000acycles of Adriamycin/cytoxan.\u000a#2. Left breast high risk for breast cancer.\u000aOperation: None given.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: #1. Locally advanced right breast cancer, s/p neoadjuvant\u000achemotherapy.\u000a#2. Left breast high risk for breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a#1. Left breast - short stitch superior, long suture axillary tail.\u000a#2. Right breast modified - short suture superior, long suture axillary tail.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. left breast short stitch superior, long axillary tail",\u000aconsists of a 510 gram left mastectomy specimen (23.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a\u000awhite/tan skin ellipse (12.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) and an attached axillary tail (8.0 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm). The nipple is everted and REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter. The specimen is\u000aoriented with two sutures (long is designated "axillary tail", short designated\u000a"superior"). A REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm hemorrhagic area is present in the upper\u000ainner quadrant, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin (inked black). The remainder\u000aof the breast parenchyma is predominantly white and firm and composed of dense\u000afibrous tissue. Within the axillary tail is a firm ill-defined area (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm), which is REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin. Three lymph nodes are\u000aidentified in the axillary tissue ranging from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest\u000adimension. The remaining breast parenchyma is predominantly fibrosis.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Hemorrhagic area to fibrous parenchyma, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Hemorrhage to deep margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: LIQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: UIQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: LOQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: UOQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin, areola, and nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 lymph node differently inked, black bisected, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional tail, mult frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Firm area within axillary tissue, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Deep margin, in relation to firm area, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. right breast", consists of a 553 gram modified radical\u000amastectomy specimen (20.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a white/tan skin ellipse (13.0\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm) and an attached axillary tail (8.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The nipple is REMOVED_DATE \u000acm and unremarkable. A radiograph of the specimen was taken and shows a clip\u000awithin the upper outer quadrant. Within the specimen, corresponding to the\u000aarea containing the clip is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm firm hemorrhagic area, which\u000ais REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin. Fifteen lymph nodes are identified in the\u000aaxillary tail, two of which are grossly matted. The lymph nodes range from REMOVED_DATE \u000ato REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Hemorrhagic area to include deep margin, mult frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: UOQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: LOQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: UIQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: LIQ, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple to include adjacent skin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 lymph nodes, one of which is bisected, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 9 lymph nodes total, 9 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: A single lymph node each, bisected, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMICRO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Two matted nodes, sectioned in four parts, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aThe area containing the clip is in cassette REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aPal/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Review by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/15/2005\u000aSurgical Addendum:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aInsufficient tumor for analysis.\u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/16/2005\u000a[report_end]
