tioning of the specimen in a\u000asuperior to inferior direction into ten slices reveals a localization clip in\u000aslice #5. The sectioning reveals breast tissue that is comprised of\u000aapproximately 60% fibrous and 40% adipose tissue. Entire specimen is submitted\u000afor histologic analysis.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior tip, 4 frags, perpendicular, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, including area around clip, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10 (inferior margin), perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aCJD/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday REMOVED_MONTH 31, 2009 at 07:50:26AM\u000a[report_end]
