VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JULIE ANN RITNER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: George Lin, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, 14 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR A REMOVED_DATE \u000aCM PALPABLE CYSTIC CIRCUMSCRIBED MASS WITH BRANCHING DYSTROPHIC AND PLEOMORPHIC\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aINVASIVE ADENOCARCINOMA, poorly differentiated, with apocrine features,\u000ameasuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size (see note). An immunoperoxidase study for\u000ap63 and cytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID confirms the absence of myoepithelial cells in\u000athe areas of invasion.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen in this limited specimen.\u000aNo carcinoma in situ is seen in this limited specimen.\u000aProminent heterotopic bone formation is present.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for\u000ainvasive carcinoma:\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE - LOW\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu POSITIVE (3+)\u000aCytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID POSITIVE\u000aCytokeratin 14 POSITIVE\u000aCytokeratin (PANK) POSITIVE\u000aNote: The carcinoma has a very unusual histologic appearance. The\u000acarcinoma invades as large circumscribed nests of cells with a micropapillary\u000aor cribriform pattern. Necrosis is present. Bone formation is present,\u000abut the associated osteocytes and reactive stroma appear to be benign.\u000aThe stromal cells are not immunoreactive for cytokeratin.\u000aThe block will be sent for FISH analysis for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu amplification.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: Large palpable complex cystic mass - very hemorrhagic. Malignant\u000acells were present on FNA. Differential diagnosis: primary breast cancer vs\u000aother malignancy. Time in formalin - 2 pm.\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left: REMOVED_DATE cm, palpable, circumscribed, complex cystic mass.\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: upper/outer.\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 14 G Ultrasound.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast core biopsy in one orange cassette.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, in one part, labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number, and "orange left breast", and consists of an orange\u000acassette, which contains multiple tan-pink fibrofatty cores (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ain aggregate), which are submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue received in orange cassette, multi frags, ESS.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in < 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >\u000a30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000almc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Friday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 18, 2009 at 05:19:40PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (9/28/09)\u000aFISH was performed by Clarient using the PathVysion HER-2 DNA Probe Kit with\u000athe following (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aResults: AMPLIFIED\u000aHER-2/CEP-17 ratio = REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average of HER-2 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average centromere 17 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aREFERENCE RANGE:\u000aNOT AMPLIFIED HER-2/CEP-17 ratio < REMOVED_DATE \u000aEQUIVOCAL HER-2/CEP-17 ratio REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE \u000aAMPLIFIED REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/CEP-17 ratio > REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday REMOVED_MONTH 28, 2009 at 05:33:55PM\u000a[report_end]
