VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth Amy Morgan, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD; Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST UPPER OUTER QUADRANT, EXCISION FOR A PALPABLE MASS:\u000aFIBROEPITHELIAL LESION (1.5 cm) (see NOTE).\u000aThe stroma is hypercellular but there is neither stromal overgrowth present nor\u000astromal nuclear atypia. No heterologous elements are present. Stromal mitoses\u000anumber up to REMOVED_DATE HPF\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia without atypia is present.\u000aThe lesion has a pushing, lobulated margin.\u000aThe lesion is marginally excised with tumor focally present < REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aposterior resection margin.\u000aPrior biopsy site changes are present.\u000aNOTE: There are a striking number of mitoses in the epithelium as well as\u000astroma which is unusual for a fibroadenoma. Note is made that the patient has\u000arecently received IVF related hormonal therapy which could possibly alter the\u000amitotic rate of a fibroadenoma. The differential diagnosis includes a cellular\u000afibroadenoma and a low grade phyllodes tumor. Over all, given the lack of\u000astromal overgrowth associated with the increased mitotic rate, and pushing\u000arather than infiltrative margin, there are insufficient features for a\u000adiagnosis of low grade phyllodes tumor and so a diagnosis of cellular\u000afibroadenoma is favored. Dr. Jane Brock and Dr. Susan Lester of the Breast\u000aPathology service have both reviewed the case and agree with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 38-year-old premenopausal woman status post REMOVED_DATE  core biopsy left\u000abreast (upper outer quadrant) of palpable mass revealing atypical epithelial\u000aproliferation present with fibroadenoma, but best evaluated with excision.\u000aOperation: Excision of left breast mass.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Not given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast mass, short suture superior, long lateral, whipstitch chest\u000awall margin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast mass short suture superior, long suture lateral,\u000awhipstitch on chest wall margin", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision\u000aof fibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating superior (short\u000asuture), lateral (long suture), and chest wall (whipstitch) margins. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange (inked by prosector). Cut surfaces are comprised of\u000apredominantly tan/pink fibrodense tissue (at least 90% of specimen), with a\u000acircumscribed REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm tan/white, lobulated firm nodule abutting the\u000achest wall margin, within REMOVED_DATE cm of the closest medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the\u000aclosest lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the closest inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of\u000aclosest anterior margin, and remote (greater than REMOVED_DATE cm) from the superior\u000amargin. The specimen is sectioned and entirely sequentially submitted from\u000asuperior to inferior, with perpendicular superior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aperpendicular inferior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adb/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
