VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Shakti Ramkissoon, M.D., Ph.D.; Justine A Barletta, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed ductal and lobular features, solid type\u000a(intermediate nuclear grade), with necrosis, is present in three blocks,\u000afocally in association with biopsy site changes (see note).\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU, solid type (low nuclear grade), is extensively present (see\u000anote).\u000aThree foci of MICROINVASION are present (largest focus measuring REMOVED_DATE cm; slides\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Microinvasion is confirmed on immunohistochemistry for\u000ap63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is identified.\u000aMicroinvasive carcinoma is present <0.1 cm from the deep margin (slides 10 and\u000a20). All other margins are negative (>0.3 cm) for invasive carcinoma.\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU (intermediate nuclear grade) is present REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep\u000amargin (slide 14).\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU (low nuclear grade) is present at or <0.1 cm from all margins\u000a(see note).\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA is present (slide 19).\u000aCalcifications present in association with benign epithelium.\u000aNOTE: The low-grade in situ component is present in twenty-three of twenty-four\u000ablocks of tissue, including at or <0.1 cm from superior, inferior, anterior,\u000aposterior, medial and lateral margins. Although this low-grade in situ\u000acarcinoma morphologically resembles LCIS, its association with intermediate\u000agrade CIS with necrosis, the association of both low-grade and\u000aintermediate-grade CIS with multifocal microinvasion and the absence of\u000aimmunoreactivity for progesterone receptor in both the low-grade and\u000aintermediate-grade CIS are unusual and suggest that both the low-grade and the\u000aintermediate-grade CIS may not behave as usual lobular in situ neoplasia.\u000aSlides also seen by Dr. Susan Lester, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 53 year old female with R breast cancer in situ carcinoma with mixed\u000aductal and lobular features.\u000aOperation: R breast wire localized partial mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R breast DCIS.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) R breast tissue with wire (standard in please don't apply extra ink to\u000acrevis(sic) on anterior margin).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen has wire and clip. Dr. BAL called/SR.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, and unit\u000anumber, and "#1. Right breast tissue with wire", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (medial\u000ato lateral) x REMOVED_DATE (inferior to superior) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior) cm\u000abreast biopsy with a localization wire in place, which enters the specimen at\u000athe lateral/anterior border. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates\u000aa clip along the superior/medial margins. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The medial and\u000alateral segments are removed and submitted as perpendicular sections, and the\u000aremainder of the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a tan/white cut\u000asurface with a focal area of hemorrhage, measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm and a tan/brown area of firmness, measuring REMOVED_DATE cm x REMOVED_DATE cm along the\u000asuperior/posterior border. The specimen is entirely submitted for analysis.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial segment, perpendicular sections, 2 frags per cassette, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral segment, perpendicular sections, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections of the specimen, bisected, 1 frag per cassette,\u000aESS (the specimen clip is located in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aSRK/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (10/02/07):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU MICROINVASION\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNOTE: Focal immunoreactivity for progesterone receptor is seen in normal\u000aepithelium and in low-grade carcinoma in situ; however, the majority of the\u000acarcinoma in situ (both low nuclear grade and intermediate nuclear grade) is\u000anegative for progesterone receptor.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
