VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy with regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Andrew G. Evans, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, MASTECTOMY, AND AXILLARY DISSECTION (TWO SPECIMENS):\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, moderately differentiated (modified Bloom-\u000aRichardson grade II/III), with micropapillary features, is present as\u000asmall foci (0.6 cm and < REMOVED_DATE cm; blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) within stromal fibrosis,\u000aadjacent to a clip marking a prior core needle biopsy site (see note).\u000aExtensive lymphovascular invasion is present in the upper inner quadrant\u000a(multiple foci in 14 of 18 blocks). Dermal lymphovascular invasion is\u000apresent at the nipple (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Focal tumor necrosis is present.\u000aNo ductal carcinoma in situ is seen (EIC negative).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aThe deep margin is free of carcinoma.\u000aMETASTATIC CARCINOMA is present in 5 of 26 lymph nodes. The largest\u000alymph node metastasis consists of multiple tumor foci over a REMOVED_DATE cm area.\u000aThe remaining metastases measure REMOVED_DATE cm, REMOVED_DATE cm, REMOVED_DATE cm, and < REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aFocal extranodal invasion is present (0.1 cm).\u000aNote: The patient presented with a palpable painful breast mass in the\u000aupper inner quadrant. The mass was REMOVED_DATE cm in size by ultrasound and 3 cm by\u000aMR. The extent of the tumor was difficult to determine as there were\u000amultiple smaller adjacent masses. The skin was thickened, but a skin\u000abiopsy did not show evidence of cancer involvement (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Two\u000alymph nodes were enlarged (2.4 cm and REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aA core needle biopsy of the largest mass revealed invasive carcinoma that\u000awas positive for ER and PR and negative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThe patient underwent neoadjuvant therapy. The mass became less evident\u000aalthough an area of fullness was present. The skin thickening resolved and\u000athe lymph nodes decreased in size. A persistent area of MRI enhancement in\u000athe upper outer quadrant was biopsied and showed LCIS involving a fibroadenoma\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aIn the mastectomy, there was a gross 28 x 12 cm fibrotic area in the\u000aupper inner quadrant. The clip marking a prior core needle biopsy was\u000apresent in this area. The majority of this area consists of fibrotic\u000abreast tissue consistent with tumor bed with numerous foci of lymphovascular\u000ainvasion. Only two small foci of stromal invasion are present. The\u000aoverall tumor cellularity is approximately 1%. The carcinoma appears\u000aviable.\u000aThe lymph node metastases are associated with mild areas of fibrosis\u000aconsistent with a minimal response to treatment. The residual carcinoma in\u000athe lymph nodes appears viable.\u000aThe overall findings are consistent with a minimal response to treatment.\u000aThe RCB score is REMOVED_DATE (RCB-III). The Miller-Payne score would be 4.\u000aThe ER, PR, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu studies will be repeated on the largest lymph\u000anode metastasis.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition) REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa with two\u000afoci of invasion.\u000aMEDIAL MASTECTOMY SKIN:\u000aNormal skin.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 52 yo surgically post-menopausal female s/p Cytoxan, Adriamycin with\u000aTaxol for L UIQ core biopsy diagnosed invasive adenocarcinoma, Her2neu\u000anegative, L axillary lymph node FNA positive for malignant cells.\u000aOperation: L modified radical mastectomy\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided\u000aClinical Diagnosis: L breast cancer, s/p neoadjuvant chemotherapy.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. Left Breast, short superior, long lateral, bowtie on axillary contents.\u000aB. Level 1+2 axillary contents.\u000aC. Medial mastectomy skin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left breast, short superior, long lateral, bow-tie on\u000aaxillary contents". The specimen consists of a REMOVED_DATE gram left total\u000amastectomy (39.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), containing a skin ellipse (26.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awith an areola (4.5 cm in diameter) and nipple (1.6 cm). The specimen is\u000aoriented by a short suture at the superior aspect of the skin ellipse, and a\u000along suture at the lateral aspect of the skin ellipse, and a suture bow-tie\u000asurrounding tissue corresponding axillary contents (7.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000adeep margin consists of a smooth fascial plane without skeletal muscle present\u000aand has been inked black. Sectioning of the breast reveals an irregular\u000apink/white infiltrative patch of firm tissue extending from within REMOVED_DATE cm of\u000athe medial aspect of the specimen to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen (total area of irregularity covers REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Within this\u000aarea of firm tissue in the upper inner quadrant, a metal clip is identified at\u000athe border of firm tissue and adipose tissue. A REMOVED_DATE cm superior and medial to\u000athe clip is an area containing at least three firm white nodular areas (0.3 cm\u000ain greatest dimension). Approximately three additional nodules are found\u000awithin the firm tissue immediately lateral to the area of clip. The extensive\u000aarea of firm tissue is heterogeneous with pockets of adipose tissue found\u000awithin it. The firm irregular tissue comes within REMOVED_DATE cm of the deep margin.\u000aThe remainder of the breast tissue, which represents the minority of the\u000aspecimen, consists of approximately REMOVED_DATE % adipose tissue. 20 lymph node\u000acandidates are identified within the axillary portion of the specimen.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast mass in the area of the clip, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area of nodularity medial to the clip, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Areas of nodularity immediately lateral to the clip, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass to nearest deep resection margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Firm breast mass REMOVED_DATE cm medial to the clip area, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Firm breast mass REMOVED_DATE cm lateral to the clip area, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Firm breast mass REMOVED_DATE cm lateral to the clip area, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 5 lymph node candidates in adipose tissue, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 7 lymph node candidates, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 lymph node candidates, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin and nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart B, is labeled "#2. Level 1 and 2 axillary contents". The specimen\u000aconsists of multiple fragments of adipose tissue (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate). Six lymph node candidates are grossly identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 lymph node candidates, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidates, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 lymph node candidates, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Medial mastectomy skin". The specimen consists of a\u000asingle brown skin fragment (11 cm in length x REMOVED_DATE cm in width x REMOVED_DATE cm in depth\u000awith up to REMOVED_DATE cm of attached subcutaneous fat). There are no grossly\u000aidentifiable masses, lumps or lesions within the tissue and the skin is normal\u000aappearing. The deep side of the skin has been inked blue.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin tips bisected, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin, REMOVED_DATE frags each, RSS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aAEV/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday REMOVED_MONTH 22, 2009 at 10:21:01PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for metastatic carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: This is the same immunoprofile of the carcinoma prior to neoadjuvant\u000achemotherapy.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in < 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 28, 2009 at 03:17:50PM\u000a[report_end]
