VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Christopher P. Elco, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Hejin P Hahn, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. INFERIOR RIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types, intermediate nuclear\u000agrade, is present in 1 of 17 blocks.\u000aNecrosis and calcifications are present in the DCIS.\u000aMicroinvasion is absent.\u000aAll (superior, inferior, medial, lateral, anterior and posterior) margins are\u000anegative (>0.3cm) for DCIS.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with calcifications is present.\u000aCore biopsy site is present.\u000aB. SUPERIOR RIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types, intermediate nuclear\u000agrade, is present in 1 of 16 blocks.\u000aNecrosis and calcifications are present in the DCIS.\u000aMicroinvasion is absent.\u000aDCIS is focally REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin (Block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThe superior, medial, lateral, anterior and posterior margins are negative\u000a(>0.3cm) for DCIS.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with calcifications is present.\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): Tis NX MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of ADH on core biopsy at BWH (Ca++).\u000aOperation: Needle wire localization, right breast x 2.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast calcification.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Inferior wire #2, right breast - short stitch superior, long lateral.\u000aB/2. Superior wire #1, right breast - short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen labeled #2: Wire, clip, calcifications in.\u000aOR CALLED. Signed: SR\u000aSpecimen from OR, labeled #1: Contains calcifications.\u000aCalled to Dr. Shoji at 3:30 pm.\u000aSigned: JES.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Inferior wire #2 R breast", consists of an ovoid\u000ayellow/tan breast excision specimen (4.5 M-L x [TAPE SKIP] S-I x REMOVED_DATE A-P cm)\u000aoriented per surgeon with two sutures: Short equals superior, long equals\u000alateral. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in place\u000aentering the superolateral aspect of the specimen and exiting the\u000aposteroinferior aspect of the specimen. The specimen was previously\u000aradiographed, confirming the presence of a wire, a clip, and calcifications\u000awithin the specimen (at superolateral aspect of the specimen). The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from lateral to medial. The central/lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen exhibits a hemorrhagic tract (1.5 cm in length x REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter)\u000awith an associated small metal clip as consistent with prior core biopsy\u000aprocedure and radiograph. The tract is REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial\u000amargin. The needle localization wire traverses the hemorrhagic tract/clip\u000asite.\u000aThe cut surface is composed of approximately 20% delicate white/tan fibrous\u000astroma and 80% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue with no discrete masses\u000agrossly identified. The tissue corresponding to the radiographically\u000aidentified calcification is grossly unremarkable. The specimen is entirely\u000asequentially submitted from lateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central specimen from lateral to medial with calcifications in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, clip site in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and tract in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Superior wire #1 R breast", consists of an irregularly\u000ashaped yellow/tan breast excision specimen (7.5 S-I x REMOVED_DATE A-P x REMOVED_DATE M-L cm)\u000aoriented per surgeon with two sutures: Short equals superior, long equals\u000alateral. There is one needle localization wire in place, entering the\u000asuperolateral aspect of the specimen and exiting the inferoposterior aspect of\u000athe specimen. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation\u000aof margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen was previously radiographed,\u000aconfirming the presence of calcifications within the specimen (localized in the\u000amore inferior aspect of the specimen, around the tip of the wire). The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior. The cut surface is\u000acomposed of approximately 45% dense/lumpy white/pink fibrous stroma\u000apredominantly in the central/inferior aspect of the specimen, corresponding to\u000athe radiographically identified calcification/wire tip, and approximately 55%\u000ayellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue (predominantly superior aspect of the\u000aspecimen). No discrete masses are grossly identified. The central/inferior\u000aportion of the specimen to include the majority of the fibrous stroma is\u000aentirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicularly sectioned, site of wire\u000atip/calcifications, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior/central specimen with majority of fibrous stroma\u000asequentially submitted from inferior to superior with inferior, medial,\u000alateral, anterior and posterior margins, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin perpendicularly sectioned, 2 frags in each, RSS.\u000aPlease note: Approximately 60% of the specimen is submitted.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000almb/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Hejin P Hahn M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/14/2008\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/15/2008\u000a[report_end]
