000aSpecial Studies (ER, PR, Her2) - Pending\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block 3):\u000aEstrogen receptor - POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor - POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - NEGATIVE (0)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity\u000ain >10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast CA by prev wire loc excision here 6 wks ago.\u000aOperation: Left modified radical mastectomy & lymph node bx.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast CA.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. L breast tissue with axillary tail. Suture in\u000aaxillary tail.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BRUNKER,PATRICIA A. R.,M.D.,D.PHIL.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number and "left breast tissue suture in axillary tail", and consists of a\u000a221-gram, left modified radical mastectomy specimen (15.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which\u000aincludes a suture placed by the surgeon to mark the axillary tail (5.2 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.6 cm). The specimen includes a tan to pink skin ellipse (17.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awhich contains a well-healed linear scar (0.6 cm in length) in the upper inner\u000aquadrant, REMOVED_DATE cm from the nipple. There is also a well-healed linear scar (2.6\u000acm in length) in the upper outer quadrant, REMOVED_DATE cm from the everted nipple (1.3\u000acm). The deep margin is a smooth fascial plane without gross muscle and is\u000ainked in black. In the upper outer quadrant, deep to the longer scar, there\u000ais a healing excision cavity (3.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which extends to the deep\u000amargin. Abutting the excision cavity and the deep margin is a firm, poorly\u000acircumscribed, tan to gray, grossly mucinous mass (3.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is\u000apresent in the lateral half of the breast in the lower outer quadrant. The\u000amass is present REMOVED_DATE cm from the overlying skin. The remainder of the\u000aparenchyma consists of golden yellow to tan fibrofatty tissue (approximately\u000a50% fatty tissue and 50% fibrous tissue) with no gross lesion or other\u000adefinite biopsy cavity identified. Multiple lymph nodes are found within the\u000afocally fibrous axillary tail, the largest of which measures REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aRepresentative sections of the breast and all lymph nodes are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  mass to include biopsy cavity and deep margin, 4 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 5: additional section of mass, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro 6: tumor to skin and skin with scar, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 7: upper outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 8: upper inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 9: lower outer quadrant (with mass), 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 10: lower inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro 11: nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro 12: 7 possible lymph nodes, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 13: 6 possible lymph nodes, 6 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  largest lymph node, trisected, 3 frags, ESS.\u000arlr\u000aReports to: JACOBSEN, DAVID M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
