VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Marian H Harris, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aColumnar cell change with flat atypia.\u000aPapilloma with calcifications.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aNote: No malignancy is seen after microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Calcifications in right breast. Core biopsy missed calcification. Now\u000afor wire-guided biopsy.\u000aOperation: Right breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Breast tissue.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast calcifications.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue - short suture = superior, long suture = lateral - FS.\u000aRADIOGRAPH:\u000aSpecimen contains wire, clip and calcifications. Called to OR 10:30 a.m.\u000aSigned: J.R.U.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "Right breast tissue, - short stitch superior, long stitch lateral", and\u000aconsists of REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, irregular fragment of fibrofatty tissue with\u000atwo orienting sutures - short stitch = superior, and long stitch = lateral. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is a needle-localization wire in-situ, which enters at the\u000alateral inferior aspect and is embedded within the parenchyma. There is also a\u000a0.3 cm area of exposed radio-opaque material directly adjacent to the entrance\u000aof the wire. The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph that shows a cluster\u000aof calcifications and a small metal clip somewhat displaced away from the\u000acalcifications. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral,\u000ashowing 50% of the cut surface along the superior to mid half of the specimen\u000aconsisting of white fibrous tissue, and the inferior half mostly fatty. Within\u000athe white tissue are multiple scattered cystic areas containing firm\u000aradio-opaque injected material, which obscures the metal clip. Most of the\u000aspecimen is submitted with all of the fibrous tissue submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular medial margins, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue at tip of wire, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial mid parenchyma with superior, inferior, posterior and\u000aanterior margins demonstrated, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mid lateral circled area of interest with dissected\u000acalcifications, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular lateral margins from superior to inferior,\u000arespectively, multiple frags, ESS. (This also demonstrates area where metal\u000aclip was embedded).\u000aWP/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Kenneth R Harris M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aResident Review by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/27/2006\u000a[report_end]
