VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Skin, plastic repair\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: David C. Park, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSKIN, RIGHT BREAST, EXCISION:\u000aSkin with scar; no carcinoma seen.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, INFERIOR MARGIN RE-EXCISION:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed ductal and lobular features (see note).\u000aNOTE: Carcinoma in situ with mixed ductal and lobular features (intermediate to\u000ahigh nuclear grade) is present as foci measuring REMOVED_DATE - REMOVED_DATE cm adjacent to the\u000abiopsy cavity in six of sixteen blocks. Immunohistochemistry for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aconfirms the presence of myoepithelium in CIS. Several small foci (0.05 to REMOVED_DATE \u000acm each) of carcinoma in situ morphologically consistent with lobular carcinoma\u000ain situ (low to intermediate nuclear grade, no necrosis, preservation of\u000alobular architecture), similar to the lower grade CIS seen previously, are\u000apresent <0.1 cm from the new inferior margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Although the\u000amorphology is consistent with LCIS in these foci, and estrogen receptor\u000aexpression is retained, the almost complete absence of immunoreactivity for\u000aprogesterone receptor would be unusual for classic LCIS in a patient not\u000apreviously treated with an aromatase inhibitor. The significance of this\u000afinding close to a margin is unknown. Slides also seen by Dr. Susan Lester, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of R breast cancer, pleomorphic CIS, (+)inferior margin, S/P 3\u000aattempts at breast conservation.\u000aOperation: Re-excision right partial mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast ca.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) scar tissue right breast.\u000aB/2) selective re-excision inferior margin, right partial mastectomy.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Scar tissue, right breast", consists of an unoriented REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.4 cm (depth) tan/white skin ellipse with a possible ill-defined linear\u000awell-healed along its length. Tissue is inked and sectioned.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin, sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Selective re-excision, inferior margin, right partial mastectomy",\u000aconsists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue. The new\u000amargin has been previously inked black by the surgeon (likely inferior margin).\u000aThe opposing surface is comprised of a red/tan biopsy cavity and is inked blue\u000aby the prosector for purposes of orientation only. The entire specimen is\u000asequentially submitted, with perpendicular ends in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adb/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Friday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 09, 2009 at 04:53:25PM\u000a[report_end]
