VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Consult slides\u000aProcedure Date:\u000aOrdering Provider: ERIC P WINER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Joseph Woodward Carlson, M.D., Ph.D.; Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aCONSULT SLIDES FROM ST. VINCENT HOSPITAL, WORCESTER, MA:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, TWO EXCISIONS (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aDESIGNATED "INCISIONAL BIOPSY":\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aNote: No tumor is present.\u000aNOT DESIGNATED:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade III/III) measuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in size on slide 2C, is present with\u000aassociated necrosis.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen.\u000aNo in situ component is seen (EIC negative).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present.\u000aInvasive carcinoma extends widely to unoriented tissue edges. The specimen was\u000areceived in fragments.\u000aSpecial studies were performed at an outside institution with the following\u000aresults (not reviewed):\u000aESTROGEN RECEPTOR: positive 1+\u000aPROGESTERONE RECEPTOR: positive 1+\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/NEU: positive (2+)\u000aNote: The carcinoma has a syncytial growth pattern, high nuclear grade, and a\u000aprominent lymphocytic infiltrate, consistent with "medullary" features.\u000aHowever, the presence of ER, PR, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID would be unusual for a tumor of this\u000atype. FISH studies for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu amplification may be helpful if clinically\u000awarranted.\u000aIf this is an incisional biopsy, the actual size of the carcinoma may be\u000alarger.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis:\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aConsult slides.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aReceived from St. Vincent Hospital, Pathology Department, 20 Worcester Center\u000aBoulevard, Worcester, MA REMOVED_CASE_ID, are eleven (11) glass slides.\u000aThe slides are labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "1FS", "1A", "1A RC REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID"\u000athrough "1A RC REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "1B", "1C", "2A" through "2C" and "2C RC REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", corresponding\u000ato 1) right breast incisional biopsy (2.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) 2) right breast\u000aexcision (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the\u000aaccompanying pathology report bearing the patient's name.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/4/2005\u000aSurgical Addendum: (2/26/07)\u000aSubsequently received on REMOVED_DATE  from Dianon Systems, Stratford, CT, are three\u000a(3) glass slides, labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "ER", "PR" and\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/NEU(+)", which correspond to immunohistochemistry studies performed on\u000athe case listed above, bearing the patient's name.\u000aSpecial studies were performed at an outside institution with the following\u000aresults (reviewed):\u000aESTROGEN RECEPTOR POSITIVE - LOW\u000aPROGESTERONE RECEPTOR NEGATIVE (rare cells weakly positive)\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/NEU HETEROGENEOUS (see note)\u000aNOTE: Immunohistochemistry for Her2/neu appears to be mostly negative (0 to\u000a1+), with focal areas of weak complete membrane immunoreactivity (2+ pattern).\u000aAreas of weak complete membrane immunoreactivity are seen near tissue edges in\u000aassociation with areas of high cytoplasmic background and may represent "edge\u000aeffect." Results are difficult to interpret in the absence of a positive\u000acontrol.\u000aSurgical Addendum: (2/27/07)\u000aSubsequently received on REMOVED_DATE  is a paraffin block, labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and\u000asublabeled "2C" which corresponds to the above case.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed at BWH on formalin fixed tissue with\u000athe following results for invasive carcinoma (block 2C):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE - LOW (see note)\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE (rare cells weakly positive)\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
