VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Eric J Burks, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Tan A Ince, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CLIPS AND AXILLARY LYMPH NODE\u000aDISECTION S/P NEO-ADJUVANT CHEMOTHERAPY:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, poorly differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade III/III), residual foci measure up to REMOVED_DATE cm (see note).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is observed.\u000aNo definite in situ component is present.\u000aInvasive carcinoma is very close (0.1 cm) to the posterior margin of excision\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aSkin with scar and suture material with multinucleated giant cell reaction.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aLymph nodes (0/21) negative for tumor.\u000aNo evidence of tumor regression.\u000aNote: The original tumor size was REMOVED_DATE cm. On gross examination, a tumor bed\u000awas not identified, however microscopic review of the entire specimen reveals\u000abiopsy site changes and residual carcinoma in 14 of 27 blocks. In 9 of these\u000aslides, the tumor is predominantly non-viable. The remaining 5 slides have\u000afoci of viable tumor. These findings would correlate best with a Miller Payne\u000ascore of 3.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Locally advanced rt breast ca. s/p neo-adjuvant.\u000aOperation: Not specified.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast ca.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right lumpectomy suture long lateral short suture superior to mammo.\u000aB/2. Right axillary lymph node dissection with suture on apex.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aSpecimen image contains two wires and three biopsy clips. Radiology - Tammy S.\u000aM.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right lumpectomy suture long lateral short suture superior\u000ato mammo", consists of one fragment of fibroadipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior to posterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000amedial to lateral. There are two wires entering the lateral margin with one\u000awire exiting the inferior, medial, and posterior margins. There is a long\u000asuture designated as lateral and a short suture designated as superior by the\u000asurgeon. The specimen is inked for the evaluation of margins: posterior =\u000ablack, anterior = blue, superior = red, inferior = orange, lateral = yellow,\u000amedial = green. Representative sections of margins are submitted for\u000amicroscopic examination. There is a skin ellipse at the superior margin\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The specimen is serially from superior to inferior to\u000areveal 10% fibrous tissue and 90% adipose tissue with no clip, mass, or lesion\u000agrossly identified. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin with skin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior and posterior margin, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior, anterior, and posterior margin bisected, 2 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior, anterior, posterior, and medial margins, bisected, 2\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margin, bisected, 2 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margin, bisected, 2 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margins, bisected, 2\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margins, bisected, 2\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral margins, bisected, 2\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral and inferior margins,\u000abisected, location of both wires tips, 2 frags.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior, lateral, and inferior margin, bisected, 2 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior, lateral, and inferior margins, bisected, 2 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral, posterior, and inferior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Right axillary lymph node dissection with suture on apex",\u000aconsists of one fragment of fibroadipose tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000awith a suture designated as apex by the surgeon. Twenty-one lymph node\u000acandidates are identified ranging from REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 lymph nodes, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 16 lymph node candidates, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aECH/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Eric C Huang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Tan A Ince M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a12/14/2005\u000aSurgical Addendum: (2/13/07)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only\u000aa portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/13/2007\u000a[report_end]
