VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DAVID I SOYBEL M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Revised Report **********\u000aResident: David C. Park, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA/1). LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aColumnar cell change with atypia.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aNo invasive carcinoma is present upon examination of the entire\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aOther findings:\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aCalcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): Tis NX MX.\u000aB/2). RIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP, AND AXILLARY NODE DISSECTION (including\u000aParts B, C, and D):\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aNEGATIVE LYMPH NODES\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aMultifocal - No\u000aHistologic type - Ductal, see note.\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - II (Moderate) (6 to 7 points)\u000aTubules - 10 to 75% (2 points)\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate (2 points)\u000aMitotic counts - 1 point\u000aEIC - Positive\u000aLVI - Present, multiple blocks.\u000aDermal LVI - Absent\u000aSkin invasion - Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Not applicable\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - All surgical margins\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - Solid and cribriform, often involving a\u000apapilloma.\u000aNuclear grade - Intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Present\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Present\u000aMicroinvasion (<0.1cm) - Absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total - REMOVED_DATE blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - Posterior, superior, medial, lateral,\u000ainferior\u000aVery close(<0.1 cm from margin)- Anterior (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aLYMPH NODES (PARTS C/3 AND D/4)\u000aAxillary lymph nodes - Negative\u000aNumber of positive nodes - 0\u000aNumber of nodes examined - 7\u000aTOTAL POSITIVE NODES (all specimens)- REMOVED_DATE \u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aSkin with mild perivascular acute inflammation, non-specific.\u000aPapilloma, multifocal.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition) - pREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aNote: The ductal carcinoma in situ multifocally involves a papilloma. The\u000ainvasive component is composed of mainly of cords with occasional tubules and\u000acribriform structures. Immunohistochemical double staining for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID was\u000aperformed in evaluation of this case. Dr. S. Lester of the Breast Pathology\u000aService reviewed selected slides of part B.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 56 yo with abnormal mammogram.\u000aOperation: Bilat wire loc.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Lt breast tissue.\u000aB/2. Rt breast tissue.\u000aC/3. Right lower axilla.\u000aD/4. Right upper axilla.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000a1. Specimen image demonstrates 2 intact hookwires + microcalcs + biopsy clip.\u000aDiscussed with Dr. Soybel at 3 pm. Andy Tsai.\u000a2. Specimen radiograph demonstrates 2 intact hookwires, biopsy clip, and\u000acalcifications. Discussed with Dr. Soybel by Dr. Andy Tsai at 3:15 pm.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in four parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "left breast short superior, long lateral", consists of a left breast\u000awire localized excision (6.2 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior,\u000a2.4 cm anterior to posterior) with a short stitch designating superior and a\u000along stitch designating lateral. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol\u000afor the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. On the lateral surface, there\u000ais an unremarkable skin ellipse (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm). An accompanying radiograph\u000ademonstrates two wires entering from the lateral margin and terminating within\u000athe specimen without exiting. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial\u000ato lateral to reveal a hemorrhagic area consistent with core biopsy site (3.4 x\u000a1.9 x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is located REMOVED_DATE cm to the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000alateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the posterior margin,\u000a1.5 cm to the inferior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm to the superior margin.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, perpendicular, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Specimen serially sectioned from medial to lateral (micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000amicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID correspond to area of clip), multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin to include skin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "right breast short superior, long lateral", consists of a right breast\u000awire localized excision (11.2 cm lateral to medial, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to\u000ainferior, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior) with a short suture designating\u000asuperior and a long suture designating lateral with an unremarkable skin\u000aellipse on the lateral surface (3.8 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is inked per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. An\u000aaccompanying radiograph demonstrates one wire entering the anterolateral aspect\u000aof the specimen without exiting and the other wire entering the superolateral\u000aaspect of the specimen without exiting. Both wires penetrate the skin ellipse.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from inferior to superior to reveal an\u000aill-defined stellate area (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the lateral margin. There is a prior biopsy site (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000aat the superior aspect of the previously described stellate area and is located\u000a0.8 cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe lateral margin and skin surface, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe anterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The entire\u000aill-defined stellate area and prior biopsy site is submitted for histological\u000aexamination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections of unremarkable fibrous tissue in between\u000athe inferior margin and ill-defined firm area, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Ill-defined area, 1 frag each, ESS (micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID correspond to\u000aarea of clip).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin to include all fibrous tissue associated with\u000amargin, perpendicular, 5 frags total, RSS.\u000aPart C, "right lower axilla", consists of yellow/tan lobulated soft tissue (7.0\u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is examined for lymph node and five lymph nodes\u000aare identified ranging in size from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 lymph nodes, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative section of examined adipose tissue, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart D, "right upper axilla", consists of multiple fragments of yellow/pink\u000alobulated soft tissue (4.6 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Two lymph nodes are identified\u000aranging in size from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node candidate each, bisected, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-08-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aDP/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aThis report was amended to correct a typographical error and include margin\u000astatus in the main report of Part A/1.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (3/4/08)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) POSITIVE (3+)\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #2 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAmendment #1 by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/7/2008\u000a[report_end]
