e,\u000aunit number, and "#1. ADH site right breast, long equals lateral, short equals\u000asuperior", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior) x REMOVED_DATE (medial to\u000alateral) x REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior) cm lumpectomy specimen. The specimen was\u000aoriented per the surgeon as long stitch equals lateral and short stitch equals\u000asuperior. The specimen was also inked by the surgeon per standard protocol as\u000ared equals superior, green equals inferior, yellow equals lateral, orange\u000aequals medial, blue equals anterior, and black equals posterior. There is a\u000alocalization wire that enters the lateral/posterior side and exits the inferior\u000aside. On serial sectioning, there is a biopsy cavity that is abutting the\u000alateral/posterior borders and a slightly firm/rubbery area adjacent to the\u000abiopsy cavity. The fibrous area is REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the inferior margin, at the posterior\u000amargin, at the lateral margin, and at the medial margin. No other masses or\u000alesions were noted and the remainder of the specimen consisted of 55% adipose\u000atissue, and 35% fibrous tissue. The entire specimen was submitted for\u000ahistological analysis to include the biopsy cavity in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aAccompanying radiograph (viewed by centricity) demonstrates intact wire,\u000acalcification, and a clip in the specimen. The portion of the specimen that\u000acontains the clip is in block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicular, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serially sectioning of the specimen, with biopsy cavity in blocks\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and associated fibrous tissue, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular, 4 frags each, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000atac/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Wednesday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 02, 2009 at 03:57:29PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in < 30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 03, 2009 at 02:40:47PM\u000a[report_end]
