VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OUTSIDE SLIDE CONSULT - BREAST\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aCONSULT SLIDES AND PARAFFIN BLOCKS FROM ST. MARY'S REGIONAL MEDICAL CENTER,\u000aLEWISTON, ME:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES OF A MASS (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aHIGH GRADE MALIGNANT UNDIFFERENTIATED SMALL BLUE CELL NEOPLASM\u000a(see Comment below).\u000aRIGHT AXILLARY LYMPH NODE EXCISION AND INCISIONAL BREAST BIOPSY (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID;\u000a5/19/04):\u000aRIGHT BREAST, INCISIONAL BIOPSY:\u000aHIGH GRADE MALIGNANT SMALL BLUE CELL NEOPLASM WITH NEURAL DIFFERENTIATION\u000a(see Comment below).\u000aA minute area of bone formation is present within the tumor.\u000aThe tumor infiltrates in normal breast parenchyma.\u000aNote: This was a small biopsy measuring REMOVED_DATE x 1 x REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aRIGHT AXILLARY LYMPH NODE:\u000aMETASTATIC HIGH GRADE MALIGNANT SMALL BLUE CELL NEOPLASM WITH NEURAL\u000aDIFFERENTIATION, is present within a lymph node. The node measured 5 cm\u000aand appears to be almost completely replaced by the tumor.\u000aThe tumor has two components. One component consists of small blue cells\u000a(round or with tapering ends) with hyperchromatic nuclei and a high\u000amitotic rate. These cells have an undifferentiated or "blastema"\u000aappearance. This cell population merges into the second component that\u000aconsists predominantly of neuropil with occasional larger cells with the\u000aappearance of ganglion cells, consistent with neural differentiaion.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed at BWH and at the original\u000ainstitution with the following results:\u000aSmall blue cells Differentiated\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID FOCAL, WEAK FOCAL\u000aSynaptophysin POSITIVE, WEAK POSITIVE\u000aNeuron specific enolase FOCAL POSITIVE, STRONG\u000aChromogranin NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aGlial fibrillary acidic protein NEGATIVE POSITIVE, STRONG\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aNeurofilament protein NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aCytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID POSITIVE, FOCAL NEGATIVE\u000aCytokeratin PANK POSITIVE NEGATIVE\u000aCytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.2 POSITIVE NEGATIVE\u000aVimentin POSITIVE POSITIVE\u000aAlpha-feto protein NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aHEP-PAR NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aCarcinoembryonic antigen - p NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aDesmin NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aMyf-4 NEGATIVE NEGATIVE\u000aNote: The small blue cells do not have the immunophenotype specific for\u000aeither neuroblastoma or hepatoblastoma. The immunoperoxidase studies\u000asupport neural differentiation in the second component of the tumor.\u000aThe overall impression is that of a neoplasm consisting of\u000aundifferentiated cells with the capacity to differentiate into neural\u000atissue. The only other possible differentiated component is a minute area\u000aof bone.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Christopher Fletcher and Dr. Antonio\u000aNascimento (Mayo Clinic) and both favored a high-grade malignant\u000aneuroblastic neoplasm, consistent with differentiating neuroblastoma.\u000aThis is a highly unusual neoplasm that has not been described previously\u000ain the breast or at other sites. The possibility of metastasis from\u000aanother site has been considered clinically. However, the only identified\u000asites of disease are the breast, axillary lymph nodes, and internal\u000amammary nodes.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: The patient is a 34 year old woman who gave birth to her third child\u000ain REMOVED_MONTH 2003. In REMOVED_MONTH while breast feeding, she developed breast\u000afullness and hardness which became painful. She underwent FNA and then core\u000aneedle biopsy. A CT scan showed the large mass completely replacing the\u000abreast and an 8 to 9 cm hemangioma of the right lobe of the liver, but no other\u000amasses or lymphadenopathy. PET showed the large breast mass and internal\u000amammary lymph nodes, but no other sites of involvement. The liver mass was not\u000apositive, consistent with a hemangioma. She subsequently underwent excision of\u000aa palpable axillary lymph node and an incisional biopsy of the breast.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: High grade neuroblastic neoplasm.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Consult slides and paraffin blocks.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by LOMO,LESLEY C.,M.D.\u000aReceived from St. Mary's Regional Medical Center, Pathology Department, Campus\u000aAvenue, P.O. Box 291, Lewiston, ME REMOVED_DATE  are fifteen (15) slides and six\u000aparaffin blocks.\u000aTwo (2) slides are labeled "S-04-2341" and sublabeled "1 (1)" and "2 (3)",\u000acorresponding to a right breast core needle biopsy performed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aaccording to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient's name.\u000aThirteen (13) slides and six (6) paraffin blocks are labeled "S-04-2534"; the\u000aslides are sublabeled "1 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (2)", "2 REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (2)" and "1 A (2)", "1 B (2)", "1B\u000aKERATIN", "1B MIC-2", "1B SYNAPTO", "1B VIM", "1B GFAP", "1 C (2) through "1 F\u000a(2)"; and the paraffin blocks are sublabeled "1A" through "1F", corresponding\u000ato a right axillary lymph node excision (5 x 4 x 2 cm) and right breast\u000aincisional biopsy performed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology\u000areport bearing the patient's name.\u000aIncluded is a consultation report from Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN; numbered\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and dated REMOVED_DATE  corresponding to the case listed above\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" bearing the patient's name. By report, molecular studies for\u000asynovial sarcoma associated gene rearrangements were negative.\u000aAlso included is a consultation report from Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN;\u000anumbered "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and dated REMOVED_DATE  referrring institution St. Mary's\u000aRegional Medical Center, Lewiston, ME; their accession number "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID"\u000abearing the patient's name.\u000aIncluded also are two (2) consultation letters; one (1) letter dated REMOVED_DATE \u000afrom Dr. Daniel W. Visscher at Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN, to Dr. Alan R.\u000aSolander at St. Mary's Regional Medical Center; referring to the case listed\u000aabove "S-04-2341"; the second letter is dated REMOVED_DATE  from Dr. Antonio G.\u000aNascimento at Mayo Clinic Rochester, MN, to Dr. Richard Malone at St. Mary's\u000aRegional Hospital, Lewinston, ME, referring to the case listed above\u000a"S-04-2534", bearing the patient's name.\u000aAlso included is an oncology consultation report from The Regional Center for\u000aCancer and Blood Disorders, affiliated with St. Mary's Regional Medical Center;\u000adated REMOVED_DATE  and signed by Dr. Amir H. Modarressi, bearing the patient's name.\u000aReports to: GOLSHAN, MEHRA M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: OUTSIDE SLIDE CONSULT - BREAST\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: OUTSIDE SLIDE CONSULT - BREAST\u000a[report_end]
