VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: J. GREGORY KANE M.D.\u000aCASE: BG-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Elizabeth M. Manion, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AT A PRIOR EXCISIONAL\u000aSITE:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, micropapillary type (low nuclear grade), with\u000acalcifications and without necrosis, is present in an area of previous\u000abiopsy site changes, covering an estimated area of REMOVED_DATE cm (2 of 5\u000ablocks)(see note).\u000aColumnar cell change is associated with DCIS.\u000aDCIS is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, anterior, and inferior margins.\u000aThe medial, lateral and superficial margins are negative for DCIS\u000a(> REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aNOTE: The patient underwent a prior core needle biopsy and excision for\u000acalcifications in 2006 that showed atypical ductal hyperplasia associated\u000awith columnar cell change (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The DCIS in this\u000aspecimen is also associated with columnar cell change.\u000aImmunohistochemical analysis for estrogen receptor, progesterone\u000areceptor, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu will be performed and reported in an addendum.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition): Tis NX.\u000aLEFT BREAST, INFERIOR MARGIN, EXCISION:\u000aNormal breast tissue.\u000aNote: No malignancy is present.\u000aLEFT BREAST, SUPERIOR MARGIN, EXCISION:\u000aNormal breast tissue.\u000aNote: No malignancy is present.\u000aENDOCERVIX, "POLYP", EXCISION:\u000aLeiomyoma, submucosal (3.2 cm).\u000aNote: This specimen was reviewed by Dr. Christopher Crum.\u000aMYOMETRIUM, "SUBMUCOSAL FIBROID", EXCISION:\u000aFragment of a leiomyoma, submucosal.\u000aNote: This specimen was reviewed by Dr. Christopher Crum.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcifications of left breast with past history of atypical\u000aductal hyperplasia. Menorrhagia with endometrial cells on smear. LMP:\u000a6/14/09.\u000aOperation: Left breast resection, wire localization, hysteroscopy, D&C.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcifications left breast, fibroid uterus.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Calcifications contained within the specimen. Dr. Goldkamp.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1/A. Left breast tissue, short suture superior, long suture lateral (to mammo)\u000a2/B. Tissue inferior to specimen #1\u000a3/C. Tissue superior to specimen #1\u000a4/D. Endocervical polyp\u000a5/E. Submucosal fibroid\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received, in five parts, each labeled with the patient's name\u000aand unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left breast tissue, short suture superior, long suture\u000alateral", consists of one fragment of fibroadipose tissue (2.2 cm from anterior\u000ato posterior margins, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000asuperior to inferior margins). There is accompanying mammograph showing the\u000awire as entering the lateral margin and ends close to superior posterior\u000amargin. There is a short suture designated as superior and a long suture\u000adesignated as lateral, per surgeon. The specimen radiograph shows multiple\u000aclusters of calcifications. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for\u000athe evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The area of calcifications\u000aaccording to the mammograph is approximately at the anterior superior margin\u000aand posterior superior margins. The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a\u000ahemorrhagic area measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm and abuts the anterior, superior\u000aand inferior margins and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. No other masses\u000aor nodules are identified. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior, anterior, inferior, posterior margins, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior, anterior, inferior, posterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area of hemorrhage and calcification, anterior, inferior, posterior,\u000asuperior margins, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, received fresh, labeled "#2. Tissue inferior to specimen #1", consists\u000aof one unoriented fragment of fibroadipose tissue (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen is entirely inked black, serially sectioned and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue inferior to the specimen #1, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, received fresh, labeled "#3. Tissue superior to specimen #1", consists\u000aof one unoriented fragment of fibroadipose tissue (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen is entirely inked black, serially sectioned and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue superior to the specimen #1, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aPart D, received in formalin, labeled and "#4. Endocervical polyp", consists of\u000aone fragment of pink/tan firm tissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Endocervical polyp, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aPart E, received in formalin, labeled "#5. Submucosal fibroid", consists of\u000amultiple fragments of pink/tan soft tissue (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aThe specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Submucosal fibroid, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aVP/am\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 20, 2009 at 06:25:00PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in < 30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 23, 2009 at 04:36:25PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDNEDUM #2:\u000aThe purpose of this addendum is to correct a designated surgical margin for\u000aDCIS in the left breast wire localized excision specimen. DCIS was reported to\u000abe > REMOVED_DATE cm from the "superficial" margin. However, this margin is actually\u000athe "superior" margin. The additional superior margin specimen was also free\u000aof carcinoma, as originally reported.\u000aAddendum #2 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Tuesday\u000aAugust 25, 2009 at 12:53:09PM\u000a[report_end]
