VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel node\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Michael A. Seidman, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT SENTINEL NODES (including FSA):\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor (3 H&E levels examined).\u000aB. AXILLARY TISSUE:\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor (3 H&E levels examined).\u000aC. RIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED RE-EXCISION TO INCLUDE ADDITIONAL\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aRESIDUAL DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types, low nuclear\u000agrade, without necrosis or calcifications, is present as scattered, small foci\u000ain four of eleven blocks (4:11).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion.\u000aNo invasive carcinoma.\u000aCalcifications are associated with apocrine cysts and normal epithelium (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aand with micropapillary hyperplasia (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aOther findings:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aExcision site changes are present extending to anterior margin.\u000aSecretory change.\u000aDCIS is present as a REMOVED_DATE cm focus with clear cell features located REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe anterior margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), as a <0.1 cm focus, located REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial\u000amargin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). A duct-lobular unit which is partially involved by clear cell DCIS\u000a(< REMOVED_DATE cm focus) is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), and a focus\u000aof DCIS (0.2 cm in size) is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aPosterior lateral and superior margins are negative for DCIS (> REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aIn combination with the prior specimen, this tumor could be classified as AJCC\u000aClassification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID(sn)\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Ca of RT breast\u000aOperation: SNB, RT ax, re-excis CA site, RT breast\u000aOperative Findings: See op note\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Ca of RT breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right sentinel node FS\u000aB/2. Axillary tissue\u000aC/3. Re-excision RT breast\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1. RIGHT SENTINEL NODE" (FSA):\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: Lucian Chirieac, M.D.\u000aResident: Astrid Weins, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aRADIOLOGY (abstracted from SPECIMEN RADIOGRAPH REPORT REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aSpecimen images contained the localization wire and targeted\u000amicrocalcifications.\u000aRadiologists: Shadpour Demehri, Sughra Raza\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each part labeled with the\u000apatient's name and medical record number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right sentinel node", consists of a single fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing one blue lymph node (0.8 cm\u000ain greatest dimension). The lymph node is bisected and entirely submitted as\u000afrozen section A.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: FSA remnant (sentinel node, bisected), 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Non-frozen fibroadipose tissue, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Axillary tissue", consists of fibroadipose tissue\u000a(aggregated REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), in which no definitive lymph nodes are grossly\u000aidentified. Specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Axillary fibroadipose tissue, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Right breast cancer re-excision", consists of a wire\u000alocalized breast excision (4.2 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to\u000alateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior), received previously inked by the\u000asurgeon as per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. A short suture corresponds to the superior surface as inked by\u000asurgeon. A long suture corresponds to lateral surface as inked by surgeon. A\u000alocalization wire enters the superior aspect and exits the inferior aspect of\u000athe specimen. Specimen radiograph is reviewed on Centricity, and indicates\u000acalcifications are located along the lateral edge of specimen. Grossly, a\u000acystic lesion (0.4 cm diameter) filled with thick viscous material is\u000aidentified REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin.\u000aA poorly-defined, hemorrhagic, fibrous lesion (0.3 cm diameter) is identified\u000a0.2 cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe superior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin. Specimen is\u000aapproximately 20% fibrous and 80% fatty. Specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior and posterior margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin to include first lesion, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining lateral margin to include anterior/posterior/superior\u000amargins, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin to include anterior/posterior/superior margins, 2\u000afrags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin to include anterior/posterior margins, 2 frags\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aSEI/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday REMOVED_MONTH 10, 2009 at 03:07:21PM\u000a[report_end]
