VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy with regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: David Wu, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Michelle S Hirsch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY LYMPH NODE DISSECTION (parts REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aMALIGNANT PHYLLODES TUMOR WITH INVASIVE CARCINOMATOUS AND SARCOMATOUS\u000aCOMPONENTS (SEE NOTE).\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMATOUS COMPONENT\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aMultifocal - No\u000aHistologic Type - Ductal\u000aHistologic Grade (SBR) - III(Poor)\u000aNuclear Grade - High\u000aEIC - Negative\u000aLymphovascular invasion - Absent\u000aDermal lymphatic invasion - Absent\u000aSkin invasion - Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Absent\u000aFinal Margins (invasive carcinoma)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - Deep\u000aSARCOMATOUS COMPONENT:\u000aMorphology - High grade\u000aMitoses - Number up to REMOVED_DATE hpfs,\u000aatypical mitotic figures present\u000aNecrosis - Present\u000aFinal Margins (sarcoma)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - Deep\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aLYMPH NODES\u000aAxillary lymph nodes: - Negative\u000aNumber of positive nodes - 0\u000aNumber of nodes examined - 13\u000aTOTAL POSITIVE LYMPH NODES/TOTAL LYMPH NODES (all specimens): REMOVED_DATE \u000aOTHER FINDINGS - Skeletal muscle with no tumor present\u000a- Calcifications in benign epithelium\u000aNOTE: The tumor is composed of both epithelial and spindle cell components.\u000aMultifocally, the sarcomatous component is associated with benign breast\u000aepithelium where it demonstrates periglandular cuffing and sarcomatous\u000aovergrowth, consistent with a Phyllodes Tumor. However, in block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, an area\u000aof invasive carcinoma is also identified. The overall findings are consistent\u000awith a MALIGNANT PHYLLODES TUMOR WITH INVASIVE CARCINOMATOUS AND SARCOMATOUS\u000aCOMPONENTS.\u000aImmunohistochemistry performed at BWH demonstrates the following staining\u000aprofile in tumor cells:\u000aPositive - REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (epithelial component only), Pan-K (epithelial component\u000aonly), Desmin (sarcomatous component only), p63 (rare cells)\u000aNegative - REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, SMA, ER, PR, Her2-neu\u000aThis case has been reviewed by Drs. A. Richardson, T. Ince and S. Schnitt\u000a(Breast Pathology Service), and Dr. C. Fletcher (Soft Tissue Pathology\u000aService), who concur.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Left breast\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast spindle cell neoplasm.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a(1) Left breast tissue with enlarged node + axillary tail, long stitch marks\u000alateral, enlarged node + axillary tail, short stitch at superior skin level @\u000a12:00.\u000a(2) Additional enlarged axillary node (left).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A specimen, labeled "Left breast tissue with enlarged node and axillary\u000atail", consists of a 1,504-gm, left, modified radical mastectomy specimen,\u000aincluding a skin ellipse and an axillary tail (30 cm medial/lateral x REMOVED_DATE \u000asuperior/inferior x REMOVED_DATE cm anterior/posterior). The specimen is received with\u000aa long stitch designating the lateral portion of the axillary tail including an\u000aenlarged lymph node, and a short stitch designating the superior portion of the\u000askin at 12:00. The modified skin ellipse is present on the anterior surface\u000a(21 x 8 cm in greatest dimension) with a centrally-located, tan/beige areola (3\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm) and a centrally-located, pink/beige nipple (1.3 cm). The\u000aposterior/deep aspect of the specimen is composed of a smooth fascial layer\u000awith no skeletal muscle grossly identified. Serial sectioning demonstrates,\u000awithin the outer quadrants, a single, well-circumscribed, fleshy, white to\u000ayellow, focally nodular mass (7.5 superior/inferior x 6 anterior/posterior x\u000a8.5 cm medial/lateral) with areas of focal hemorrhage and myxoid change.\u000aTowards the medial aspect, the mass becomes more irregular and less\u000awell-circumscribed. The closest deep margin is located within REMOVED_DATE - REMOVED_DATE cm but\u000ais distinctly separated by fascial plane and fibroadipose tissue. Directly\u000aretroareolar/retro-nipple, there is a small, irregularly circumscribed area of\u000afibrous tissue (6 cm superior/inferior x REMOVED_DATE cm anterior/posterior x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000amedial/lateral). This fibrous tissue appears to be distinct from the\u000aaforementioned mass. Within the lower inner quadrant, there is a firm area of\u000afibrous tissue (2.5 cm x REMOVED_DATE cm x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is not suspicious for tumor.\u000aThe specimen is composed of approximately 80% of fibroadipose tissue, and the\u000aremaining 20% represents either fibrous tissue or areas suspicious for tumor.\u000aRepresentative sections of the mass are submitted for cytogenetics and electron\u000amicroscopy. Lymph nodes are carefully dissected and submitted entirely after\u000aBowen's fixation. Photographs of the specimen are taken.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Closest deep margins, multiple frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tumor mass, multiple frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Adjacent area of fibrous tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant area of fibrous tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper/outer quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower/outer quadrant, possible lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper/inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower/inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple base, en face, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple, perpendicular, 75% inversion, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Dermal irregularities in the outer quadrant, 8 ? and 11 cm\u000afrom nipple, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Stitched node, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 possible nodes, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 possible nodes, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible node, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible node, ESS.\u000aPart B specimen, labeled "Additional enlarged axillary node left", consists of\u000aa portion of fibroadipose tissue (2.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Present also is an\u000aimbedded lymph node (1.7 cm in greatest dimension).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Bisected node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000adwu/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by David Wu M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/27/2005\u000a[report_end]
