VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: J. GREGORY KANE M.D.\u000aCASE: BG-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Dick Guang-Iong Hwang, M.D., Ph.D.; Eleanor Y. Chen, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000a1. RIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aComplex sclerosing lesion (0.7 cm) with florid epithelial hyperplasia, apocrine\u000ametaplasia and focal cytologic atypia.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia, apocrine cysts and fibroadenoma.\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy including evaluation of margins.\u000a2. "TISSUE SUPERIOR TO SPECIMEN #1", EXCISION:\u000aApocrine cysts, columnar cell change and focal fibroadenomatoid changes.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy including evaluation of margins.\u000a3. MASS, RIGHT AXILLA, EXCISION:\u000aMETASTATIC POORLY DIFFERENTIATED CARCINOMA present in eight (8) of twelve\u000a(12) lymph nodes. The largest metastatic focus measures REMOVED_DATE cm and shows\u000aextensive associated tumor necrosis. Extranodal extension is present (0.2 cm).\u000aOne of twelve lymph nodes shows isolated tumor cells. Intravascular carcinoma\u000ais present within axillary tissue.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left modified radical mastectomy in May 2007 for recurrent left breast\u000acancer. 1st Breast cancer in 1970's treated with lumpectomy and radiation.\u000aHad 2 foci of infiltrating cancer REMOVED_DATE & REMOVED_DATE cm grade III. In February 2008;\u000ahad right axillary node (4 cm) with metastatic ca. Now has recurrent mass in\u000aright axilla. Also has slightly suspicious area in right breast on MRI core\u000ashowing diagnosis of complex sclerosing lesion with atypia.\u000aOperation: Right breast lumpectomy with wire localization.\u000a2. Breast excision large axillary mass on right.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Right breast tissue short superior, long lateral\u000a2. Tissue superior to specimen #1\u000a3. Right axilla mass\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Right breast tissue", and consists of a wire localized\u000abreast excision specimen (10.0 cm medial to lateral x 4 cm superior to inferior\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior). The specimen is oriented by the surgeon with\u000aa long suture lateral and a short suture superior. A localization wire is\u000areceived inside the container but not attached to the specimen. Accompanying\u000aradiograph shows a clip embedded within the specimen near the central part of\u000athe specimen.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. Two clips are identified centrally associated with a\u000ahemorrhagic core biopsy site (0.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The biopsy site is\u000aassociated with focal fat necrosis with no gross masses identified. The\u000aremainder of the specimen consists of 5% white/tan fibrous tissue and 95%\u000ayellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen is serially sectioned and\u000aentirely submitted from medial to lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections from medial to lateral (clips in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, two\u000acomplete slices each in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 complete slice in remaining\u000ablocks), 1 frag each in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 2 frags each in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 2 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Tissue superior to specimen 1", consists of a breast\u000aexcision specimen (4.3 cm from medial to lateral x REMOVED_DATE cm from superior to\u000ainferior x REMOVED_DATE cm from posterior to anterior). The specimen is oriented by the\u000asurgeon with a long suture lateral and a short suture superior.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is composed of yellow lobulated adipose tissue.\u000aNo gross lesions are identified. The specimen is entirely submitted for\u000amicroscopic examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections from lateral to medial, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, 3 frags each, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Right axillary mass", consists of multiple fragments of\u000awhite/tan fibroadipose tissue (6.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). Thirteen\u000awhite/tan firm lymph node candidates are identified (up to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aRepresentative sections of the largest lymph node candidate are submitted for\u000amicroscopic examination and the entirety of the remainder of the specimen is\u000asubmitted for microscopic examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative largest lymph node candidate, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 lymph node trisected, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 lymph nodes bisected, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 lymph node candidates, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 5 lymph node candidates, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining fibroadipose tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Tuesday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 09, 2008 at 06:54:15PM\u000a[report_end]
