VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Tanya Rege, M.D., Ph.D.; Wai Chin Foo, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Hejin P Hahn, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aINTRACYSTIC PAPILLARY CARCINOMA (1.8 cm as measured on glass slide), with\u000aapocrine features, well-differentiated (see note)\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform, micropapillary and papillary types with\u000aapocrine features (intermediate and high nuclear grade) (22 of 22 total\u000aslides), associated with MICROINVASION (<0.1 cm, four foci; slides REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). DCIS is associated with calcifications and necrosis. DCIS is present\u000aassociated with and extends away from intracystic papillary carcinoma, (EIC\u000apositive).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is absent\u000aA fragment of tumor is present in a lymphovascular space (see note).\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aIntracystic papillary carcinoma is present close (0.2 cm) to the posterior\u000amargin (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). All other margins are negative for intracystic papillary\u000acarcinoma.\u000aDCIS is present very close (< REMOVED_DATE cm) to the lateral and superior margin. DCIS\u000ais present at the posterior, medial, anterior and inferior margins.\u000aMicroinvasive carcinoma is present very close (<0.1 cm ) to the posterior\u000amargin (slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aImmunohistochemistry (p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) confirms the absence of myoepithelial cells\u000ain the intracystic papillary carcinoma and microinvasive foci.\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDc NX MX.\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aNOTE: Intracystic papillary carcinomas lack myoepithelial cells along the\u000afibrovascular septae and also at the periphery of the cyst lining. Although\u000aloss of myoepithelial cells is usually associated with stromal invasion, the\u000amajority of intracystic papillary carcinomas have a clinical course similar to\u000acases of DCIS.\u000aA fragment of tumor is present in a lymphovascular space (slide 17). The tumor\u000ais papillary and friable. Artefactual disruption and displacement of tumor\u000acannot be excluded.\u000aDr. Michael Bennett reviewed this case and concurs. In addition selected slides\u000awere reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester who also concurs with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 68 year old female with left breast hypoechoic lesion.\u000aOperation: L breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue (short suture superior, long suture lateral).\u000aRADIOLOGY: Ultrasound images showed the lesion indurated in the specimen.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral wire\u000aout", and consists of a portion of yellow/red fibroadipose tissue (5.3 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a2.9 cm), with two orienting sutures (short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral). The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph with a density marked by\u000athe radiologist. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from medial to lateral, exhibiting a multiloculated, hemorrhagic\u000acystic lesion (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with loosely adherent pink/tan papillary\u000aexcrescences. The cystic lesions grossly abuts the anterior margin, comes\u000awithin REMOVED_DATE cm of the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of\u000athe superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral\u000amargin. The lesion grossly corresponds to the aforementioned density within\u000athe accompanying radiograph. The remaining breast tissue is composed of\u000aapproximately 60% white/tan, diffusely cystic, focally firm fibrous seroma and\u000a40% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. No other gross lesions are\u000aidentified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast tissue sequentially from medial to lateral, multi frags,\u000aESS. Please note, cystic lesion in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adl/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Hejin P Hahn M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/28/2007\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (8/30/07)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for microinvasive carcinoma and DCIS:\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) POSITIVE (3+)\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by James L Connolly M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
