VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Priscilla S Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, "LESION CLOSER TO NIPPLE", WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A DENSITY:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aFibroadenomatoid changes.\u000aNote: A specific mass-forming lesion is not seen. The entire specimen is\u000aexamined microscopically, including margins.\u000aLEFT BREAST, 12:00, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP MARKING A PRIOR CORE\u000aNEEDLE BIOPSY SITE:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, well differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson grade\u000aI/III), REMOVED_DATE cm, is present immediately adjacent to the prior core needle biopsy\u000asite (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). An immunohistochemical study for p63 and cytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aconfirms the absence of myoepithelial cells associated with the carcinoma.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen.\u000aNo carcinoma in situ is seen (EIC negative).\u000aInvasive carcinoma is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin. The remaining margins\u000aare widely free of carcinoma.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies are performed with the following results for invasive\u000acarcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: This carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition) REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa NX MX.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal (L) MRI: #1 density, #2 mass which showed ADH on core needle\u000abiopsy\u000aOperation: Left needle localized breast biopsy x 2.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast lesion closer to nipple, short stitch superior, long lateral\u000aB/2. 12 o'clock lesion left breast (near clip), short stitch superior, long\u000astitch lateral\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: #1: Wire in specimen\u000a#2: Wire and clip in specimen\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left breast lesion closer to nipple, short stitch\u000asuperior, long stitch lateral", consists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to\u000apink/tan fibrofatty tissue (6.0 gm, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is oriented per\u000asurgeon as follows: short suture = superior, long suture = lateral. The\u000aspecimen is inked per the prospector per pathology protocol for the evaluation\u000aof margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle localization wire entering\u000athe medial aspect of the specimen and exiting the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen was previously radiographed and the radiograph shows a\u000adiffusely dense biopsy without a discrete mass lesion.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial into a total of seven\u000aslices (slice 1 = most lateral, slice = most medial). Throughout the specimen\u000a(slices REMOVED_DATE ) there is approximately 90% diffusely dense white/tan to white/pink\u000afibrous tissue, which abuts all inked margins. The wire traverses the dense\u000afibrous tissue throughout the specimen. The remainder of the specimen is\u000acomposed of approximately 10% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. No discrete\u000amasses or lesions are identified. The specimen is entirely submitted from\u000alateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 1 (most lateral), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 5, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 6, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 7, (most medial), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. 12 o'clock lesion left breast (near clip)", consists of an\u000airregular fragment of yellow/tan fibrofatty tissue (23.0 gm, REMOVED_DATE cm (M-L) x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm (A-P) x REMOVED_DATE cm (S-I), which is oriented per surgeon as follows: short stitch\u000a= superior, long stitch = lateral. The specimen is inked by the prosector per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThere is one needle localization wire, which exits the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen with no clear entrance. The specimen was previously radiographed and\u000athe radiograph shows a wire, the clip and diffusely dense tissue without a\u000aspecific mass.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial into a total of\u000afourteen slices (slice 1 = most lateral, slice 14 = most medial). The specimen\u000ais composed of approximately 95% dense and diffusely hemorrhagic focally cystic\u000awhite/pink fibrous stroma abutting all the inked surgical margins. There are\u000aapproximately 5 white synthetic beads extending from slices REMOVED_DATE (corresponding\u000ato area of clip). No discrete masses or lesions are identified. The remainder\u000aof the specimen is composed of less than 5% yellow/tan lobulated adipose\u000atissue. The specimen is entirely submitted from lateral to medial as follows:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slices 1 and 2, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 5 bisected, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 6, (clip), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 7, bisected, 1 frag, in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 8, bisected, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 9, bisected, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 10, sectioned, 2 frags in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 11, bisected, 1 frag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 12, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slices 13 and 14, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aLmb/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/1/2005\u000a[report_end]
