VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, mastectomy w/o regional lymph nodes\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Michael H Roh, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aNo residual in situ or invasive carcinoma present.\u000aScarring, fibrosis and foreign body giant cell reaction consistent with biopsy\u000asite changes.\u000aSkin with scarring.\u000aLEFT BREAST, MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY:\u000aMETASTATIC CARCINOMA (3.5 cm) to one (1) of sixteen (16) axillary lymph nodes\u000a(see NOTE).\u000aTumor shows extensive necrosis.\u000aSmall foci of extracapsular extension are identified.\u000aImmunohistochemistry performed at BWH demonstrates the following staining\u000aprofile in tumor cells:\u000aPositive - ER, PR (low; <1% of cells), Her2/neu (weak, patchy)\u000aThe immunohistochemical profile supports the above diagnosis.\u000aBreast with one intraparenchymal lymph node showing fibrosis and scarring\u000aconsistent with prior biopsy site.\u000aNo in situ or invasive carcinoma identified in breast parenchyma.\u000aSkeletal muscle present.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aNOTE: The tumor in the current specimen (left axillary lymph node) is\u000ahistologically slightly different from this patient's contralateral primary\u000abreast carcinoma diagnosed in 2000 in the right breast (BWH ref REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID;\u000a8/14/2000). The current neoplasm shows some columnar changes and scattered\u000apleomorphic cells. These differences could probably be attributed to prior\u000atreatment. In addition, the current tumor is weakly positive for Her2/neu and\u000avery focally positive for PR (the contralateral primary was ER and PR positive,\u000aand Her2/neu negative). The latter finding is likely related to aromatase\u000ainhibitor therapy. The weak and patchy positivity for Her2/neu is of uncertain\u000asignificance and the paraffin block will be submitted for fluorescence in situ\u000ahybridization (FISH) for analysis of amplification of the gene. The results of\u000athis study will be reported in an addendum. The left mastectomy specimen has\u000abeen thoroughly examined in an attempt to identify a possible tiny left breast\u000acarcinoma but no tumor mass was identified in the breast parenchyma. Although\u000athere are some morphologic and immunophenotypic differences between the current\u000aneoplasm and the one diagnosed in 2000 (right breast), spread from the\u000acontralateral primary cannot be excluded.\u000aSelected slides of this case were reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, Breast\u000aPathology Service, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 55 year old woman with R invasive ductal CA treated 2000 with\u000aBCT/XRT/chemo tx presented with mass in left axilla. Core bx L ax mass and\u000aimaging c/w metastatic breast disease; occult disease in left breast suspected.\u000aOperation: L MRM, R simple prophylactic mastectomy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left occult breast CA.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) right breast (suture @ tail of spence).\u000aB/2) left breast (suture on axillary tail).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patients\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1 - right breast (suture @ tail of spence)", consists of a\u000aright simple mastectomy specimen weighing 723 grams (20.5 x 16 x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000aincluding a skin ellipse (17.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm), which contains a nipple (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm). There is a 3 cm linear scar that is present at the inferior/lateral\u000aaspect of the nipple (at a distance of 1 cm from the areola). The tail of\u000aspence is marked by a suture per surgeon and measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The\u000adeep aspect of the specimen consists of a smooth and glistening fascial plane\u000awith no skeletal muscle grossly evident. This deep surface is inked blue.\u000aSerial sectioning of the specimen reveals an area of granular fibrous tissue\u000a(3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) within the breast parenchyma that is located 1 cm to the\u000anearest inked deep margin. This area corresponds to and is deep to the scar on\u000athe skin ellipse. The remainder of the breast parenchyma consists of over 90%\u000aadipose tissue with intervening white fibrous strands. No other lesions are\u000aidentified. The tail of spence is examined for lymph nodes and none are\u000aidentified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: granular area including skin with scar, 1 frag per cassette,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: granular area with respect to deep margin, 3 frags total,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: remainder of granular area, 2 frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper/outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower/outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper/inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower/inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2 - left breast suture on axillary tail", consists of a left\u000amodified radical mastectomy specimen (1.2 kilograms; 30 x 20 x 5 cm), including\u000aan axillary tail (12 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) marked by suture per surgeon and a skin\u000aellipse (22 x REMOVED_DATE cm) that includes a nipple (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The deep aspect\u000aof the specimen consists of a smooth and glistening fascial plane (inked blue)\u000awith no skeletal muscle grossly evident. Within the axillary tail there is a\u000aprominent enlarged lymph node (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that is grossly involved by\u000afirm white tissue interspersed with tan/brown areas. This node is well\u000aencapsulated. Examination of the breast parenchyma reveals no grossly apparent\u000alesions; in fact, over 95% of the breast parenchyma is composed of adipose\u000atissue with scarce intervening tan/white fibrous tissue strands. There is a\u000asingle intramammary lymph node at the 3 o'clock position marked by a clip\u000aconsistent with a previously biopsied node. This node measures approximately\u000a0.8 cm in greatest dimension. Examination of the axillary tail tissue reveals\u000aan additional lymph node measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. This node displays\u000afatty replacement. Fourteen additional nodes are identified. This gross\u000aspecimen was reviewed with Dr. Lester.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: largest lymph node and surrounding fibroadipose tissue, 1\u000afrag per cassette, RSS (approximately 40% of this node is\u000asubmitted for histologic evaluation).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: second largest lymph node with fatty replacement, 2 frags\u000atotal, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: bisected lymph node, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: three lymph nodes, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: four lymph nodes, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: two lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: two lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: area surrounding clip including previously biopsied\u000aintramammary node, 3 frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper/outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower/outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper/inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower/inner quadrant, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aROH/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Michael H Roh M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aPrelim Dx Release by Michael H Roh M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aNo Action - Prelim Sent by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/17/2006\u000aSurgical Addendum: (1/24/06)\u000aFISH was performed by USLABS using the PathVysion HER-2 DNA Probe Kit with the\u000afollowing results (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aResult: NOT AMPLIFIED\u000aThe Her-2/cen 17 ratio = REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average of Her-2/neu copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe average of cen 17 copies per cell: REMOVED_DATE \u000aThe cutoff point for assessing HER-2/neu gene amplification is a ratio of REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/24/2006\u000a[report_end]
