VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, REMOVED_DATE cm, poorly differentiated (modified\u000aBloom-Richardson grade III/III).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aNo DCIS present.\u000aThe deep margin is free of malignancy.\u000aOne (1) lymph node with no evidence of malignancy.\u000aNOTE: Immunoperoxidase studies will be performed and reported in an\u000aaddendum.\u000aThe histologic appearance would be consistent with a recurrence of the\u000apatient's prior poorly differentiated ductal carcinoma diagnosed in 1987.\u000aSKIN, "SUPERIOR FLAP AND OLD SCAR":\u000aSkin with cicatrix.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma:\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aThe patient's prior breast carcinoma in 1987 was also negative for estrogen and\u000aprogesterone receptors.\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity\u000ain >10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 52-year-old female with history of right breast cancer (11/87)\u000a(infiltrating ductal), treated with lumpectomy and axillary node dissection,\u000aradiation therapy and chemotherapy. Mammogram (5/20/99) showed a high density,\u000around mass in lower inner quadrant, also visible on ultrasound. Core biopsy\u000a(5/21/99) showed invasive ductal carcinoma, poorly differentiated.\u000aOperation: Right simple mastectomy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Recurrent right breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1 Right breast: short suture superior; long suture lateral;\u000abowtie on axillary tail.\u000a#2 Superior flap and old scar.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by STERN,HOWARD M.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two (2) parts, each labeled with the\u000apatient's name and unit number.\u000aPart one, labeled "right breast with short suture superior, long lateral,\u000abowtie on axillary tail", consists of an 828 gram mastectomy specimen (27 x\u000a23.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with three (3) orienting sutures (short designating superior,\u000along designating lateral, bowtie designating axillary tail). There is a\u000atan/pink skin ellipse (14.7 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a REMOVED_DATE cm centrally located nipple.\u000aA REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm firm, tan/white mass with central hemorrhage is located\u000a4.0 cm from the deep margin (inked black). The mass is inferior and deep to\u000athe nipple. One lymph node and four candidates are identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Mass, 4 fragments, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Deep margin over mass, 3 fragments, RSS.\u000aMicro 8: Upper inner quadrant, 1 fragment, RSS.\u000aMicro 9: Upper outer quadrant, 1 fragment, RSS.\u000aMicro 10: Lower outer quadrant, 1 fragment, RSS.\u000aMicro 11: Lower inner quadrant, 1 fragment, RSS.\u000aMicro 12: Nipple, 1 fragment, RSS.\u000aMicro 13: One lymph node, 2 fragment, ESS.\u000aMicro 14: Four candidate lymph nodes, 4 fragments, ESS.\u000aPart two, labeled superior flap and old scar, consists of a single tan/pink\u000askin ellipse (10.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) excised to a depth of REMOVED_DATE cm. Within the skin\u000aellipse is a REMOVED_DATE cm well-healed surgical scar. The deep and lateral margins\u000aare inked black. No nodules are identified. Approximately 25% of the specimen\u000ais submitted in alternating sections.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Superior flap and old scar, multiple fragments, RSS.\u000ahs\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: SKIN, PLASTIC REPAIR\u000a[report_end]
