VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN LABELED "RIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY":\u000aMass-like area with dense interlobular fibrosis and periductal and perivascular\u000alymphoplasmacytic infiltrates.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aCore biopsy site.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aMultiple levels examined.\u000aNOTE: The stromal fibrosis and lymphocytic infiltrates raise the possibility of\u000aa lymphocytic mastopathy. Similar chronic inflammatory infiltrates are present\u000ain the prior core biopsy and in the skin biopsy.\u000aCase reviewed by the Breast Pathology Service on August 5, 2008.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 56 year old female with right nipple erythema with atypical\u000aintraepidermal cells and abnormal right breast MRI.\u000aOperation: Right breast MRI guided wire localized excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast abnormal MRI.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast MRI guided wire localized excisional biopsy short superior\u000along lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip in specimen. Wire intact. Called to Dr. Golshan by Dr. Ritner.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "right breast MRI guided wire local excision biopsy, short superior, long\u000alateral", consists of an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose\u000abreast tissue (2.9 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm anterior to posterior) with a suture designating the superior margin, and a\u000along suture designating the lateral margin. There is a needle-localization\u000awire in place which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits\u000athrough the medial aspect of the specimen. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol (superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior\u000ablue, posterior black) and is serially sectioned from medial to lateral. The\u000acut surface is tan/yellow and homogeneous with focal area of dense white\u000afibrous tissue constituting approximately 45% of the breast parenchyma. Within\u000athe fibrous tissue is a silver metallic surgical clip. No discrete lesions or\u000aareas of disruption are grossly identified. The specimen is submitted in its\u000aentirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections medial to lateral, block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include\u000aclip, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aSEK/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
