VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: John W. Hanna, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, EXCISION WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP\u000aAXILLARY NODES DISSECTION (including Parts A and B):\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aNEGATIVE LYMPH NODES\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aMultifocal - No\u000aHistologic type - Papillary\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - I (Well) (3 to 5 points)\u000aTubules - > 75% (1 point)\u000aNuclear grade - Low (1 point)\u000aMitotic counts - 1 point\u000aEIC - Positive\u000aLVI - Absent\u000aDermal LVI - Not applicable\u000aSkin invasion - Not applicable\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Not applicable\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Superior, inferior, medial,\u000alateral, anterior, posterior\u000aLYMPH NODES\u000aAxillary lymph nodes - Negative\u000aNumber of positive nodes - 0\u000aNumber of nodes examined - 1\u000aTOTAL POSITIVE NODES (all specimens)- 0:1\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aBiopsy cavity\u000aPapilloma\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition) - REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID MX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Performed previously at BWH\u000aNOTE:\u000aThe tumor is similar in morphology to the core biopsy (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aB. LEFT AXILLARY NODES:\u000aPlease see Part A.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: L papillary neoplasm; this is the excision and sentinel node biopsy.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire-localized lumpectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None provided.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast lumpectomy; long stitch - lateral, short stitch - superior -\u000amammo.\u000aB/2) Left axillary node.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, "left breast lumpectomy, long stitch lateral, short superior", consists\u000aof a 66-gram left breast wire localized lumpectomy specimen with the short\u000astitch designated as superior and the long stitch designated as lateral per the\u000asurgeon (3.4 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior\u000ato posterior). The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation\u000aof margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. A wire enters at the lateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen and is not seen to exit the specimen. The specimen is opened along\u000athe plane of the wire to reveal the termination of the wire as well as the\u000aclip. The clip is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000ainferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin,\u000a3.3 cm from the anterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin. The\u000amedially adjacent area displays mild dark discoloration as well as some focal\u000agranularity consistent with calcifications. The remaining breast tissue\u000aconsists of approximately 80% adipose tissue and 20% fibrous tissue. No firm\u000amasses or lesions are grossly noted. The entire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area surrounding clip, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of breast tissue, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aPart B, "left axillary node", consists of a single fragment of white/tan soft\u000atissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), consistent with lymph node. The sample is\u000abisected and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left axillary lymph node bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aJWH/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 08, 2009 at 03:01:38PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE (but heterogeneous)\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Immunoperoxidase studies for p63 and cytokeratin REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID reveal that\u000athere is a minor component of DCIS (with myoepithelial cells) within the area\u000aof invasive carcinoma (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The invasive carcinoma and DCIS\u000aare e-cadherin positive.\u000aThe PR shows marked heterogeneity in the carcinoma with some positive areas and\u000asome negative areas.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in < 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Friday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 16, 2009 at 12:11:59PM\u000a[report_end]
