VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Qinghong Yang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Hejin P Hahn, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SENTINEL LYMPH NODE, LEFT AXILLA (including FSA):\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor (0:1).\u000a(3 H&E levels examined).\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aRECURRENT INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm, with associated matrix\u000aproduction, located in scar of prior excision site\u000aMultifocal - no\u000aHistologic Type - ductal\u000aHistologic Grade (SBR) - III (Poor)\u000aNuclear Grade - high\u000aEIC - negative\u000aLymphovascular invasion - absent\u000aDermal lymphatic invasion - absent\u000aSkin invasion - absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - absent (only tiny fragment of skeletal\u000apresent)\u000aFinal Margins (invasive) -\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - deep margin\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aTypes - comedo, solid, cribriform\u000aNuclear grade - high\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - absent\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - present\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks:total - 4:13 blocks\u000aFinal Margins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) - deep margin\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aBiopsy cavity\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aSkin with scar\u000aTiny fragment of skeletal muscle\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) - performed previously\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 59-year-old female with history of left breast cancer status post\u000abreast conservation for ER negative, PR negative, HER-2/neu negative, recurrent\u000aleft breast cancer at lumpectomy site.\u000aOperation:\u000a1. Left axilla sentinel node biopsy.\u000a2. Left breast simple mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Recurrent left breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left axilla sentinel node to FS.\u000aB/2. Left breast simple mastectomy short stitch-superior, long-axillary tail.\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aArrival time: 8:22 am\u000aReport time: 8:37 am\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT AXILLA SENTINEL NODE" (FSA):\u000aOne (1) lymph node, negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: JASON L. HORNICK, M.D., PH.D.\u000aResident: ELIZABETH A. STEENSMA, MD\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left axilla sentinel node", received fresh in the frozen section\u000aroom consists of one fragment of pink/tan soft tissue (2.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000acontaining one lymph node candidate (0.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The lymph node\u000acandidate was previously entirely frozen as FSA.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: FSA remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Left breast simple mastectomy short superior long axillary tail",\u000aconsists of a 630 g left simple mastectomy specimen (17.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000aoriented per surgeon with two sutures as indicated. There is an adherent tan\u000askin ellipse (10.6 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a central everted tan/pink nipple (0.7 cm in\u000adiameter) surrounded by a tan wrinkled areola (6.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The epithelial\u000asurface is focally discolored blue/green and is consistent with dye for\u000asentinel node detection. A REMOVED_DATE cm lateral to the nipple, there is a\u000aretracted/puckered scar (3.0 cm). The specimen is serially sectioned from\u000amedial to lateral. A REMOVED_DATE cm deep to the lateral skin/scar and coming to within\u000a0.2 cm of the deep margin at the junction of the upper outer and lower outer\u000aquadrants, there is an ill-defined firm tan/pink multinodular mass (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a2.0 cm). The mass is focally friable yellow/tan necrotic. Contiguous with the\u000ainferior aspect of the mass is a well delineated fibrous cavity (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.0 cm) consistent with prior lumpectomy procedure, which comes to within REMOVED_DATE \u000acm of the deep margin in the lower outer quadrant. The cavity is surrounded by\u000aareas of hemorrhage and gritty yellow/tan possible fat necrosis.\u000aRepresentative mass/tissue is previously allocated to the breast tissue bank.\u000aThe remainder of the cut surface is composed of approximately 25% focally lumpy\u000awhite/tan fibrostroma and 75% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue with no\u000aadditional discrete lesions identified.\u000aAn axillary tail is not present. The most lateral breast tissue is examined\u000afor intramammary lymph node candidates, but none are grossly identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple/areola and skin with scar, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Ill-defined mass, junction upper outer and lower outer quadrants\u000asequentially submitted from lateral to medial (deep margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi\u000afrags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Biopsy cavity, lower outer quadrant sequentially submitted from\u000alateral to medial, 1 frag in each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrostroma upper outer quadrant, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrostroma upper inner quadrant, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrostroma lower outer quadrant, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fibrostroma lower inner quadrant, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aLMB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Hejin P Hahn M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/19/2007\u000a[report_end]
