VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Anna Laury, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia arising in columnar cell change,\u000aassociated with calcifications.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aCore biopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aentire specimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 56 year old female, S/P REMOVED_DATE breast conserving therapy whose REMOVED_DATE \u000amammograms demonstrate LUOQ calcifications which were increased. Stereotactic\u000acore biopsy on REMOVED_DATE  revealed atypical ductal hyperplasia for which excision\u000ais recommended.\u000aOperation: L breast wire localization biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal L mammogram, history of R breast cancer.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) left breast tissue with short stitch/superior, long stitch/lateral, whip\u000astitch/deep margin (to mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCalled Dr. Rhei - specimen with clip, wire, and calcs, Swami 3:30 pm.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000amedical record number, and "#1. Left breast tissue with short stitch superior,\u000along stitch lateral; whipstitch deep margin", and consists of a lumpectomy\u000aspecimen (6.1 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aanterior to posterior). The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is a localization wire\u000aentering at the inferior/anterior aspect of the specimen and exits at the\u000aposterior/superior aspect of the specimen. The specimen is received with an\u000aaccompanying radiograph indicating the presence of a mass and a clip. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to reveal a core biopsy\u000asite (1.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a metal clip. This biopsy site abuts the\u000aanterior and superior inks, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from inferior,\u000a1.0 cm from lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm from medial. Approximately 60% of the breast\u000atissue is composed of white fibrous tissue, the remainder is yellow lobulated\u000abreast tissue. The specimen is entirely submitted for histology with the\u000abiopsy cavity in cassettes REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and the clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen from medial to lateral, multi frags, ESS.\u000aARL/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday REMOVED_MONTH 24, 2009 at 04:13:35PM\u000a[report_end]
