VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Lymph nodes, regional resection\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: SARA RUSSELL M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kyle L. Viani, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT AXILLARY CONTENTS:\u000aMETASTATIC MELANOMA to three of twenty seven lymph nodes (3:27).\u000aB. ADDITIONAL RIGHT AXILLARY LEVEL 3 LYMPH NODES:\u000aThree lymph nodes, negative for tumor (0:3).\u000aC. RIGHT BREAST. T:\u000aMETASTATIC MELANOMA (2.4 cm), present as a nodule within the deep subcutaneous\u000atissue of the breast.\u000aMargins are negative for tumor (the closest margin is the superior margin which\u000aalso appears to be negative.).\u000aBreast epithelium with columnar cell change.\u000aImmunohistochemistry performed at BWH demonstrates the following\u000astaining profile in lesional cells:\u000aPositive - S-100, MART-1\u000aNegative - Pan-K\u000aThe immunohistochemical profile supports the above diagnosis.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 78 male with right axillary nodal met melanoma and suspicious right\u000abreast mass assoc'd with nipple/areola (gynecomastia vs carcinoma).\u000aOperation: Radical right axillary lymphadenectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None provided.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Right axillary contents 9suture = superior).\u000aB/2) Additional right axillary Level 3 lymph nodes.\u000aC/3) Right breast (suture - superior)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "right axillary contents, suture superior", consists of an\u000airregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose tissue (15.8 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a3.0 cm) with a suture designating the superior margin. There is an attached\u000atan/white wrinkled skin ellipse (6.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The epidermal surface is\u000atan/white and bosselated with no discrete lesions grossly identified. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned and there are several well-circumscribed\u000apink/tan smooth rubbery nodules consistent with lymph nodes (ranging from REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). Representative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Attached skin ellipse, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 8 possible lymph nodes, 8 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 7 possible lymph nodes, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 5 possible lymph nodes, 5 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible lymph node bisected, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible lymph node serially sectioned, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 possible lymph node serially sectioned.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "additional right axillary level 3 lymph nodes", consists of\u000amultiple irregular tan/yellow lobulated portions of fibroadipose tissue (4.5 x\u000a2.8 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is serially sectioned. There are six\u000awell-circumscribed pink/tan smooth rubbery nodules consistent with lymph nodes\u000a(ranging from REMOVED_DATE to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension). The specimen is submitted\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 possible lymph nodes, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 possible lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of fibroadipose tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "right breast", consists of an irregular tan/yellow lobulated\u000aportion of fibroadipose breast tissue (7.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an overlying\u000atan/white wrinkled skin ellipse (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a nipple-areolar complex\u000a(3.2 cm in diameter) and a flattened nipple (0.7 cm in diameter). The\u000aepidermal surface is tan/white bosselated with focal irregular tan/brown macule\u000a(0.4 x REMOVED_DATE cm) which comes within less than REMOVED_DATE cm to the nearest radial\u000amargin. There is a suture designating the superior margin (prosector\u000adesignated 12 o'clock). The specimen is inked (9 o'clock-12 o'clock-3 o'clock\u000ablue, 3 o'clock-6 o'clock-9 o'clock green). The nipple-areolar complex is\u000amarkedly firm. The specimen is serially sectioned from 3 o'clock to 9 o'clock.\u000aUnderlying the nipple is a well-circumscribed pink/tan to tan/white\u000awell-circumscribed firm nodule (2.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which grossly abuts the\u000aoverlying nipple-areolar complex, and comes within and is located REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe nearest inked margin (green). The specimen is serially sectioned and\u000anodules submitted in its entirety. The fibroadipose tissue cut surface is\u000atan/yellow and homogenous with no additional discrete lesions grossly\u000aidentified. There is a scant amount of admixed fibrous tissue which\u000aconstitutes less than 5% of remainder of the breast parenchyma.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 o'clock end, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections nodule, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include one\u000aentire slice quadrisected, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Macule at 9 o'clock aspect of skin ellipse, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 9 o'clock end, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Tuesday REMOVED_MONTH 24, 2009 at 01:26:30AM\u000a[report_end]
