VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A MASS:\u000aIntraductal papilloma.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Hx of right nipple discharge on imaging, intraluminal lesion @ 8\u000ao'clock REMOVED_DATE cm from nipple, felt to be papilloma too small for core bx,\u000aexcision recommended by Radiology\u000aOperation: Right mammographically-directed breast biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right nipple discharge\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aRight breast tissue @ 8 o'clock, (white) Vicryl suture marks area of concern,\u000ashort=superior, long=lateral\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aDon't definitely seen the nodule, but probably there.\u000a---S. Nielsen\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "right breast tissue", and consists of a portion of fibroadipose tissue\u000a(5.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with two orienting sutures (short=superior, long=lateral),\u000aan additional Vicryl (white) suture marking "area of concern," and a needle\u000alocalization wire (entering at the lateral aspect). The specimen is inked per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The\u000aanterior portion of the breast (also marked by Vicryl suture) was previously\u000aincised, creating a flap of breast tissue attached only at the superior aspect.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral revealing a dilated\u000aduct (2.9 cm in length and REMOVED_DATE 4 cm in diameter), approximately REMOVED_DATE cm deep\u000ato the Vicryl suture. Within the duct, there is a tan, slightly firm, adherent,\u000anodular lesion (0.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) abutting the anterior margin and coming to\u000awithin REMOVED_DATE cm of the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the posterior and inferior\u000amargins, REMOVED_DATE cm of the medial margin, and 2 cm of the lateral margin. The\u000alocalization wire terminates just adjacent to the nodular lesion within the\u000aduct. The specimen is accompanied by two radiographs, which show vague\u000adensities but no definite mass. The remaining breast tissue consists of\u000aapproximately 90% tan-white dense fibrous stroma and 5% yellow lobulated\u000aadipose tissue. No other gross lesions are identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: duct, to include lesion, sequentially from medial to lateral,\u000amultiple frags, ESS (nodule in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: medial margin nearest lesion, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral margin nearest lesion, 2 frags, RSS\u000aDFI/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a4/1/2006\u000a[report_end]
