or. There is a firm, white area measuring REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in the central portion of the specimen (Slices 4, 5 and 6),\u000awhich abuts the lateral and inferior margins and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior\u000amargin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin and\u000agreater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin. In addition, there is a tan/grey\u000aarea (possible intraparenchymal lymph node) measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm,\u000alocated in Slice 3. Based on the specimen radiograph, calcifications are most\u000alikely to be located in Slices 3, 4 and 5. The entire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen serially sectioned from superior to\u000ainferior\u000a(7 slices), 7 frags, ESS.\u000amah/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Marian H Harris M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
