VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aRICHARDSON, ANDREA, L., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR A MASS:\u000aAbundant inflammation with microabscess formation and multinucleated\u000agiant cells. The inflammation is diffuse and involves lobules,\u000ainterlobular stroma and fat, and around blood vessels.\u000aThere is no evidence of malignancy.\u000aAcid fast and silver stains are negative for microoganisms.\u000aNOTE: The findings are non-specific. The presence of giant cells raises\u000athe possibility of an abscess related to SMOLD (squamous metaplasia of\u000alactiferous ducts). If a rebiopsy is performed, a portion of the specimen\u000ashould be sent to microbiology for culture.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 35 year old female with left breast mass.\u000aOperation: Core biopsy\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Core biopsy left breast\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by QIAN,XIAOHUA,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber and "L breast core bx", and consists of four tan/yellow cylindrical soft\u000atissue cores measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate.\u000aMicro 1L breast core biopsy, 4 frags, ESS\u000akks\u000aReports to: MATORY, YVEDT M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
