VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: CAPRICE CHRISTIAN GREENBERG M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR AN AREA OF ARCHITECTURAL DISTORTION:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU intermediate nuclear grade involving sclerosing adenosis, see\u000aNote.\u000aColumnar cell change with cysts, calcifications and atypia.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aB. RIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A 4 CM AREA OF HETEROGENEOUS\u000aENHANCEMENT:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU intermediate nuclear grade involving sclerosing adenosis, see\u000aNote.\u000aColumnar cell change with cysts, calcifications and atypia.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aFibroadenoma (0.4 cm)\u000aApocrine cysts with calcifications.\u000aIntraductal papilloma.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for CIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNOTE: The CIS shows a solid pattern involving sclerosing adenosis with\u000acalcifications, which creates a morphological mimic of invasive carcinoma\u000a(several p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID double stains confirm retention of myoepithelial cells\u000aaround involved ducts and lobules). Although the CIS lacks membranous\u000aE-cadherin on stains performed, and in places is low nuclear grade typical of\u000aclassic LCIS, many blocks show intermediate nuclear grade and increased mitoses\u000awhich is not typical of classic LCIS. Both excisions lack necrosis although\u000avery rare single cell necrosis is seen, and the neoplastic cells are ER and PR\u000apositive. In part B, the right breast excision shows CIS with focally even\u000agreater nuclear pleomorphism and increased mitoses present but these areas\u000astill lack E-cadherin membranous staining and are clearly part of the same\u000aneoplastic process (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). In both excisions, the CIS is present at or within\u000a0.1 cm of all 6 margins, plus it is extensively present in 12 of 12 slides in\u000aboth excisions.\u000aOverall, the lesions are very difficult to classify and predicting whether the\u000abiological behavior is more that of DCIS or LCIS is not possible. Some\u000apathologists would certainly consider this to be pleomorphic LCIS. Dr. Susan\u000aLester has reviewed selected slides from the case and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: None given.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Bilateral breast masses.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast loc - short superior, long lateral.\u000aB/2. Right breast loc.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Mass and clip are in specimen. OR. Called, LBS. Clip in specimen, OR\u000acalled, LBS.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. L breast wire localization short superior long lateral",\u000aconsists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue, oriented by\u000asutures designating superior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange (inked by prosector). A localizing wire enters the superolateral\u000aaspect of the specimen and exits medially. Specimen is received with an\u000aaccompanying radiograph demonstrating the localizing wire in place in the area\u000aof the central ill-defined mass marked by a metallic surgical clip. Sectioning\u000ademonstrates a REMOVED_DATE cm hemorrhagic biopsy site filled with an amorphous material\u000abearing the metallic clip. Tissue in this area is ill-defined, firm and\u000afibrous (maximally REMOVED_DATE cm), but a definitive mass or lesion cannot be\u000aidentified. The irregular-appearing fibrous tissue extends to the anterior,\u000ainferior, superior and medial margins, and approaches within REMOVED_DATE cm of the\u000aclosest deep margin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest lateral margin. The entire\u000aspecimen is submitted, sequentially from medial to lateral, with perpendicular\u000amedial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, perpendicular lateral margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and area of\u000ainterest (per metallic clip, wire) in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPlease Note: Tissue is held for further fixation prior to processing.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. R breast wire loc long lateral short superior", consists\u000aof a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures\u000adesignating superior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange (inked by prosector). A localizing wire enters the superomedial\u000aaspect of the specimen and exits superolaterally. Specimen is received with an\u000aaccompanying radiograph demonstrating a metallic clip in the lateral end of the\u000aspecimen within an ill-defined area of encircled abnormality. Please Note:\u000aTissue in this area is focally surgically disrupted and is loosely associated\u000aby a black suture; tissue is partially reconstructed and likely true margins in\u000athis area have been inked. Sectioning in this area demonstrates a REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ahemorrhagic biopsy site filled with granular amorphous material bearing the\u000ametallic surgical clip. Surrounding tissue is fibrous but grossly\u000aunremarkable, without palpable or visible masses, lesions or other\u000aabnormalities grossly. Fibrous tissue is present at all inked margins and\u000acomprises approximately REMOVED_DATE % of the specimen. Tissue is held for further\u000afixation prior to processing. Tissue is entirely submitted, sequentially from\u000alateral to medial, with perpendicular lateral margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, perpendicular\u000amedial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and area of interest per localizing wire, surgical clip in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. Tissue from surgically disrupted area in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially from lateral to medial, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone\u000areceptor, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis\u000ausing the following criteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor\u000acells but only a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for\u000ainvestigational use only.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aDB/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aThursday REMOVED_MONTH 02, 2009 at 06:35:30PM\u000a[report_end]
