VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aFOLKERTH, REBECCA, DUNN, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by FOLKERTH,REBECCA DUNN,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN DESIGNATED "LEFT BREAST; WIRE-LOCALIZATION BIOPSY FOR A CLIP AND A\u000aMAMMOGRAPHIC ABNORMALITY":\u000aBreast tissue with atypical lobular hyperplasia, dense interlobular\u000afibrosis, adenosis, and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 53 year old female with prior history of LCIS, s/p REMOVED_DATE  core\u000abiopsy, LUOQ area of mammographic focal asymmetric density with U/S\u000ademonstrating a REMOVED_DATE cm mass, pathology demonstrates densely fibrotic stroma.\u000aOperation: L breast wire localization biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: L abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. L breast tissue: short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral, whip stitch deep margin.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Masses in specimen. Gougoutas\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BUNDOCK,ELIZABETH A.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number and "#1 L breast tissue", and consists of an oriented portion of\u000afibroadipose tissue (5.5 x 4 x 2 cm) with two localization wires in place and\u000aone short stitch to designate superior, and one long stitch to designate\u000alateral. Also received is a specimen radiograph which demonstrates two (2)\u000awires and a clip. The specimen is received in pathology inked. The specimen\u000ais inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior =\u000ablack; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow;\u000amedial = orange. The specimen is serially sectioned at 3 mm intervals from\u000amedial to lateral revealing a specimen which is entirely composed of tan/white\u000afibrous tissue. A biopsy site is identified and consists of hemorrhagic\u000atissue. The clip is identified in this hemorrhagic focus. No foci are\u000agrossly suspicious for invasive carcinoma, however, the entire specimen is\u000asubmitted for microscopic examination. The biopsy site is submitted in\u000acassettes #3&4 with the area of the clip in cassette #4.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 20 frags, ESS.\u000aeab\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
