VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Qinghong Yang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA/1). LEFT BREAST, UPPER INNER QUADRANT, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (slides REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (slides REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aFlorid epithelial hyperplasia and columnar cell change.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins, see note.\u000aNote: Per operative report, Part C/3 corresponds to a medial marginal\u000aexcision of the specimen in Part A/1, to include a clip that had been\u000aplaced at time of biopsy. This specimen is also reviewed by Dr. D.\u000aDillon of the Breast Pathology Service, who concurs.\u000aB/2). LEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER QUADRANT, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, sold and clinging types, (intermediate to high\u000anuclear grade) with associated calcifications and necrosis, present in\u000aeight (8) of twenty-one (21) blocks.\u000aDCIS is present less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and is at least REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, lateral, superior,\u000amedial and anterior margins.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (low nuclear grade) (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aNote: Although the presence of DCIS in a primarily "clinging" pattern is\u000aunusual, the presence of associated necrosis, high nuclear grade and\u000aloss of progesterone receptor expression (see addendum for special\u000astudies for ER, PR and Her2/Neu) support classification as this lesion\u000aas DCIS. Selected slides are also seen by Dr. D. Dillon and Dr. S.\u000aLester of the Breast Pathology Service, who concur.\u000aAt the time of initial biopsy for two areas of microcalcifications in\u000athe left breast, a clip was placed at both sites. The excision\u000aspecimens (parts A through C) contain one clip by radiographic imaging,\u000aassociated with the upper inner quadrant specimens (parts A and C). A\u000aclip is not present in the upper outer quadrant excision radiograph;\u000ahowever biopsy site changes are present histologically. Clinical\u000acorrelation is advised.\u000aC/3). LEFT BREAST, UPPER INNER QUADRANT, ADDITIONAL MEDIAL MARGIN RESECTION FOR\u000aA CLIP:\u000aBenign breast tissue with intraductal papilloma.\u000aBiopsy site changes are present.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 62 y o woman with multiple sites micro-calcs S/P stereo core LUQ\u000aconsistent with ADH; LU mid/inner consistent with ADH. Recommended for\u000asurgical excisional biopsy.\u000aOperation: Needle localization breast biopsy two sites.\u000aOperative Findings: None specified.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast abnl mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) LUIQ middle --> Re-mammo 13:50 p.m.; L breast.\u000aB/2) LUOQ posterior bracketed, L breast.\u000aC/3) Additional medial margin; short - superior, long - lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aA/1) No clip in specimen. OR called/ PCS.\u000aB/2) Specimen image showed 2 intact hook-wires and calcifications. Spoke\u000ato\u000aDr. Lockhart at 2:35 p.m.. - Any Tsai.\u000a3/C) Specimen image demonstrates biopsy clip; discussed with D. Lockhart-\u000aAndy Tsai\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A is sublabeled "#1. LUIQ middle" and consists of a breast excision\u000aspecimen (4.5 lateral to medial x REMOVED_DATE superior to anterior x REMOVED_DATE anterior to\u000aposterior) with a long suture marking the lateral margin and a short suture\u000amarking the superior margin. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per\u000apathology protocol (red = superior, green = inferior, lateral = yellow, medial\u000a= orange, anterior = blue, posterior = black). An accompanying radiograph of\u000athe specimen shows a localization wire but no clip or calcifications present in\u000athe specimen. The localized wire was separated from the specimen when received\u000aby the prosector. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral\u000ainto 11 slices (medial margin slice #1 and lateral margin slice #11). The\u000aspecimen is composed by about 60% tan/white ill-defined fibrotic tissue and 40%\u000atan/yellow unremarkable adipose tissue. There is no discrete mass lesion\u000aidentified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: The medial margin, slice #1, perpendicularly sectioned, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, bisected, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, trisected, 1 frag in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and 2 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, bisected, 1 frag in each block, 2 frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, bisected, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: The lateral margin, slice #11, perpendicularly sectioned, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aPart 2 is sub-labeled "#2. LUOQ posterior bracketed left breast" and consists\u000aof a breast excisional specimen (5.5 lateral to medial x REMOVED_DATE anterior to\u000aposterior x REMOVED_DATE superior to inferior) with two localization wire entering from\u000athe lateral inferior edge of the specimen and ends at the medial side of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol (red =\u000asuperior, green = inferior, lateral = yellow, medial = orange, anterior = blue,\u000aposterior = black). There is an accompanying radiograph showing the two\u000alocalization wires in place (surrounding a tight cluster of calcifications) at\u000athe medial half of the specimen. No clips are identified. There is a\u000atan/white fibrotic ill-defined area extending from the medial to the lateral\u000amargin (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that abuts the closest inferior, anterior, medial,\u000alateral and superior margins and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest posterior margin.\u000aThe specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral into nine slices\u000a(medial margin in slice #1 and lateral margin in slice #9). There are possible\u000abiopsy site changes in slice #2 and #3.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1, the medial margin, perpendicularly sectioned, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, trisected, 3 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, 4 frags, 1 frag in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID each and 2 frags in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, bisected, 2 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, trisected, 3 frags, 1 frag in each block, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: The lateral margin, slice #9, perpendicularly sectioned, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aPart 3 is sub-labeled "#3. Additional medial margin, short superior, long\u000alateral" and consists of a breast excisional specimen (3.5 inferior to superior\u000ax REMOVED_DATE anterior to posterior x REMOVED_DATE lateral to medial) with a short suture\u000amarking the superior margin and a long suture marking the lateral margin. The\u000aspecimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol (red = superior, green\u000a= inferior, lateral = yellow, medial = orange, anterior = blue, posterior =\u000ablack). An accompanying radiograph demonstrates a metal clip in the middle of\u000athe specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior\u000ainto eight slices (superior margin slice #1 and inferior margin slice #8).\u000aThere is an ill-defined tan/white fibrotic area (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with\u000abiopsy changes at the superior side where the clip is found in slice #2. This\u000aill-defined tan/white fibrotic area abuts all margins.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, slice #1, perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, the inferior margin, perpendicularly sectioned, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aQY/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/4/2008\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (3/08/2008):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000ainvasive carcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
