VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:REMOVED_CASE_ID Report Status: Signed\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aDIAGNOSIS:\u000aLeft breast, needle biopsy at 12 o'clock:\u000aCarcinoma in situ, indeterminate whether lobular or ductal.\u000aForeign body reaction with cholesterol clefts.\u000aNOTE: E-Cadherin stain is mixed positive and negative in the carcinoma in\u000asitu.\u000aThe case was referred to Dr. Frederick Koerner, Massachusetts General\u000aHospital, who made the following comments:\u000a"The underlying tissue in this core biopsy specimen contains a collection\u000aof debris and histiocytes, which one might see as a consequence of rupture\u000aof a duct. A uniform population of atypical epithelial cells fills and\u000adistends the acini in an adjacent lobules. The cells appear enlarged and\u000athey contain slightly enlarged nuclei, homogeneous chromatin,\u000ainconspicuous nucleoli, and modest amounts of eosinophilic cytoplasm. The\u000acells do not appear polarized nor do they form cribriform spaces; instead,\u000athey grow as a sheet of polygonal cells. Although these findings\u000aestablish the diagnosis of carcinoma in situ, they do not allow me to\u000aidentify the nature of the malignant cells. The stain for E-cadherin\u000apresents a complicated picture. The residual luminal cells of the acini\u000ashow strong, linear staining for the protein, and the residual\u000amyoepithelial cells stain with a fainter, granular, and interrupted\u000apattern. Slight proliferation and off-center planes of section make the\u000amyoepithelial cells seem especially numerous. Among these preexisting\u000abenign cells sit the carcinoma cells, which fail to stain for E-cadherin.\u000aI would therefore conclude that the carcinoma in situ most likely\u000arepresents lobular carcinoma in situ. Because of the complexity of the\u000aimmunohistochemical staining pattern and the small size of the focus, it\u000astrikes me as prudent to postpone classification of the carcinoma in situ\u000auntil one can study an excision specimen through."\u000aImmunohistochemical staining on the formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded\u000ablock reveals these results for the ductal carcinoma in situ:\u000aEstrogen receptor: POSITIVE (>10% of nuclei stained)\u000aFinal Diagnosis by: Richard J. Sampson, M.D.\u000aClinical diagnosis & history:\u000aStatus post right mastectomy in Maine ten years ago for cancer; solid appearing\u000amass at 12 o'clock, left breast; previous benign biopsy, left; cancer;\u000acomplicated cyst; fibroadenoma\u000aTissue-Designation:\u000aBreast, needle biopsy - left, 12 o'clock\u000aGross description:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin in one container, labeled "guided breast\u000abiopsies, left breast" and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. aggregate of soft,\u000atan-pink, glistening needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter each. 1\u000aagg. all.\u000aDictated by: LV____(signature on file)____DENNIS J. FEEN, M.D. REMOVED_DATE  (Final)\u000a____(signature on file)____RICHARD SAMPSON, M.D. REMOVED_DATE  (Final)\u000a[report_end]
