VAccession Number  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_CASE_ID Report Status  Signed Type  Surgical Pathology Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  DIAGNOSIS  Left breast  needle biopsy at 12 o clock  Carcinoma in situ  indeterminate whether lobular or ductal  Foreign body reaction with cholesterol clefts  NOTE  E Cadherin stain is mixed positive and negative in the carcinoma in situ  The case was referred to Dr  Frederick Koerner  Massachusetts General Hospital  who made the following comments   The underlying tissue in this core biopsy specimen contains a collection of debris and histiocytes  which one might see as a consequence of rupture of a duct  A uniform population of atypical epithelial cells fills and distends the acini in an adjacent lobules  The cells appear enlarged and they contain slightly enlarged nuclei  homogeneous chromatin  inconspicuous nucleoli  and modest amounts of eosinophilic cytoplasm  The cells do not appear polarized nor do they form cribriform spaces  instead  they grow as a sheet of polygonal cells  Although these findings establish the diagnosis of carcinoma in situ  they do not allow me to identify the nature of the malignant cells  The stain for E cadherin presents a complicated picture  The residual luminal cells of the acini show strong  linear staining for the protein  and the residual myoepithelial cells stain with a fainter  granular  and interrupted pattern  Slight proliferation and off center planes of section make the myoepithelial cells seem especially numerous  Among these preexisting benign cells sit the carcinoma cells  which fail to stain for E cadherin  I would therefore conclude that the carcinoma in situ most likely represents lobular carcinoma in situ  Because of the complexity of the immunohistochemical staining pattern and the small size of the focus  it strikes me as prudent to postpone classification of the carcinoma in situ until one can study an excision specimen through   Immunohistochemical staining on the formalin fixed  paraffin embedded block reveals these results for the ductal carcinoma in situ  Estrogen receptor  POSITIVE   10  of nuclei stained  Final Diagnosis by  Richard J  Sampson  M D  Clinical diagnosis   history  Status post right mastectomy in Maine ten years ago for cancer  solid appearing mass at 12 o clock  left breast  previous benign biopsy  left  cancer  complicated cyst  fibroadenoma Tissue Designation  Breast  needle biopsy   left  12 o clock Gross description  The specimen is received in formalin in one container  labeled  guided breast biopsies  left breast  and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm  aggregate of soft  tan pink  glistening needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm  in diameter each  1 agg  all  Dictated by  LV____ signature on file ____DENNIS J  FEEN  M D  REMOVED_DATE   Final  ____ signature on file ____RICHARD SAMPSON  M D  REMOVED_DATE   Final   report_end 
