VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRODSKY, GILBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRODSKY,GILBERT,M.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aCysts and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with benign epithelium\u000aCore biopsy site.\u000aNOTE: No areas of adenosis with atypia or florid hyerplasia are present in\u000athis specimen, and there is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of\u000athe specimen, including margins.\u000aDr. A. Richardson has reviewed the case and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Hx adenosis with atypia on core for calcs @ BWH; clip placed at\u000atime of core bx in REMOVED_MONTH has migrated in 6 cm medial to\u000acalcifications & probably will not be included in specimen.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. R breast tissue. Short superior, long lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcs in specimen. OR called. lbt\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by SMOUSE,JASON H.,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "#1 right breast tissue, long lateral, short superior", and consists of a\u000asingle fragment of fibroadipose tissue (6 x 4 x 2 cm) which is oriented with\u000atwo sutures, short superior, long lateral, and has a needle localizing wire\u000aprotruding from the lateral aspect of the specimen. The specimen is\u000aaccompanied by a radiograph designating that calcifications are within the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is inked by the pathologist per pathology protocol for\u000athe evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red;\u000ainferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from posterior to anterior revealing a fibrofatty breast parenchyma\u000awith no discrete masses or lesions. The specimen consists of 30% fibrous\u000atissue and 70% adipose tissue. The area of calcifications are entirely\u000asubmitted for histologic evaluation. 100% of the specimen is submitted\u000afor histologic evaluation. A single area of hemorrhage is identified and\u000alocated equidistant between the anterior and posterior margins measuring REMOVED_DATE cm\u000ain greatest dimension and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin and greater than\u000a0.5 cm from all other margins.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  area of calcification, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 6: potential core biopsy site, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of fibrous tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of specimen, ESS\u000ajs\u000aReports to: CARTER, KATHARINE M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
