VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kevin B Long, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Robert D Odze, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aFocal columnar change and calcifications associated with benign epithelium (see\u000anote).\u000aCore biopsy site identified.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aNOTE:\u000aThere are areas of irregular architecture such as cribriform spaces associated\u000awith the columnar cell change, as seen on the core biopsy. The lack of\u000asignificant nuclear atypia supports the diagnosis of columnar cell change\u000aversus flat epithelial atypia or atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Left breast needle loc.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left breast core biopsy.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue, FS; short superior, long lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and wire in specimen. Chad Harston, MD REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue; short superior, long lateral", and consists of an\u000aovoid fragment of yellow/tan to tan/pink fibroadipose tissue (4.5 S/I x REMOVED_DATE M/L\u000ax REMOVED_DATE A/P cm), which is oriented per surgeon with two sutures as indicated.\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is one needle-localization wire in place entering the\u000asuperior aspect of the specimen and exiting the anteroinferior aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen was previously radiographed (per radiologist, "clip and\u000awire in specimen"). Specimen is serially sectioned from superior to inferior.\u000aIn the mid to inferior portion of the specimen, there is an encapsulated\u000afleshy, tan/pink focally hemorrhagic mass (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000alateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, and\u000a2.5 cm from the superior margin. The needle-localization wire traverses the\u000aparenchyma adjacent to the mass, and a metal clip is identified within the\u000amass. The remainder of the cut surface is composed of greater than 90%\u000ayellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue and less than 10% delicate white/tan\u000afibrous tissue. No additional masses or lesions are identified.\u000aRepresentative sections to include the mass in entirety are submitted as\u000afollows:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicularly sectioned, 4 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass sequentially submitted from superior to inferior (clip\u000aidentified within mass in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) with anterior, posterior, and lateral margins, 1\u000afrag in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin and additional anterior and posterior margins close\u000ato mass, 1 frag in each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicularly sectioned, 3 frags each, RSS.\u000aLMB/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-9757.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Robert D Odze M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
