VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aFLETCHER, CHRISTOPHER, D.M., M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by FLETCHER,CHRISTOPHER D.M.,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#1. LEFT EXCISIONAL BREAST BIOPSY WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR A\u000aCLIP AND CALCIFICATIONS":\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (two (2) microscopic foci).\u000aFlorid epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign breast epithelium.\u000aPrior biopsy site changes (clip identified).\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#2. LEFT BREAST SUPERIOR 12 O'CLOCK EXCISIONAL BIOPSY":\u000aFibroadenoma (0.5 cm).\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aCalcifications with benign breast epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalcifications left breast, two sites. Fibroadenoma identified\u000aon core @ site of clip. History of DCIS with microinvasion on right.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire loc REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aOperative findings: Microcalcifications in both specimens.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcifications left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Both specimens - ? calcifications, ? fibroadenoma to mammo.\u000a#1 - left breast - inferior wire needle @ 3 o'clock.\u000a#2 - left breast - superior, 12 o'clock, ? calcifications.\u000aRadiograph report: Marker and calcifications in both specimens. Dr. Smith\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WU,DAVID,M.D.\u000aThe specimens are received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit\u000anumber, in two parts.\u000aPart one, specimen labeled "left breast, inferior wire 3 o'clock, ?\u000afibroadenoma", consists of a 6 gram oriented lumpectomy specimen (2.6 cm -\u000asuperior/inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm - medial/lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm - anterior/posterior).\u000aThe specimen comes previously inked by the surgeon with a metallic wire\u000aentering the lateral portion of the specimen, according to the pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue;\u000asuperior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange.\u000aDiscussion with the surgeon states that the pathologist is not to\u000areinforce/re-ink the specimen. The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph\u000alabeled with the patient's name and unit number, and a report that reads\u000a"marker and calcifications in specimen". The specimen is serially sectioned\u000afrom medial to lateral in eight slices to identify a marker metallic clip\u000alocated in the third medial site. Adjacent to the localization of this clip is\u000aa focus of hemorrhage/fibrous tissue (0.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) that comes to within\u000a0.3 cm of the inferior, posterior, anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm of the superior, and\u000agreater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial and lateral margins. The specimen is\u000acomposed of 15% fibrous tissue; the remainder adipose tissue, and the specimen\u000ais serially submitted entirely from medial to lateral.\u000aMicro #1: Medial perpendicular margin, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro #2: Slice 2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro #3: Slice 3 with clip, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro #4-7: Medial to lateral, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro #8-9: Lateral perpendicular margin, may appear green, multiple frags,\u000aESS.\u000aPart two, specimen labeled "superior wire 12 o'clock, ? microcalcs", consists\u000aof a partially oriented 5 gram lumpectomy specimen (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen comes previously inked by the surgeon, according to the pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue;\u000asuperior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. However,\u000adue to extensive green ink leakage, definitive determination of the margins is\u000aimpossible. The gross exam of the specimen was reviewed with Dr. Susan Lester,\u000aBreast Pathology. Moreover, the surgeon was contacted and the surgeon states\u000athat the inked margins are not to be re-inked/re-inforced by the pathologist.\u000aPhotographs of the specimen are taken from multiple prospectives. The specimen\u000ais serially sectioned to reveal dense yellow/white fibrous tissue present at\u000aall margins. There is a single foci of possible hemorrhage (0.3 cm in greatest\u000adimension) that comes to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the nearest inked margin. The marker\u000aclip is not definitively localized by gross exam, nor by radiograph of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is othewise entirely submitted sequentially.\u000aMicro #10: Perpendicular margin, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aMicro #11-16: Sequentially submitted slices, possible lesion in micros REMOVED_DATE  1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro #17: Other perpendicular fragment, multiple frags, ESS.\u000aThe jar containers of both specimens are saved.\u000adwu/\u000aReports to: LESNIKOSKI, BETH-ANN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
