ctions were listed). The diagnoses are\u000aunchanged.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 77 yo F with R breast invasive mucinous Ca + (+) margins.\u000aOperation: R breast re-excision/R ax SLNB.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R breast Ca.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right axillary SL nodes (FS).\u000aB/2. Rt breast re-excision lumpectomy.\u000aC/3. Deep lateral margin.\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aArrival time: 12:48 PM\u000aReport time: Not provided\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1. SENT LN(S) RIGHT AXILLA" (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aOne lymph node with extensive fatty replacement.\u000aNegative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: ALESSANDRA F. NASCIMENTO, M.D.\u000aResident: MATTHIAS D. HOFER, MD\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in three parts each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Right axillary sentinel lymph node", and consists of a single\u000afragment of tan/yellow fibroadipose tissue (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a single\u000awhite/tan lymph node identified. The specimen is entirely submitted as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Non frozen fat, multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Right breast reexcision lumpectomy", consists of a 51-gram\u000areexcision lumpectomy specimen (7.6 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior, REMOVED_DATE cm inferior to superior). There are two orienting sutures, and\u000athe specimen is received inked by the surgeon. The specimen includes a single\u000atan/white skin ellipse on the anterolateral aspect of the specimen (2.7 x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) which contains a well-healed incision. The specimen is serially sectioned\u000ato reveal a biopsy cavity in the center of the specimen (1.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awhich is grossly hemorrhagic and filled with blood. This cavity is located REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from superior, REMOVED_DATE b cm from anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000aposterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from lateral and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin. There is a\u000afirm white/yellow area of fibrous tissue surrounding the cavity which is REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000asuperior, and greater than REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior and medial margin. The\u000aremainder of the specimen consists of yellow lobulated breast tissue with less\u000athan 5% fibrous tissue. Representative sections are submitted for histology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Biopsy cavity, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: White/yellow area adjacent to biopsy cavity, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative posterior and medial margins, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative section of anterior margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections of lateral margin, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional section of biopsy cavity, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aPart C, "#3. Deep lateral margin", and consists of numerous fragments of\u000aunoriented fibroadipose tissue (7.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). No