VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Anna Laury, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED BIOPSY FOR A MASS WITH A CLIP:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, cribriform type (low nuclear grade) with\u000acalcifications, is present in 4 of 15 slides (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). No\u000anecrosis is seen.\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aExtensive atypical ductal hyperplasia associated with columnar cell\u000achange and mucin production is present.\u000aA core needle biopsy site is present.\u000aDCIS is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin (one block) and lateral margin\u000a(one block). The remaining margins are difficult to evaluate due to the\u000aextensive atypical hyperplasia and cautery artifact.\u000aNote: Immunoperoxidase studies for ER, PR, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu will be reported\u000ain an addendum.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition) Tis NX.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 68 year old postmenopausal female with a family history of breast\u000acancer in her mother (60) and daughter with ovarian cancer (24). Bilateral\u000amammogram on REMOVED_DATE  demonstrated a lobular mass; 16 mm by ultrasound. REMOVED_DATE \u000acore biopsy revealed mucin with detached fragments of atypical epithelial\u000acells. Excision is recommended.\u000aOperation: L breast WL excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: none.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast tissue; short suture - superior, long - lateral; whip stitch\u000aon chest wall margin to mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and mass with intact wire seen in the specimen image. Called\u000ato OR at 11 AM.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000amedical record number, and "#1. Left breast tissue short suture superior, long\u000asuture lateral with stitch on chest wall margin", and consists of a single\u000abreast specimen (48.0 grams, REMOVED_DATE S-I, REMOVED_DATE A-P, REMOVED_DATE cm M-L). The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A\u000alocalization wire enters at the inferomedial aspect of the specimen; and is\u000apushed to exit the superolateral aspect. A prior biopsy site is identified\u000aadjacent to the superior aspect of the specimen (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000ahemorrhagic biopsy cavity extends to the red and blue ink, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe orange ink, REMOVED_DATE cm from yellow, REMOVED_DATE cm from green and REMOVED_DATE cm from black\u000aink. The remainder of the specimen is composed of yellow lobulated adipose\u000atissue; no additional suspicious areas are identified. The entire specimen is\u000asubmitted for permanent section.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Biopsy cavity, with clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections of breast tissue from superior to\u000ainferior, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of specimen from superior to inferior, multi frags,\u000aESS.\u000aARL/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday REMOVED_MONTH 23, 2009 at 11:31:04AM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: (6/26/09)\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for DCIS (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aFriday REMOVED_MONTH 26, 2009 at 03:58:57PM\u000a[report_end]
