VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christopher P. Elco, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, RE-EXCISION:\u000aExcision site changes, extending to the posterior, anterior, inferior,\u000aand lateral margins.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aSkin with foreign body giant cell reaction.\u000aNote: Diagnostic features of residual phyllodes tumor are not seen.\u000aThe evaluation of margins is limited by the present of reactive fibroblasts\u000aassociated with the excisional site at the margin.\u000aThe entire specimen was examined microscopically, including margins.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, INFERIOR SHAVE MARGIN, EXCISION:\u000aFibroadenoma, REMOVED_DATE cm (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aExcision site changes are present at the old margin but not at the new\u000amargin.\u000aNote: Diagnostic features of residual phyllodes tumor are not seen\u000aafter complete microscopic examination of the specimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 22 year old female s/p REMOVED_DATE  right core needle biopsy of a mass\u000ashowing a fibroepithelial lesion. S/p REMOVED_DATE  right breast excision of a mass\u000ashowing a low grade REMOVED_DATE cm Phyllodes tumor with positive medial and anterior\u000amargins. Close inferior and lateral margins.\u000aOperation: Right breast re-excision.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast phyllodes tumor.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue, short = superior; long = lateral; Whip stitch = at\u000adeep margin, scar is anterior.\u000aB/2. Right breast inferior shaved margin, Whip stitch = new margin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. R breast tissue-short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral, whip stitch at deep margin, scar is anterior", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating\u000asuperior (short suture), lateral (long suture), and deep (whip stitch) margins.\u000aThere is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm tan skin ellipse with a linear, well-healed scar along\u000aits length on the anterior surface of the specimen. The specimen is inked per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange\u000a(inked by surgeon). Sectioning demonstrates a large band of dense nodular\u000atan/white fibrous tissue (at least REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) in the area of the\u000aparenchymal scar (at least REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Scarred tissue abuts the\u000aclosest posterior, lateral, and inferior margins and approaches within REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aof the closest superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the closest anterior margin, and is\u000aremote (greater than REMOVED_DATE cm) from the closest medial margin grossly. The dense\u000afibrous tissue involves majority of the inferior half of the specimen and\u000aoccupies approximately 70% of the total specimen. The specimen is sectioned\u000ainto eight slices from lateral to medial with visibly scarred parenchyma\u000apresent in slices #2, #3, #4, and #5. Remaining tissue is relatively\u000aunremarkable, without definitive discrete masses grossly. The entire specimen\u000ais examined microscopically.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slices #2 scarred parenchyma, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3 scarred parenchyma, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 scarred parenchyma, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 scarred parenchyma, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1 lateral margin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2 closest inferior, superior, and anterior margins, 2 frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3 closest inferior, superior, and anterior margins, 2 frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 closest inferior, superior, and anterior margins, 2 frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 inferior, anterior margins, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6 breast tissue, anterior and inferior margins, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, #8 medial margins, multi frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining tissue, mult frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. R breast inferior shaved margin, whip stitch new margin",\u000aconsists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue with a whip\u000astitch on one broad surface designating "new margin". The new margin is inked\u000ablack by prosector, opposing surface is inked blue, and the specimen is\u000aserially sectioned revealing focal fibrosis and fat necrosis along the\u000ablue-inked surface, with a REMOVED_DATE cm palpably firm nodule at this blue surface,\u000a1.0 cm from the closest true margin (black). The remainder of the specimen is\u000acomprised of REMOVED_DATE % dense white lobulated fibrous tissue without additional\u000apalpable or visible abnormalities grossly. The entire specimen is examined\u000amicroscopically.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Palpably firm nodule, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Majority of remaining fibrous tissue, sequentially, multi frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining tissue, mult frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aDB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday May 27, 2009 at 02:36:20PM\u000a[report_end]
