VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final Type: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OPERATION DATE: 19 REMOVED_MONTH 95 ACCESSIONED ON: 19 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 15:28 CLINICAL DATA: 78-year-old female with biopsy showing adenocarcinoma of the right breast, now undergoing modified radical mastectomy with axillary node dissection. =============================================================================== *** FINAL DIAGNOSIS *** BREAST (RIGHT), MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY: INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA, GRADE 2, REMOVED_DATE CM, DISPLAYING FOCAL MUCIN PRODUCTION AND FOCAL ENDOCRINE DIFFERENTIATION, INVADING LYMPHATIC VESSELS AND EXTENDING TO REMOVED_DATE CM FROM THE MAGIN OF THE SPECIMEN. MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN. SEE NOTE. LYMPH NODES (RIGHT AXILLA): METASTATIC ADENOCARCINOMA IN TWENTY-ONE OF TWENTY-THREE LYMPH NODES. SEE NOTE. NOTE: Blood vessel invasion is not seen. The metastases in the axillary lymph nodes appear confined to the nodes. Diagnosis by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 25 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 12:54 =============================================================================== *** RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum *** RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST (RIGHT): The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein. The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: In this laboratory, we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way (paraffin sections) and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay. 91% of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. 96% of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections, the predictive value of a positive result was 97% and the predictive value of a negative result was 95%. In this laboratory, we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay. Sixty-five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay, and 98% of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors. THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY. THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES. Added by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 31 REMOVED_MONTH 95 at 16:43 GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number, and "right breast", is a 440 gm modified radical mastectomy specimen designated right, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm with REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm skin ellipse and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm axillary tail. The skin ellipse has a eccentrically-placed nipple and areola complex, REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter, extending to the superior/medial skin margin. No other lesions or incisions are noted on the skin. The superficial aspect of the breast is lobulated, fibroadipose tissue with fascia on the posterior aspect. In the superior/lateral aspect is a palpable nodule. The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm white-tan poorly circumscribed nodule extending to within REMOVED_DATE cm of a deep margin and REMOVED_DATE cm of the superior margin. The remaining breast tissue is fibrous, white-pink and unremarkable. Within the axillary tail is a aggregate of matted nodes, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension. Many of these lymph nodes have a white gritty cross section. Other free but firm to hard nodes are identified within the axilla. SECTION CODE: A: nipple and areolar complex. B,C: tumor with deep margin. D,E: tumor with superior margin. F,G: representative tumor. H,I: breast tissue adjacent to tumor. J: representative breast tissue. K-Q: axillary lymph nodes. Resident: Mark vanGorder, MD =============================================================================== SOURCE CARE UNIT: MGH-INPATIENT (FLR.UNKNOWN) REPORTS TO: Frederick William Ackroyd, M.D. PART : BREAST (RIGHT) MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY\u000a[report_end]
