VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRENN, THOMAS, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRENN,THOMAS,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "NEEDLE-DIRECTED RIGHT BREAST BIOPSY":\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with calcifications.\u000aColumnar alteration of lobules with prominent snouts and secretions with\u000aatypia and calcifications.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread.\u000aCore biopsy site.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000a(Levels examined.)\u000aCase reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson, Breast Pathology, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Microcalc showed ADH wire clip.\u000aOperation: Right needle directed right breast biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast calcifications.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Right breast tissue stitch superficial and medial.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip wire in specimen. No answer in OR/SR\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by RUCKER-SCHMIDT,RACHEL L.,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number, and "right breast tissue, stitch superficial and medial",\u000aconsists of a golden yellow excision breast. The specimen (5.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awhich includes a suture placed by the surgeon to designate the "superficial and\u000amedial" orientation of the specimen. The specimen is accompanied by a\u000aradiograph which shows a localization wire adjacent to a clip which are\u000aadjacent to clusters of microcalcifications. Opposite the needle localization\u000aentrance on the radiograph, there is a larger cluster of microcalcifications.\u000aWith the suture designated as "medial" and the wire designated as "lateral",\u000athe specimen was difficult to orient, but with the help of Dr. Susan Lester the\u000aspecimen is inked as follows: red = superior, green = inferior, blue =\u000aanterior, black = posterior, yellow = lateral, and orange = medial. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral to reveal no discrete\u000amass. Approximately 60% of the specimen is a deep tan to grey, firm fibrous\u000atissue which is focally cystic and grossly extends to the medial, superior,\u000aanterior, and posterior margins. The remainder of the specimen is golden\u000ayellow, lobulated fatty tissue. Representative sections, including all of the\u000afibrous tissue, are submitted for microscopic examination. Approximately 90%\u000aof the specimen was submitted for microscopic examination.\u000aMICRO #1-2: Medial margin, perpendicular, corresponding to largest cluster of\u000amicrocalcifications, 7 frags, ESS.\u000aMICRO #3-4: Sections of body of specimen to include anterior, posterior,\u000asuperior, and inferior margins, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO #5: Section near clip, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO #6-7: Section, bisected, near clip, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO #8-9: Section, bisected, next to clip, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMICRO #10: Section to include inferior and lateral margins, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMICRO #11: Lateral margin, perpendicular, 2 frags, RSS.\u000arlr\u000aReports to: PETERSON, LYNN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
