VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Eric L Snyder, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU.\u000aFibroadenomas, one hyalinized with ossification.\u000aSclerosing adenosis, apocrine metaplasia, and calcifications associated\u000awith benign epithelium.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of right breast mass @ 12 o'clock. Patient had core biopsy @\u000aBWH (clip left). Path was fibroadenoma with LCIS. Excisional biopsy\u000arecommended by radiology.\u000aOperation: Right mammographically-directed breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Abnormal right mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) right breast tissue @ 12 o'clock (short=superior, long=lateral).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aFew Ca++ near clip and wire and coarse calcifications in specimen.\u000aR. Birdwell, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "right breast tissue", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm irregular\u000afragment of fibrofatty tissue with two orienting sutures: short\u000astitch=superior; long stitch=lateral. The anterior aspect is friable. The\u000amargins are inked per the pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is a needle localization wire in-situ, which enters\u000alaterally and appears to be embedded in the parenchyma. The specimen is also\u000aaccompanied by an x-ray with a circled area of interest showing a metal S-clip\u000awith adjacent minute microcalcifications. There is also a large opacified area\u000aoutside of the circle of interest. There is a pink sheet, which states "few\u000acalcifications near clip and wire, and coarse calcification in specimen". The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from anterior to posterior, respectively,\u000ashowing a dense white cut surface with moderate interspersed fat, particularly\u000aat the anterior and posterior ends, where the mid-section is more fibrous. The\u000amacrocalcification is identified in the anterior half of the specimen and\u000ameasures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, and abuts the anterior and superior margins. The\u000amid-portion of the specimen shows an ill-defined area containing radiopaque\u000ainducted material, which obscures the metal clip. There is focal fresh\u000ahemorrhage in this area and there are a few small cysts directly at the tip of\u000athe wire. Representative sections with all of the fibrous tissue are submitted\u000afrom anterior to posterior, respectively in cassettes REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: perpendicular anterior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: anterior breast tissue, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: macrocalcification, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: anterior breast tissue with surrounding margins, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: tissue at tip of wire with small cluster of cyst, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: circled area of interest with surrounding margins, 1 frag in\u000aeach, and blocked in its entirety, ESS (sequential sections with the area of\u000ainterest in Blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: perpendicular posterior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000awp/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Eric L Snyder M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/26/2006\u000a[report_end]
