VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast implant\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-10-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Benjamin J-C Chen, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST IMPLANT, REMOVAL:\u000aGross examination only.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST TISSUE, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA\u000aSize - REMOVED_DATE cm\u000aMultifocal - No\u000aHistologic type - Ductal with associated lymphoid\u000ainfiltrate\u000aHistologic grade (SBR) - III (Poor) (8 or 9 points)\u000aTubules - < 10% (3 points)\u000aNuclear grade - High (3 points)\u000aMitotic counts - 3 points\u000aEIC - Negative\u000aLVI - Absent\u000aDermal LVI - Absent\u000aSkin invasion - Absent\u000aSkeletal muscle invasion - Absent\u000aFinal Margins (invasive)\u000aNegative (> REMOVED_DATE cm) - Deep\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present\u000aTypes - Solid\u000aNuclear grade - High\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Absent\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total - REMOVED_DATE blocks\u000aMargins (DCIS)\u000aClose (0.1 to REMOVED_DATE cm) - Deep (0.1 cm), focal, block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS\u000aCore biopsy site\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium\u000aAJCC Classification (7th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb NX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER, PR, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - Pending\u000aNOTE: Double stain (p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) demonstrates myoepithelial basal cells around\u000afoci of DCIS.\u000aC. LEFT MASTECTOMY SKIN, EXCISION:\u000aSkin with no tumor present.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: LEFT ADH bordering on DCIS (See prior BWH consult); H/O Hodgkins S/P\u000aMantle irradiation; right H/O DCIS 2007.\u000aOperation: Left skin-sparing mastectomy; removal of breast implant; insertion\u000atissue expander with AlloDerm sling.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Lt breast CA.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Lt breast implant.\u000aB/2) Lt breast - stitch --> short - superior, long --> lateral.\u000aC/3) Lt mastectomy skin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "left breast implant", consists of a translucent smooth surface breast\u000aimplant (10.0 cm in diameter x REMOVED_DATE cm in depth) which is filled with a clear\u000agelatinous material consistent with silicone. There are identifying markers\u000apresent "Style 15, lot #REMOVED_CASE_ID, McGhan, 176 cc". No defects or adherent soft\u000atissue is grossly identified. Gross photographs are taken and the specimen is\u000afor gross examination only. No sections are submitted.\u000aPart B, "left breast long lateral, short superior", consists of a 400-g left\u000asimple mastectomy specimen (19.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an overlying tan/white\u000awrinkled skin ellipse (6.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a nipple areolar complex (2.5 cm in\u000adiameter) with an everted nipple (1.1 cm in diameter). There is a short suture\u000adesignating the superior margin, and a long suture designating the lateral\u000amargin. The posterior margin is inked black, and there is a focal pink/tan\u000amembranous area (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) consistent with a breast implant capsule. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned. There is a well-circumscribed pink/tan to\u000ared/brown previous biopsy cavity (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is filled with a\u000ared/brown granular material. The previous biopsy cavity comes within REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the posterior margin (7.0 cm from the nipple areolar complex), and is\u000alocated at the 10:30 position aspect of left upper outer quadrant. Adjacent to\u000athe biopsy cavity is a silver metallic previous biopsy clip. The remainder of\u000athe breast tissue parenchyma is tan/yellow and homogeneous with focal areas of\u000adense white fibrous tissue constituting less than 10% of the breast parenchyma.\u000aNo additional lesions are grossly identified. Representative sections are\u000asubmitted. The lateral aspect of the specimen is sectioned with no lymph nodes\u000agrossly identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple areolar complex, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections of biopsy cavity block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include\u000aarea of metallic clip, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left upper outer quadrant, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left lower inner quadrant, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left upper inner quadrant, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Left lower inner quadrant, 2 frags each, RSS.\u000aPart C, "left mastectomy skin", consists of multiple irregular tan/white\u000awrinkled fragments of skin (7.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The epidermal\u000asurface is tan/white and bosselated with no discrete lesions grossly\u000aidentified. Representative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 4 frags, RSS.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday February 01, 2010 at 04:51:49PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aEstrogen receptor NEGATIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aNote: Normal epithelial cells are appropriately immunoreactive for estrogen\u000aand progesterone receptors.\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in < 30%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >30% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aTuesday February 16, 2010 at 09:29:17AM\u000a[report_end]
