specimen is\u000athoroughly sectioned from medial to lateral, to exhibit a white/tan, firm,\u000afairly well defined mass (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which grossly extends to the\u000amedial margin, and comes to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the inferior and anterior margins,\u000a1.2 cm of the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the posterior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm of\u000athe nearest lateral margin. Surrounding the aforementioned mass is a focus of\u000ahemorrhage (0.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) and multiple tan-white gelatinous synthetic\u000abeads. Within one of the gelatinous beads, a silver metal clip is identified.\u000aThe needle localization wire traverses this area of the mass and gelatinous\u000abeads. The remaining breast tissue consists of approximately 80% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue and 20% tan and white fibrous stromas. The specimen\u000ais accompanied by a radiograph within which radiology confirmed the presence of\u000aa mass, clip, and wire within the specimen.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast tissue sequentially from medial to lateral,\u000amultiple frags, ESS.\u000aNote: Mass present within micros REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aNote: Clip present within micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin nearest mass, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of specimen, 1 frag each, ESS\u000aNOTE: The entire specimen was submitted for review.\u000aDB/mmm\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/3/2006\u000a[report_end]
