VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Dick Guang-Iong Hwang, M.D., Ph.D.; Rachel L Rucker-Schmidt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jonathan N Glickman, M.D., Ph.D.; Andrea L Richardson, M.D.,\u000aPh.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, bordering on low grade DUCTAL CARCINOMA\u000aIN SITU cribriform type (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multiple levels examined). The severely\u000aatypical hyperplasia is associated with calcifications but not necrosis.\u000aThe focus of severely atypical ductal hyperplasia involves a REMOVED_DATE cm area and is\u000apresent less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMultiple additional foci of atypical ductal hyperplasia are present in multiple\u000ablocks.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia.\u000aFibroadenoma (0.8 cm), adjacent to core biopsy site changes.\u000aNOTE: Dr. Susan Lester has also reviewed slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 43 y/o female with h/o bilateral breast reduction with atypical\u000aductal hyperplasia bilaterally. New left breast abnormal MRI with core biopsy\u000arevealing atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None specified.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast ADH.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast wire-localized excisional biopsy at mammo; short suture -\u000asuperior; long suture - lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY: specimen contains wire and clip. (signed by Dr. Lisa Zorn)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "#1. Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy, short\u000astitch superior, long lateral", and consists of a wire-localized breast\u000aexcision specimen (8.2 cm anterior to posterior x REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior x\u000a1.7 cm medial to lateral). The specimen is oriented by the surgeon with a long\u000asuture indicating lateral and a short suture superior. The specimen is inked\u000aper pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A\u000alocalization wire enters the posterior aspect of the specimen with the tip\u000aembedded in the anterior superior aspect. An accompanying radiograph shows a\u000aclip associated with the tip of the wire. The radiograph also shows three\u000aadditional clips, which on the specimen represent clips on the orienting\u000asutures and not within the specimen itself. Associated with the tip of the\u000awire in the clip is apparent core biopsy site with surrounding fat necrosis\u000a(1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with distance to the margins as follows: Anterior REMOVED_DATE cm,\u000asuperior REMOVED_DATE cm, medial REMOVED_DATE cm, lateral REMOVED_DATE cm, inferior REMOVED_DATE cm, and posterior\u000a4.3 cm. Remainder of the specimen consists of 80% yellow lobulated adipose\u000atissue and 20% white/tan fibroadipose tissue. The entire specimen is serially\u000asectioned from anterior to posterior and submitted for microscopic analysis.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, perpendicularly sectioned (core biopsy site in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aand REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections from anterior to posterior (one complete slice in\u000aeach cassette, core biopsy site in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior margin, perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aDH/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-08-REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Andrea L Richardson M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a3/5/2008\u000a[report_end]
