VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, partial mastectomy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kremena V. Star, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY (1098 g):\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aBiopsy cavity.\u000aNo residual DCIS identified.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aB. RIGHT BREAST, ANERIOR INFERIOR SHAVE MARGIN:\u000aAdipose tissue with no significant pathologic change.\u000aC. RIGHT MASTECTOMY SCAR:\u000aSkin with scar.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 55 year old female with PMH of left mastectomy for DCIS in 2000, right\u000areduction mammoplasty. REMOVED_DATE  mammogram supplemented with UIS demonstrates a\u000a7:00 N+6 cm 7 mm mass. Core biopsy REMOVED_DATE  demonstrates atypical papillary\u000alesion. REMOVED_DATE  excision revealed intermediate grade DCIS, EP/PR+, Her2/neu-.\u000aOperation: Right simple skin sparing mastectomy with latissimus dorsi flap\u000areconstruction.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right DCIS, history of L DCIS.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast (breast simple mastectomy) long - lateral short superior.\u000aB/2. Right breast anterior inferior shaved margin whip stitch new margin.\u000aC/3. Right mastectomy scar.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "right breast", consists of a 1098 g right simple mastectomy\u000aspecimen (26.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an overlying pink-tan nipple areolar\u000acomplex (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with an everted nipple (1.1 cm in diameter), and a short\u000asuture designating superior margin, and a long suture designating lateral\u000amargin. There is an irregular tan/white wrinkled skin ellipse (4.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) located within the central breast between the right lower outer quadrant,\u000aand the right lower inner quadrant, with an adjacent irregular smooth walled\u000avariegated pink/tan to red/brown defects (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE cm) consistent with the\u000aprevious biopsy cavity. The previous biopsy cavity is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000anipple areolar complex, REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin (black), and grossly\u000ainvolves the anterior aspect of the specimen (blue). The specimen is serially\u000asectioned and the remainder of the breast parenchyma is tan/yellow and\u000ahomogenous with focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting\u000aapproximately less than 5% of the breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions are\u000agrossly identified. The lateral aspect of the specimen is sectioned with no\u000alymph nodes grossly identified. Within the central portion of the breast is an\u000airregular punctate dense white fibrous firm area (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is\u000alocated REMOVED_DATE cm from the previous biopsy cavity. Representative sections are\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nipple-areolar complex, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior aspect defect/biopsy cavity to include skin ellipse, 3\u000afrags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Biopsy cavity to nipple-areolar complex, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Biopsy cavity to deep margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections biopsy cavity medial and lateral.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections right upper outer quadrant, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections right lower outer quadrant, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections right upper inner quadrant, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Representative sections lower inner outer quadrant, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Separate ill-defined punctate fibrous areas central breast, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "right anterior breast inferior shave margin", consists of an\u000airregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose tissue (5.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm) with a suture designating "new margin". The surface designated by the\u000asuture is the "new margin" and is tan/yellow, smooth and concave and inked\u000ablue. The opposite surface "old margin" is tan/yellow and slightly ragged and\u000ainked black. The specimen is serially sectioned and the cut surface is\u000atan/yellow and homogenous with no discrete lesions or areas of disruption\u000agrossly identified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, labeled "right mastectomy scar", consists of an irregular tan/white\u000awrinkled skin ellipse (21.2 cm x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The epidermal surface is\u000atan/white and bosselated with no discrete lesions grossly identified.\u000aRepresentative sections are submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Cross sections, 5 frags, RSS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000asek/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aFriday May 29, 2009 at 12:21:12PM\u000a[report_end]
