VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, 12:00, 11 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY FOR A SOLID,\u000aLOBULATED NODULE:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA, POORLY DIFFERENTIATED, at least REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000asize (SEE NOTE).\u000aNo in situ carcinoma is seen.\u000aImmunoperoxidise studies are performed on formalin fixed tissue:\u000aER - POSITIVE\u000aPR - NEGATIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/NEU - NEGATIVE\u000aGCDFP - FOCALLY POSITIVE\u000aNOTE: The carcinoma consists of sheets and nests of cells with large\u000apleomorphic nuclei and abundant cytoplasm. The carcinoma is\u000ahistologically similar to areas of the patient's ovarian carcinoma\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and colon metastasis (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aCompared to the ovarian carcinoma, the breast carcinoma demonstrates\u000aslightly more GCDFP immunoreactivity and diminished progesterone receptor\u000aand REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu positivity. Both carcinomas are immunoreactive for estrogen\u000areceptor. Although metastatic ovarian carcinoma to the breast is favored,\u000aa separate breast primary carcinoma cannot be excluded.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe core needle biopsy as well as the subsequent breast excision (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aand prior ovarian excision (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) were reviewed by Dr. Paul Peter Rosen at\u000aCornell Medical Center (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ) and the following is his\u000aconsultation report:\u000aThe slides of ovarian and breast tumors from the above-named patient were\u000areviewed by Dr. Hoda in my absence and are now re-reviewed at the request of\u000athe patient.\u000aSlides "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" show a high-grade, solid and papillary adenocarcinoma of the\u000aovary, predominantly serous type.\u000aSlides "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" a needle core biopsy, and "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" a lymph node and\u000abreast biopsy, show poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma with a modest\u000alymphoplasmacytic reaction in breast tissue. There is no obvious intraductal\u000acarcinoma at the site of the approximately 6mm breast tumor.\u000aAlthough, the carcinoma in the breast bears a resemblance to the ovarian\u000acarcinoma, there are some notable differences. The structure of the breast\u000atumor is entirely solid and virtually syncytial, lacking the papillary and\u000asecondary gland features of the ovarian carcinoma. Mitoses are more numerous\u000ain the ovarian tumor (35 per 10 high power fields) than in the breast lesion (5\u000aper 10 high power fields). There are also subtle cytologic differences since\u000athe breast tumor is composed of cells with more uniform round nuclei that have\u000aprominent, central nucleoli whereas carcinoma cells in the ovary have nuclei\u000awith more varied shapes and absent or small heterogeneous nucleoli. Sections\u000aof a lymph node do not reveal metastatic carcinoma.\u000aSome of the cytologic variation might be related to laboratory techniques,\u000asince the specimens were processed by separate institutions, there is enough\u000adifference to leave me unconvinced that the breast harbors metastatic ovarian\u000acarcinoma and to rule out the possibility of a primary mammary carcinoma.\u000aVarious reports for these specimens refer to marker immunostains done on the\u000aovarian and breast tumors. These could be crucial to the diagnosis but none\u000ahave been supplied for me to review, a situation I hope you will address. In\u000athis regard, there are additional marker studies which can be compared between\u000athe two lesions and for this purpose I would like to receive blocks "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000a#2" and "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID #6" or fifteen unstained sections from each suitable for\u000aimmunohistochemistry.\u000a(NB: Unstained slides were sent to Dr. Rosen after his report was issued.)\u000aAdditional consultation report (9/21/01):\u000aImmunostains for hormone receptors (ER, PR and androgen) performed on the\u000abreast tumor show nuclear reactivity for ER (++) but no reactivity for PR (-)\u000aor androgen receptor (-). The ovarian tumor shows nuclear reactivity for both\u000aER (++) and PR (+++) and absence of staining for androgen receptor (-). The\u000aprognostic marker Her-2/neu was negative in both the breast and ovarian tumors.\u000aA proliferative marker (Ki-67) showed strong reactivity (+++) in both the\u000abreast and ovarian tumors. An immunostain for gross cystic disease fluid\u000aprotein (Brst) shows absence of staining (-) in both the breast and ovarian\u000atumors, a result that differs from the findings in your report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. An\u000aimmunostain (Mito 113) shows reactivity (+++) in the breast and ovarian tumors.\u000aA p53 immunostain could be performed only on the ovarian tumor as we did not\u000ahave any more unstained slides from the breast tumor. It shows strong p53\u000a(+++) reactivity within the ovarian tumor.\u000aThe pattern of reactivity for the above-cited markers is similar (except for\u000aPR) in both tumors and therefore of limited assistance in assessing the breast\u000alesion. The marker results for the breast lesion are consistent with a primary\u000abreast tumor. In my opinion, based on the histomorphology of both tumors, I am\u000ainclined to favor the breast tumor as a primary and not metastases from the\u000aovary.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Solid nodule, L 12:00 breast. History of ovarian cancer.\u000aOperation: 11 gauge ultrasound guided.\u000aOperative findings: Solid lobulated nodule.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: FA vs IDC.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast tissue in a green cassette.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by CARTER,MARK RYAN,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast", in a single light green cassette labeled "left", and\u000aconsists of multiple, soft, fragmented, yellow to white tissue cores (2.6 x\u000a0.6 x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate).\u000aMicro 1: light green cassette labeled "left", multi frags, ESS.\u000akks\u000aReports to: SMITH, DARRELL, NOLAN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
