VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final Type: Surgical Pathology Pathology Report: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID OPERATION DATE: 23 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON: 23 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10:17 CLINICAL DATA: None provided. =============================================================================== *** FINAL DIAGNOSIS *** BREAST (RIGHT), MASTECTOMY: 1. INFILTRATING SIGNET RING (MUCINOUS) CARCINOMA CONSISTENT WITH PRIMARY BREAST CARCINOMA, SEE NOTE. NOTE: Although the histology of the tumor, especially the pattern, is quite consistent with a ductal carcinoma of the breast, other mucinous carcinomas such as gastrointestinal and pulmonary, cannot be ruled out completely as a primary source. The deep lateral resection margins are free of tumor. The inflammatory reaction in the wall of the biopsy cavity appears histologically older than the one week clinical history. Frozen section diagnosis is confirmed. A nest of tumor cells is found deeper in the block of tissue used for permanent section. 2. SKIN AND NIPPLE, THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY. Diagnosis by: G. Richard Dickersin, M.D. Signed On: 30 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 17:01 Consulting Pathologist(s): Dennis Sgroi, M.D. =============================================================================== *** RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum *** RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST (RIGHT): The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptorm protein. The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein. See note. Note: In this laboratory, we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way (paraffin sections) and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay. 91% of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. 96% of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay. When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections, the predictive value of a positive result was 97% and the predictive value of a negative result was 95%. In this laboratory, we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay. Sixty-five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay, and 98% of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors. THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY. THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES. Added by: Frederick Carl Koerner, M.D. Signed On: 04 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 14:48 GROSS DESCRIPTION: Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab, labeled with the patient's name and unit number only, is a 20 x 12 x 4 cm, 370 gram mastectomy specimen with an overlying 19 x 10 cm skin ellipse with a central REMOVED_DATE cm unremarkable nipple. A 12 x REMOVED_DATE cm area of red-yellow to blue discolored skin is centrally located on the ellipse. A REMOVED_DATE cm well-healed scar is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the nipple. A biopsy cavity is located REMOVED_DATE cm deep to the scar and REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest surgical margin which is the deep margin. The biopsy cavity measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The biopsy cavity is almost completely filled with hemorrhagic, granulation tissue. Surrounding the biopsy cavity is white, fibrotic tissue without evidence of carcinoma. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is composed to breast tissue and adipose tissue without evidence of carcinoma. SECTION CODE: A: biopsy cavity, lateral. B: biopsy cavity, middle. C: biopsy cavity and skin. D: biopsy cavity. E: biopsy cavity. F: deep margin. G: lateral margin. H: lateral margin. I: nipple and skin. J: representative breast tissue. K: FX#1. Resident: Eugene F. Ruffolo, MD *** INTRA-OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS *** FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS #1: (RIGHT BREAST): BIOPSY CAVITY WITH ACUTE AND CHRONIC INFLAMMATION AND HEMOSIDERIN DEPOSITION. O.R. Resident: Jacqueline Haas, MD O.R. Diagnosis by: Asif Rashid, MD =============================================================================== SOURCE CARE UNIT: MGH-INPATIENT (FLR.UNKNOWN) REPORTS TO: Michael Ned Margolies, M.D. PART : RIGHT BREAST\u000a[report_end]
