00asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A localizing wire\u000aenters the anterior/inferior surface of the specimen and exits the\u000ainferior/posterior surface. The specimen is received with an accompanying\u000aradiograph demonstrating the localizing wire in place within the specimen\u000aadjacent to an area of calcification. Sectioning in the area of interest,\u000athere is a small focus of irregular-appearing dense tissue, at least REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm which exhibits dilated ducts filled with thick, yellow fluid. The\u000alesion is REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest inferior and anterior margins, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest posterior margin, and at least REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest lateral\u000amargin. The remaining specimen is comprised predominantly of tan/yellow soft,\u000alobulated adipose tissue with no lesions. The specimen is approximately 40%\u000afibrous, 60% adipose tissue. Focal primary up to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension,\u000ain the inferior medial margin of the specimen and so at the superior/lateral.\u000aThe entire specimen is submitted sequentially from medial to lateral with area\u000aof interest in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen is sequentially submitted from medial to\u000alateral, multi frags, ESS.\u000aDB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/21/2006\u000a[report_end]
